window.ESAT_QUESTIONS = (window.ESAT_QUESTIONS || []).concat([
{"id": "ial-phy1-001", "type": "mcq", "spec": "phy1:1.4", "specs": ["phy1:1.13"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "Which of the following lists contains *only* vector quantities? **(1)**", "options": ["displacement, speed, force", "velocity, mass, momentum", "acceleration, momentum, weight", "distance, kinetic energy, weight"], "answer": "C", "solution": "A vector quantity has both magnitude and direction. A scalar quantity has magnitude only.\n\n- **C is correct:** acceleration, momentum ($p = mv$, which points the same way as the velocity) and weight (a force acting vertically downwards) all have a direction, so all three are vectors.\n- A is wrong: speed is a scalar because it is the magnitude of velocity.\n- B is wrong: mass is a scalar.\n- D is wrong: distance and kinetic energy are scalars. Only weight is a vector."},
{"id": "ial-phy1-002", "type": "mcq", "spec": "phy1:1.12", "specs": ["phy1:1.8", "phy1:1.10"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "A book rests on a horizontal table. The weight of the book is the gravitational force exerted by the Earth on the book.\n\nWhich force forms a Newton's third law pair with the weight of the book? **(1)**", "options": ["the gravitational force exerted by the book on the Earth", "the normal contact force exerted by the table on the book", "the normal contact force exerted by the book on the table", "the weight of the table"], "answer": "A", "solution": "The two forces in a Newton's third law pair are equal in magnitude and opposite in direction. They are the same type of force and they act on different bodies. The weight is the gravitational pull of the Earth on the book, so its pair is the gravitational pull of the book on the Earth.\n\n- **A is correct** for this reason.\n- B is wrong: this force acts on the same body (the book) and it is a contact force, not a gravitational force. It equals the weight only because the book is in equilibrium (Newton's first law). The two are balanced forces on one body, not a third-law pair.\n- C is wrong: this is a contact force. It forms a third-law pair with the force in B.\n- D is wrong: the weight of the table is the Earth's pull on the table, which belongs to a different interaction."},
{"id": "ial-phy1-003", "type": "mcq", "spec": "phy1:2.8", "specs": ["phy1:2.6", "phy1:2.7"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A tensile stress–strain graph for a metal sample is a straight line from the origin to the point where the strain is 0.0020 and the stress is $1.4 \\times 10^{8}$ Pa. After this point the gradient of the graph gradually decreases while the stress keeps rising. The sample breaks at a strain of 0.15 and a stress of $2.6 \\times 10^{8}$ Pa.\n\nWhich row gives the Young modulus and the breaking stress of the metal? **(1)**", "options": ["Young modulus $= 7.0 \\times 10^{10}$ Pa; breaking stress $= 1.4 \\times 10^{8}$ Pa", "Young modulus $= 1.7 \\times 10^{9}$ Pa; breaking stress $= 2.6 \\times 10^{8}$ Pa", "Young modulus $= 1.4 \\times 10^{8}$ Pa; breaking stress $= 2.6 \\times 10^{8}$ Pa", "Young modulus $= 7.0 \\times 10^{10}$ Pa; breaking stress $= 2.6 \\times 10^{8}$ Pa"], "answer": "D", "solution": "The Young modulus is the gradient of the linear region of the stress–strain graph:\n$$E = \\frac{\\text{stress}}{\\text{strain}} = \\frac{1.4 \\times 10^{8}}{0.0020} = 7.0 \\times 10^{10}\\ \\mathrm{Pa}$$\nThe breaking stress is the stress at which the sample breaks, $2.6 \\times 10^{8}$ Pa.\n\n- **D is correct.**\n- A is wrong: $1.4 \\times 10^{8}$ Pa is the stress at the limit of proportionality, not the breaking stress.\n- B is wrong: $2.6 \\times 10^{8} / 0.15 = 1.7 \\times 10^{9}$ Pa uses a point outside the linear region, where stress is not proportional to strain.\n- C is wrong: $1.4 \\times 10^{8}$ Pa is a value of stress, not the gradient of the linear region."},
{"id": "ial-phy1-004", "type": "mcq", "spec": "phy1:1.3", "specs": ["phy1:1.2"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A car is travelling in a straight line at $4.0\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$. At time $t = 0$ it starts to accelerate in its direction of motion. Its acceleration–time graph is a straight line that falls from $3.0\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-2}}$ at $t = 0$ to zero at $t = 8.0$ s.\n\nWhat is the velocity of the car at $t = 8.0$ s? **(1)**", "options": ["$12\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$", "$16\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$", "$24\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$", "$28\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$"], "answer": "B", "solution": "The area under an acceleration–time graph equals the change in velocity. Here the area is a triangle:\n$$\\Delta v = \\tfrac12 \\times 3.0 \\times 8.0 = 12\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$$\n$$v = 4.0 + 12 = 16\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$$\n\n- **B is correct.**\n- A is wrong: $12\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$ is only the change in velocity, without the initial velocity added.\n- C is wrong: it treats the acceleration as a constant $3.0\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-2}}$ ($3.0 \\times 8.0$) and also ignores the initial velocity.\n- D is wrong: $4.0 + 3.0 \\times 8.0$ treats the acceleration as constant. The acceleration falls steadily, so the area is a triangle, not a rectangle."},
{"id": "ial-phy1-005", "type": "mcq", "spec": "phy1:1.22", "specs": ["phy1:1.21", "phy1:1.19"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "An electric motor lifts a load of mass 30.0 kg vertically through a height of 12.0 m at constant speed in 15.0 s. The electrical power input to the motor is 400 W.\n\nWhat is the efficiency of the motor? **(1)**", "options": ["6.0%", "41%", "59%", "170%"], "answer": "C", "solution": "The useful energy output is the gain in gravitational potential energy:\n$$\\Delta E_{grav} = mg\\Delta h = 30.0 \\times 9.81 \\times 12.0 = 3530\\ \\mathrm{J}$$\nUseful power output $= \\dfrac{3530}{15.0} = 235$ W\n$$\\text{efficiency} = \\frac{\\text{useful power output}}{\\text{total power input}} = \\frac{235}{400} = 0.59$$\nso the efficiency is 59%.\n\n- **C is correct.**\n- A is wrong: it uses the mass instead of the weight (it leaves out $g$). That gives $30.0 \\times 12.0 / 15.0 = 24$ W and 6.0%.\n- B is wrong: 41% is the fraction of the input power that is wasted, $1 - 0.59$.\n- D is wrong: it divides the input power by the output power. An efficiency cannot be more than 100%."},
{"id": "ial-phy1-006", "type": "mcq", "spec": "phy1:2.3", "difficulty": 2, "stem": "Which of the following statements about fluid flow and viscous drag is correct? **(1)**", "options": ["Stokes's law gives the viscous drag on a large sphere moving at high speed through a fluid.", "For a small sphere in laminar flow, the viscous drag is proportional to the square of its radius.", "The viscosity of a liquid such as oil increases as its temperature increases.", "In laminar flow, adjacent layers of fluid do not mix and the velocity of the fluid at any point remains constant."], "answer": "D", "solution": "- **D is correct:** in laminar (streamline) flow the fluid moves in layers that do not mix, and the velocity at any given point stays constant. In turbulent flow there are eddies and the velocity at a point keeps changing.\n- A is wrong: Stokes's law, $F = 6\\pi\\eta r v$, applies only to small spheres moving at low speeds with laminar flow. A large, fast sphere produces turbulent flow.\n- B is wrong: $F = 6\\pi\\eta r v$, so the drag is proportional to $r$, not to $r^2$.\n- C is wrong: the viscosity of a liquid decreases as its temperature increases. Warm oil flows more easily."},
{"id": "ial-phy1-007", "type": "written", "spec": "phy1:1.1", "specs": ["phy1:1.2", "phy1:1.3"], "difficulty": 1, "marks": 10, "stem": "A tram travels along a straight, horizontal track between two stops, P and Q. It starts from rest at P and accelerates uniformly for 15.0 s until it reaches a speed of $12.0\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$. It then travels at this constant speed for 40.0 s. Finally it decelerates uniformly and comes to rest at Q, 20.0 s after it starts to decelerate.\n\n**(a)** Calculate the acceleration of the tram during the first 15.0 s. **(2)**\n\n**(b)** Describe the velocity–time graph for the journey from P to Q. Your description should give the shape of each section of the graph and the values of time and velocity at the start and end of each section. **(3)**\n\n**(c)** Use the velocity–time graph to calculate the distance between P and Q. **(3)**\n\n**(d)** Calculate the average velocity of the tram for the journey from P to Q. **(2)**", "markScheme": "**(a)**\n- **M1** Use of $a = \\dfrac{v-u}{t}$ or $v = u + at$, e.g. $a = \\dfrac{12.0 - 0}{15.0}$\n- **A1** $a = 0.80\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-2}}$ (unit required; accept 0.8)\n**(b)**\n- **B1** Straight line of constant positive gradient from the origin to $t = 15.0$ s, $v = 12.0\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$\n- **B1** Horizontal straight line at $v = 12.0\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$ from $t = 15.0$ s to $t = 55.0$ s\n- **B1** Straight line of constant negative gradient from $t = 55.0$ s, $v = 12.0\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$ down to $v = 0$ at $t = 75.0$ s\n**(c)**\n- **M1** Distance = area under the velocity–time graph. Area of one sloping section, e.g. $\\tfrac{1}{2} \\times 15.0 \\times 12.0 = 90$ m or $\\tfrac{1}{2} \\times 20.0 \\times 12.0 = 120$ m (or use of $s = \\tfrac{1}{2}(u+v)t$)\n- **M1** Adds all three sections, $90 + 40.0 \\times 12.0 + 120$, or uses the trapezium $\\tfrac{1}{2}(40.0 + 75.0) \\times 12.0$\n- **A1** 690 m\n**(d)**\n- **M1** Use of average velocity $= \\dfrac{\\Delta s}{\\Delta t}$ with total time 75.0 s, e.g. $\\dfrac{690}{75.0}$ (ft their distance from (c))\n- **A1** $9.2\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$ (from P towards Q); ft their (c)", "solution": "**(a)** $a = \\dfrac{v-u}{t} = \\dfrac{12.0 - 0}{15.0} = 0.80\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-2}}$\n\n**(b)** The graph has three straight sections:\n- a straight line of constant positive gradient ($0.80\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-2}}$) from the origin up to $t = 15.0$ s, $v = 12.0\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$\n- a horizontal line at $v = 12.0\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$ from $t = 15.0$ s to $t = 55.0$ s\n- a straight line of constant negative gradient ($-0.60\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-2}}$) from $t = 55.0$ s down to $v = 0$ at $t = 75.0$ s\n\n**(c)** Distance = area under the graph:\n$$s = \\tfrac{1}{2}(15.0)(12.0) + (40.0)(12.0) + \\tfrac{1}{2}(20.0)(12.0) = 90 + 480 + 120 = 690\\ \\mathrm{m}$$\n\n**(d)** The track is straight, so the displacement is 690 m from P towards Q.\n$$\\text{average velocity} = \\frac{\\Delta s}{\\Delta t} = \\frac{690}{75.0} = 9.2\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}\\ \\text{(from P towards Q)}$$"},
{"id": "ial-phy1-008", "type": "written", "spec": "phy1:1.11", "specs": ["phy1:1.10", "phy1:1.1"], "difficulty": 1, "marks": 11, "stem": "A student measures the acceleration of free fall $g$ using a small steel ball, an electromagnet, a trapdoor and an electronic timer. When the electromagnet is switched off, the ball is released from rest and the timer starts. The timer stops when the ball strikes the trapdoor and opens it. The student measures the height $h$ through which the ball falls and the time $t$ taken.\n\n**(a)** State what is meant by gravitational field strength, and show that the unit $\\mathrm{N\\,kg^{-1}}$ is equivalent to $\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-2}}$. **(2)**\n\n**(b)** Show that $2h = gt^2$. **(2)**\n\n**(c)** Describe how the student should carry out the experiment and use a graph to obtain an accurate value for $g$. **(4)**\n\n**(d)** For one height the student recorded $h = 0.800$ m and a mean time $t = 0.405$ s. Calculate the value of $g$ given by this pair of readings. **(2)**\n\n**(e)** Suggest why a small steel ball is used rather than a table-tennis ball of the same diameter. **(1)**", "markScheme": "**(a)**\n- **B1** Gravitational field strength is the gravitational force per unit mass (on a mass placed in the field), $g = F/m$\n- **B1** From $F = ma$, $1\\ \\mathrm{N} = 1\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-2}}$, so $\\mathrm{N\\,kg^{-1}} = \\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-2}\\,kg^{-1}} = \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-2}}$\n**(b)**\n- **M1** Use of $s = ut + \\tfrac{1}{2}at^2$ with $u = 0$, $s = h$ and $a = g$\n- **A1*** $h = \\tfrac{1}{2}gt^2$, so $2h = gt^2$\n**(c)**\n- **B1** Measure $h$ with a metre rule from the bottom of the ball to the trapdoor, with the eye level with the scale (or using a set square) to avoid parallax\n- **B1** Take readings for a range of different heights (at least five)\n- **B1** Repeat the timing at each height (at least three times) and calculate a mean $t$\n- **B1** Plot a graph of $2h$ against $t^2$, draw a line of best fit and take $g$ = gradient (or plot $h$ against $t^2$ and take $g = 2 \\times$ gradient)\n**(d)**\n- **M1** Use of $g = \\dfrac{2h}{t^2} = \\dfrac{2 \\times 0.800}{0.405^2}$\n- **A1** $9.75\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-2}}$ (accept 9.8; accept $\\mathrm{N\\,kg^{-1}}$)\n**(e)**\n- **B1** Air resistance on the steel ball is negligible compared with its weight. The table-tennis ball has a much smaller weight, so air resistance on it would be significant and it would not be in free fall", "solution": "**(a)** Gravitational field strength is the gravitational force acting per unit mass, $g = F/m$. Since $F = ma$, $1\\ \\mathrm{N} = 1\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-2}}$, so\n$$1\\ \\mathrm{N\\,kg^{-1}} = 1\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-2}} \\div 1\\ \\mathrm{kg} = 1\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-2}}$$\n\n**(b)** The ball starts from rest, so $u = 0$. With $s = h$ and $a = g$:\n$$s = ut + \\tfrac{1}{2}at^2 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; h = \\tfrac{1}{2}gt^2 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; 2h = gt^2$$\n\n**(c)**\n- Measure $h$ with a metre rule from the bottom of the ball to the trapdoor, with your eye level with the scale to avoid parallax error.\n- Take readings for at least five different heights, spread over as wide a range as possible.\n- Repeat the timing at least three times for each height and calculate the mean $t$.\n- Plot $2h$ (y-axis) against $t^2$ (x-axis). Comparing $2h = g t^2$ with $y = mx$, the graph should be a straight line through the origin, and $g$ equals the gradient of the line of best fit.\n\n**(d)**\n$$g = \\frac{2h}{t^2} = \\frac{2 \\times 0.800}{0.405^2} = \\frac{1.600}{0.164025} = 9.75\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-2}}$$\n\n**(e)** The steel ball is small and dense, so air resistance is negligible compared with its weight and it is effectively in free fall. A table-tennis ball of the same size has a much smaller weight, so air resistance would be a large fraction of its weight and its acceleration would be noticeably less than $g$."},
{"id": "ial-phy1-009", "type": "written", "spec": "phy1:1.7", "specs": ["phy1:1.5", "phy1:1.1", "phy1:1.6", "phy1:1.9"], "difficulty": 2, "marks": 12, "stem": "A stone is thrown from the edge of a vertical cliff. The point of release is 25.0 m above the surface of the sea. The stone leaves the thrower's hand with a velocity of $15.0\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$ at an angle of 30.0° above the horizontal. Air resistance is negligible.\n\n**(a)** Calculate the horizontal and vertical components of the initial velocity of the stone. **(2)**\n\n**(b)** Explain why the horizontal component of the velocity of the stone remains constant during its flight. **(2)**\n\n**(c)** Show that the stone takes about 3.1 s to reach the sea. **(3)**\n\n**(d)** Calculate the horizontal distance from the foot of the cliff to the point where the stone enters the sea. **(2)**\n\n**(e)** Calculate the speed of the stone just before it enters the sea. **(3)**", "markScheme": "**(a)**\n- **B1** Horizontal component $= 15.0\\cos 30.0^\\circ = 13.0\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$ (accept 12.99)\n- **B1** Vertical component $= 15.0\\sin 30.0^\\circ = 7.50\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$ (upwards)\n**(b)**\n- **B1** No horizontal force acts on the stone (air resistance is negligible and the weight acts vertically)\n- **B1** So there is no horizontal acceleration (Newton's first law, or $\\Sigma F = ma$ with $\\Sigma F = 0$)\n**(c)**\n- **M1** Use of $s = ut + \\tfrac{1}{2}at^2$ vertically with a consistent sign convention, e.g. $-25.0 = 7.50t - \\tfrac{1}{2}(9.81)t^2$ (or two-stage method: time to highest point $= 7.50/9.81 = 0.765$ s and greatest height above sea $= 25.0 + 2.87 = 27.9$ m)\n- **M1** Solves $4.905t^2 - 7.50t - 25.0 = 0$ (or time to fall from rest through 27.9 m, $\\sqrt{2 \\times 27.9/9.81} = 2.38$ s, added to 0.765 s)\n- **A1*** $t = 3.15$ s\n**(d)**\n- **M1** Use of horizontal distance = horizontal velocity × time, e.g. $13.0 \\times 3.15$\n- **A1** 40.9 m (accept 40.3 m to 41.0 m; 40.3 m comes from using 3.1 s)\n**(e)**\n- **M1** Vertical velocity at the sea: $v = 7.50 - 9.81 \\times 3.15 = -23.4\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$, or $v^2 = 7.50^2 + 2 \\times 9.81 \\times 25.0$ giving $23.4\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$ downwards\n- **M1** Combines the perpendicular components using Pythagoras, $\\sqrt{13.0^2 + 23.4^2}$ (the energy method $v^2 = 15.0^2 + 2 \\times 9.81 \\times 25.0$ scores both M marks)\n- **A1** $26.7\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$ (accept 26.3 to 26.8; 26.3 comes from using 3.1 s)", "solution": "**(a)**\n$$u_x = 15.0\\cos 30.0^\\circ = 13.0\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}, \\qquad u_y = 15.0\\sin 30.0^\\circ = 7.50\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}\\ \\text{upwards}$$\n\n**(b)** Air resistance is negligible, so the only force on the stone is its weight, which acts vertically. There is no horizontal force, so there is no horizontal acceleration, and the horizontal component of velocity stays constant (Newton's first law).\n\n**(c)** Take upwards as positive: $s = -25.0$ m, $u = 7.50\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$, $a = -9.81\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-2}}$.\n$$-25.0 = 7.50t - 4.905t^2 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; 4.905t^2 - 7.50t - 25.0 = 0$$\n$$t = \\frac{7.50 + \\sqrt{7.50^2 + 4(4.905)(25.0)}}{2(4.905)} = \\frac{7.50 + 23.38}{9.81} = 3.15\\ \\mathrm{s} \\approx 3.1\\ \\mathrm{s}$$\n\n**(d)**\n$$s_x = u_x t = 12.99 \\times 3.148 = 40.9\\ \\mathrm{m}$$\n\n**(e)** Vertical velocity at the sea: $v_y = 7.50 - 9.81 \\times 3.148 = -23.38\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$, i.e. $23.4\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$ downwards. The horizontal component is still $12.99\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$.\n$$v = \\sqrt{12.99^2 + 23.38^2} = \\sqrt{715.5} = 26.7\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$$"},
{"id": "ial-phy1-010", "type": "written", "spec": "phy1:1.9", "specs": ["phy1:1.5", "phy1:1.8", "phy1:1.10"], "difficulty": 2, "marks": 12, "stem": "A rope pulls a crate of mass 45.0 kg up a straight ramp. The ramp is inclined at 20.0° to the horizontal and the rope is parallel to the surface of the ramp. While the crate moves, a constant frictional force of 120 N acts on it.\n\n**(a)** Describe a free-body force diagram for the crate by naming each force acting on the crate and stating its direction. **(3)**\n\n**(b)** Show that the component of the weight of the crate parallel to the ramp is about 150 N. **(2)**\n\n**(c)** Calculate the normal contact force exerted by the ramp on the crate. **(2)**\n\n**(d)** The crate moves up the ramp at a constant velocity. Calculate the tension in the rope. **(2)**\n\n**(e)** The tension in the rope is increased to 350 N. Calculate the acceleration of the crate. **(3)**", "markScheme": "**(a)**\n- **B1** Weight ($W$ or $mg$) acting vertically downwards\n- **B1** Normal contact force (normal reaction) acting perpendicular to the surface of the ramp, away from the ramp\n- **B1** Tension acting up the ramp and friction acting down the ramp, both parallel to the ramp (max 2 marks for (a) if any extra force is included, e.g. a separate 'downhill force' as well as the weight)\n**(b)**\n- **M1** Use of $W = mg$ and resolving parallel to the ramp: $45.0 \\times 9.81 \\times \\sin 20.0^\\circ$\n- **A1*** 151 N\n**(c)**\n- **M1** Resolving perpendicular to the ramp: $R = mg\\cos 20.0^\\circ = 441 \\times \\cos 20.0^\\circ$\n- **A1** 415 N\n**(d)**\n- **M1** Constant velocity, so the resultant force is zero (Newton's first law): $T = 151 + 120$\n- **A1** 271 N (accept 270 N, which comes from using 150 N)\n**(e)**\n- **M1** Resultant force up the ramp $= 350 - 151 - 120 = 79$ N\n- **M1** Use of $\\Sigma F = ma$: $a = \\dfrac{79}{45.0}$\n- **A1** $1.76\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-2}}$ up the ramp (accept 1.75 to 1.78)", "solution": "**(a)** Four forces act on the crate:\n- weight $W = mg$, vertically downwards, acting at the centre of gravity\n- normal contact force $R$ from the ramp, perpendicular to the ramp surface and away from it\n- tension $T$ in the rope, parallel to the ramp and up the slope\n- friction of 120 N, parallel to the ramp and down the slope (opposing the motion)\n\n**(b)** $W = mg = 45.0 \\times 9.81 = 441.5$ N. The component parallel to the ramp is\n$$W\\sin 20.0^\\circ = 441.5 \\times 0.342 = 151\\ \\mathrm{N} \\approx 150\\ \\mathrm{N}$$\n\n**(c)** There is no acceleration perpendicular to the ramp, so\n$$R = W\\cos 20.0^\\circ = 441.5 \\times 0.940 = 415\\ \\mathrm{N}$$\n\n**(d)** The velocity is constant, so the resultant force is zero (Newton's first law). Along the ramp:\n$$T = W\\sin 20.0^\\circ + 120 = 151 + 120 = 271\\ \\mathrm{N}$$\n\n**(e)** Resultant force up the ramp: $\\Sigma F = 350 - 151 - 120 = 79$ N\n$$a = \\frac{\\Sigma F}{m} = \\frac{79}{45.0} = 1.76\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-2}}\\ \\text{up the ramp}$$"},
{"id": "ial-phy1-011", "type": "written", "spec": "phy1:1.14", "specs": ["phy1:1.13", "phy1:1.18", "phy1:1.12", "phy1:1.9"], "difficulty": 2, "marks": 13, "stem": "Two gliders, A and B, move along a straight, horizontal air track on which friction is negligible. Glider A has mass 0.250 kg and glider B has mass 0.150 kg. Before they collide, A moves to the right at $0.600\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$ and B moves to the left at $0.400\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$. After the collision, A moves to the right at $0.100\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$.\n\n**(a)** State the principle of conservation of linear momentum. **(2)**\n\n**(b)** Calculate the velocity of B after the collision. **(3)**\n\n**(c)** Determine whether the collision is elastic. **(3)**\n\n**(d)** During the collision, A exerts a force on B and B exerts a force on A. Use Newton's laws of motion to explain why the total momentum of the two gliders is the same before and after the collision. **(3)**\n\n**(e)** The gliders are in contact for 0.0500 s. Calculate the average force exerted on B during the collision. **(2)**", "markScheme": "**(a)**\n- **B1** The total momentum of a system remains constant, or total momentum before a collision = total momentum after\n- **B1** provided no resultant external force acts on the system (closed or isolated system)\n**(b)**\n- **M1** Total momentum before, taking right as positive: $0.250 \\times 0.600 + 0.150 \\times (-0.400) = 0.0900\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$ (correct use of signs required)\n- **M1** Equates to the momentum after: $0.0900 = 0.250 \\times 0.100 + 0.150\\,v_B$\n- **A1** $v_B = 0.433\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$ to the right (direction required)\n**(c)**\n- **M1** Use of $E_k = \\tfrac12 mv^2$ for the total kinetic energy before: $\\tfrac12(0.250)(0.600)^2 + \\tfrac12(0.150)(0.400)^2 = 0.0570$ J\n- **M1** Total kinetic energy after: $\\tfrac12(0.250)(0.100)^2 + \\tfrac12(0.150)(0.433)^2 = 0.0153$ J (ft their $v_B$)\n- **E1** Kinetic energy is not conserved (it decreases), so the collision is not elastic (it is inelastic); the conclusion must follow from their values\n**(d)**\n- **B1** By Newton's third law, the force of A on B is equal in magnitude and opposite in direction to the force of B on A\n- **B1** The two forces act for the same time\n- **B1** From $F = ma = m\\Delta v/\\Delta t$, $F\\Delta t = \\Delta(mv)$, so the changes in momentum of A and B are equal and opposite and the total change in momentum is zero\n**(e)**\n- **M1** Change in velocity of B $= 0.433 - (-0.400) = 0.833\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$ and use of $F = ma = m\\Delta v/\\Delta t$: $F = \\dfrac{0.150 \\times 0.833}{0.0500}$ (or uses A: $\\dfrac{0.250 \\times 0.500}{0.0500}$)\n- **A1** 2.50 N (accept 2.5 N); ft their $v_B$", "solution": "**(a)** The total momentum of a system stays constant, so total momentum before an interaction equals total momentum after it, provided no resultant external force acts on the system.\n\n**(b)** Take velocity to the right as positive.\n$$p_{\\text{before}} = 0.250(0.600) + 0.150(-0.400) = 0.150 - 0.060 = 0.0900\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$$\n$$0.0900 = 0.250(0.100) + 0.150\\,v_B \\;\\Rightarrow\\; v_B = \\frac{0.0900 - 0.0250}{0.150} = 0.433\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$$\nB moves to the right at $0.433\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$.\n\n**(c)** Before: $E_k = \\tfrac12(0.250)(0.600)^2 + \\tfrac12(0.150)(0.400)^2 = 0.0450 + 0.0120 = 0.0570$ J\n\nAfter: $E_k = \\tfrac12(0.250)(0.100)^2 + \\tfrac12(0.150)(0.433)^2 = 0.00125 + 0.0141 = 0.0153$ J\n\nThe kinetic energy falls by 0.0417 J, so the collision is inelastic, not elastic.\n\n**(d)** By Newton's third law, the force exerted by A on B is equal in magnitude and opposite in direction to the force exerted by B on A. Both forces act for the same time $\\Delta t$. By Newton's second law, $F = ma = m\\dfrac{\\Delta v}{\\Delta t}$, so $F\\Delta t = \\Delta(mv)$. The change in momentum of B is therefore equal and opposite to the change in momentum of A. The total change in momentum is zero, so the total momentum does not change.\n\n**(e)** $\\Delta v_B = 0.433 - (-0.400) = 0.833\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$\n$$F = ma = \\frac{m\\,\\Delta v}{\\Delta t} = \\frac{0.150 \\times 0.833}{0.0500} = 2.50\\ \\mathrm{N}\\ \\text{(to the right)}$$\nCheck using A: $\\dfrac{0.250 \\times (0.600 - 0.100)}{0.0500} = 2.50$ N, acting to the left on A, as Newton's third law requires."},
{"id": "ial-phy1-012", "type": "written", "spec": "phy1:1.16", "specs": ["phy1:1.15", "phy1:1.6", "phy1:1.10", "phy1:1.9"], "difficulty": 3, "marks": 14, "stem": "A uniform ladder of length 5.00 m and weight 220 N rests with its top against a smooth vertical wall. The foot of the ladder rests on rough horizontal ground, 1.40 m from the base of the wall. A painter of weight 750 N stands on the ladder at a point 3.50 m from its foot, measured along the ladder. The ladder is in equilibrium. Treat the painter as a point load.\n\n**(a)** **(i)** Explain why the weight of the ladder can be taken to act at its midpoint. **(1)**\n\n**(ii)** Explain why the force exerted by the wall on the ladder is horizontal. **(1)**\n\n**(b)** Show that the force exerted by the wall on the ladder is about 190 N. **(4)**\n\n**(c)** Calculate the magnitude and direction of the resultant force exerted by the ground on the ladder. **(4)**\n\n**(d)** The maximum frictional force that the ground can exert on the foot of the ladder is 230 N. Calculate the greatest distance, measured along the ladder from its foot, that the painter can climb before the ladder starts to slip. **(4)**", "markScheme": "**(a)(i)**\n- **B1** The ladder is uniform, so its centre of gravity (the point at which its whole weight can be taken to act) is at its midpoint\n**(a)(ii)**\n- **B1** The wall is smooth, so it exerts no frictional force; it exerts only a normal contact force, which is perpendicular to the vertical wall\n**(b)**\n- **B1** Height of the top of the ladder $= \\sqrt{5.00^2 - 1.40^2} = 4.80$ m (or angle of ladder to ground $= \\cos^{-1}(1.40/5.00) = 73.7^\\circ$)\n- **M1** Perpendicular distances from the foot to the lines of action of the weights: ladder 0.70 m, painter $\\tfrac{3.50}{5.00} \\times 1.40 = 0.98$ m\n- **M1** Principle of moments about the foot: $R \\times 4.80 = 220 \\times 0.70 + 750 \\times 0.98$\n- **A1*** $R = 185$ N\n**(c)**\n- **B1** Vertical component $= 220 + 750 = 970$ N and horizontal component (friction) $= 185$ N towards the wall\n- **M1** Use of Pythagoras: $\\sqrt{970^2 + 185^2}$\n- **A1** 988 N (accept 987 N to 990 N)\n- **A1** At 79° above the horizontal, directed towards the wall, from $\\tan^{-1}(970/185)$ (accept 11° to the vertical); ft their components\n**(d)**\n- **M1** Limiting case: friction = 230 N, so the force from the wall = 230 N (zero resultant horizontal force)\n- **M1** Moments about the foot: $230 \\times 4.80 = 220 \\times 0.70 + 750x$, where $x$ is the horizontal distance of the painter from the foot\n- **M1** $x = 1.27$ m, converted to a distance along the ladder: $d = 1.27 \\times \\dfrac{5.00}{1.40}$\n- **A1** 4.52 m", "solution": "**(a)(i)** The ladder is uniform, so its centre of gravity, the point where its whole weight can be taken to act, is at its midpoint.\n\n**(a)(ii)** The wall is smooth, so it cannot exert a frictional force. It exerts only a normal contact force, which is perpendicular to the vertical wall and therefore horizontal.\n\n**(b)** Height of the top of the ladder: $h = \\sqrt{5.00^2 - 1.40^2} = \\sqrt{23.04} = 4.80$ m.\n\nHorizontal (perpendicular) distances from the foot: the ladder's weight acts 0.70 m from the foot, and the painter is $\\tfrac{3.50}{5.00} \\times 1.40 = 0.98$ m from the foot.\n\nTake moments about the foot, where the ground forces have no moment:\n$$R \\times 4.80 = 220 \\times 0.70 + 750 \\times 0.98 = 154 + 735 = 889\\ \\mathrm{N\\,m}$$\n$$R = \\frac{889}{4.80} = 185\\ \\mathrm{N} \\approx 190\\ \\mathrm{N}$$\n\n**(c)** Vertical equilibrium: normal force $N = 220 + 750 = 970$ N upwards. Horizontal equilibrium: friction $F = R = 185$ N towards the wall.\n$$\\text{resultant} = \\sqrt{970^2 + 185^2} = 988\\ \\mathrm{N}$$\n$$\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{970}{185}\\right) = 79^\\circ\\ \\text{above the horizontal, directed towards the wall}$$\n\n**(d)** When the ladder is about to slip, friction = 230 N, so the wall force is also 230 N. Let the painter be a horizontal distance $x$ from the foot and take moments about the foot:\n$$230 \\times 4.80 = 220 \\times 0.70 + 750x \\;\\Rightarrow\\; 1104 = 154 + 750x \\;\\Rightarrow\\; x = 1.27\\ \\mathrm{m}$$\nDistance along the ladder:\n$$d = 1.267 \\times \\frac{5.00}{1.40} = 4.52\\ \\mathrm{m}$$"},
{"id": "ial-phy1-013", "type": "written", "spec": "phy1:1.20", "specs": ["phy1:1.17", "phy1:1.18", "phy1:1.19", "phy1:1.21", "phy1:1.22"], "difficulty": 3, "marks": 14, "stem": "A tow cable pulls a skier of mass 72.0 kg up a straight slope. The slope is 350 m long and is inclined at 12.0° to the horizontal. The skier moves up the slope at a constant speed of $2.50\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$. The cable makes an angle of 20.0° with the surface of the slope. An average resistive force of 90.0 N acts on the skier, parallel to the slope.\n\n**(a)** Show that the gain in gravitational potential energy of the skier is about $5 \\times 10^4$ J. **(2)**\n\n**(b)** **(i)** Calculate the work done by the tow cable on the skier as she moves from the bottom to the top of the slope. **(3)**\n\n**(ii)** Hence calculate the tension in the cable. **(2)**\n\n**(c)** The tow cable is driven by an electric motor with an efficiency of 65%. Assume that the useful output of the motor is the work done by the cable on this skier. Calculate the electrical power input to the motor. **(3)**\n\n**(d)** At the top, the skier starts from rest and skis down a different route. The route is 260 m long and she descends a vertical height of 40.0 m. Her speed at the bottom is $18.0\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$. Calculate the average resistive force acting on her during the descent. **(4)**", "markScheme": "**(a)**\n- **M1** Vertical height gained $\\Delta h = 350\\sin 12.0^\\circ = 72.8$ m and use of $\\Delta E_{grav} = mg\\Delta h$: $72.0 \\times 9.81 \\times 72.8$\n- **A1*** $5.14 \\times 10^4$ J\n**(b)(i)**\n- **M1** Work done against the resistive force $= 90.0 \\times 350 = 3.15 \\times 10^4$ J\n- **M1** Kinetic energy is constant, so work done by cable = gain in gravitational potential energy + work done against resistance: $5.14 \\times 10^4 + 3.15 \\times 10^4$\n- **A1** $8.29 \\times 10^4$ J (accept $8.3 \\times 10^4$ J)\n**(b)(ii)**\n- **M1** Use of $\\Delta W = F\\Delta s\\cos\\theta$: $T = \\dfrac{8.29 \\times 10^4}{350 \\times \\cos 20.0^\\circ}$ (or resolving: $T\\cos 20.0^\\circ = mg\\sin 12.0^\\circ + 90.0$)\n- **A1** 252 N; ft their (b)(i)\n**(c)**\n- **M1** Time $= \\dfrac{350}{2.50} = 140$ s and use of $P = \\dfrac{W}{t}$: useful output power $= \\dfrac{8.29 \\times 10^4}{140} = 592$ W (or $P = Tv\\cos 20.0^\\circ$)\n- **M1** Use of efficiency = useful power output ÷ total power input: $P_{in} = \\dfrac{592}{0.65}$\n- **A1** 911 W (accept 910 W); ft their (b)(i)\n**(d)**\n- **M1** Loss of gravitational potential energy $= 72.0 \\times 9.81 \\times 40.0 = 2.83 \\times 10^4$ J\n- **M1** Gain in kinetic energy $= \\tfrac12 \\times 72.0 \\times 18.0^2 = 1.17 \\times 10^4$ J\n- **M1** Energy dissipated $= 2.83 \\times 10^4 - 1.17 \\times 10^4 = 1.66 \\times 10^4$ J and use of work done $= F\\Delta s$: $F = \\dfrac{1.66 \\times 10^4}{260}$\n- **A1** 63.8 N (accept 63.8 N to 64 N)", "solution": "**(a)** $\\Delta h = 350\\sin 12.0^\\circ = 72.8$ m\n$$\\Delta E_{grav} = mg\\Delta h = 72.0 \\times 9.81 \\times 72.8 = 5.14 \\times 10^4\\ \\mathrm{J} \\approx 5 \\times 10^4\\ \\mathrm{J}$$\n\n**(b)(i)** Work done against the resistive force $= 90.0 \\times 350 = 3.15 \\times 10^4$ J. The speed is constant, so the kinetic energy does not change and\n$$W_{cable} = 5.14 \\times 10^4 + 3.15 \\times 10^4 = 8.29 \\times 10^4\\ \\mathrm{J}$$\n\n**(b)(ii)** The cable is at 20.0° to the direction of motion, so $\\Delta W = T\\Delta s\\cos\\theta$:\n$$T = \\frac{8.29 \\times 10^4}{350 \\times \\cos 20.0^\\circ} = \\frac{8.29 \\times 10^4}{328.9} = 252\\ \\mathrm{N}$$\nCheck: $T\\cos 20.0^\\circ = 72.0 \\times 9.81 \\times \\sin 12.0^\\circ + 90.0 = 146.9 + 90.0 = 236.9$ N, which gives $T = 252$ N.\n\n**(c)** Time on the slope $= 350 / 2.50 = 140$ s.\n$$P_{out} = \\frac{8.29 \\times 10^4}{140} = 592\\ \\mathrm{W}, \\qquad P_{in} = \\frac{P_{out}}{\\text{efficiency}} = \\frac{592}{0.65} = 911\\ \\mathrm{W}$$\n\n**(d)** Loss of $E_{grav} = 72.0 \\times 9.81 \\times 40.0 = 2.83 \\times 10^4$ J\n\nGain of $E_k = \\tfrac12 \\times 72.0 \\times 18.0^2 = 1.17 \\times 10^4$ J\n\nBy conservation of energy, the energy dissipated (work done against resistive forces) is $28\\,250 - 11\\,660 = 1.66 \\times 10^4$ J.\n$$F = \\frac{\\text{work done}}{\\Delta s} = \\frac{1.66 \\times 10^4}{260} = 63.8\\ \\mathrm{N}$$"},
{"id": "ial-phy1-014", "type": "written", "spec": "phy1:2.4", "specs": ["phy1:2.1", "phy1:2.2", "phy1:2.3", "phy1:1.9"], "difficulty": 3, "marks": 13, "stem": "A student uses a falling-ball method to find the viscosity of glycerol. A tall measuring cylinder is filled with glycerol of density $1260\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m^{-3}}$. Two marks are made on the cylinder 30.0 cm apart, with the upper mark well below the surface of the glycerol. A steel ball bearing is released from rest just below the surface, and the student measures the time it takes to fall between the two marks. The table shows the student's measurements.\n\n| Quantity | Value |\n|---|---|\n| radius of ball bearing | 1.50 mm |\n| mass of 20 identical ball bearings | 2.21 g |\n| distance between the marks | 30.0 cm |\n| mean time to fall between the marks | 13.1 s |\n\n**(a)** Explain why the ball bearing reaches a terminal velocity. **(3)**\n\n**(b)** Show that the density of the steel is about $7800\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m^{-3}}$. **(3)**\n\n**(c)** Calculate the viscosity of the glycerol. **(5)**\n\n**(d)** **(i)** Explain why the upper mark is placed well below the surface of the glycerol. **(1)**\n\n**(ii)** Explain why the student should measure the temperature of the glycerol. **(1)**", "markScheme": "**(a)**\n- **B1** Initially the weight is greater than the upthrust plus viscous drag, so there is a downward resultant force and the ball accelerates\n- **B1** Viscous drag increases as the speed of the ball increases\n- **B1** Eventually weight = upthrust + viscous drag, so the resultant force is zero and the acceleration is zero: the ball falls at a constant (terminal) velocity\n**(b)**\n- **M1** Use of $V = \\tfrac43\\pi r^3 = \\tfrac43\\pi(1.50 \\times 10^{-3})^3 = 1.41 \\times 10^{-8}\\ \\mathrm{m^3}$\n- **M1** Mass of one ball $= 2.21 \\times 10^{-3} / 20 = 1.105 \\times 10^{-4}$ kg and use of $\\rho = m/V$\n- **A1*** $7.82 \\times 10^{3}\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m^{-3}}$\n**(c)**\n- **M1** Terminal velocity $v = \\dfrac{0.300}{13.1} = 0.0229\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$\n- **M1** Upthrust = weight of glycerol displaced: $U = 1260 \\times 1.41 \\times 10^{-8} \\times 9.81 = 1.75 \\times 10^{-4}$ N\n- **M1** Weight of ball $W = mg = 1.105 \\times 10^{-4} \\times 9.81 = 1.08 \\times 10^{-3}$ N (or $\\rho_{steel}Vg$)\n- **M1** At terminal velocity $W = U + 6\\pi\\eta r v$, so $\\eta = \\dfrac{W - U}{6\\pi r v}$ with $r = 1.50 \\times 10^{-3}$ m\n- **A1** 1.40 Pa s (accept 1.4; accept the unit $\\mathrm{N\\,s\\,m^{-2}}$ or $\\mathrm{kg\\,m^{-1}\\,s^{-1}}$)\n**(d)(i)**\n- **B1** So that the ball has already reached terminal velocity (is no longer accelerating) when it passes the upper mark\n**(d)(ii)**\n- **B1** Viscosity depends on temperature (the viscosity of glycerol decreases as temperature increases), so the temperature must be known and kept constant for the measured viscosity to have meaning", "solution": "**(a)** When the ball is released, its weight is greater than the upthrust plus the viscous drag, so there is a downward resultant force and the ball accelerates. As its speed increases, the viscous drag ($6\\pi\\eta r v$) increases. Eventually weight = upthrust + viscous drag, so the resultant force is zero, the acceleration is zero and the ball falls at a constant (terminal) velocity.\n\n**(b)**\n$$V = \\tfrac43\\pi r^3 = \\tfrac43\\pi(1.50 \\times 10^{-3})^3 = 1.414 \\times 10^{-8}\\ \\mathrm{m^3}$$\n$$m = \\frac{2.21 \\times 10^{-3}}{20} = 1.105 \\times 10^{-4}\\ \\mathrm{kg}$$\n$$\\rho = \\frac{m}{V} = \\frac{1.105 \\times 10^{-4}}{1.414 \\times 10^{-8}} = 7.82 \\times 10^{3}\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m^{-3}} \\approx 7800\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m^{-3}}$$\n\n**(c)** Terminal velocity: $v = \\dfrac{0.300}{13.1} = 0.0229\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$\n\nUpthrust = weight of glycerol displaced: $U = \\rho_{g}Vg = 1260 \\times 1.414 \\times 10^{-8} \\times 9.81 = 1.75 \\times 10^{-4}$ N\n\nWeight of ball: $W = mg = 1.105 \\times 10^{-4} \\times 9.81 = 1.084 \\times 10^{-3}$ N\n\nAt terminal velocity, $W = U + 6\\pi\\eta r v$, so\n$$\\eta = \\frac{W - U}{6\\pi r v} = \\frac{1.084 \\times 10^{-3} - 1.75 \\times 10^{-4}}{6\\pi \\times 1.50 \\times 10^{-3} \\times 0.0229} = \\frac{9.09 \\times 10^{-4}}{6.48 \\times 10^{-4}} = 1.40\\ \\mathrm{Pa\\,s}$$\n\n**(d)(i)** The ball must already be at terminal velocity when it passes the upper mark, so that its speed between the marks is constant and equals the terminal velocity used in the calculation.\n\n**(d)(ii)** Viscosity depends strongly on temperature: the viscosity of glycerol falls as its temperature rises. The value obtained applies only at the measured temperature, which must stay constant during the experiment."},
{"id": "ial-phy1-015", "type": "written", "spec": "phy1:2.9", "specs": ["phy1:2.5", "phy1:2.6", "phy1:2.7", "phy1:2.8", "phy1:2.10"], "difficulty": 3, "marks": 14, "stem": "A student measures the Young modulus of copper using a long copper wire. One end of the wire is clamped. The wire passes over a pulley and masses hang from its other end. The original length of wire between the clamp and a reference marker fixed to the wire is 2.50 m, and the diameter of the wire is 0.380 mm. The student adds loads and records the extension $\\Delta x$ of the wire from the movement of the marker against a fixed millimetre scale.\n\n| Force $F$ / N | 0 | 3.0 | 6.0 | 9.0 | 12.0 | 15.0 | 18.0 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Extension $\\Delta x$ / mm | 0 | 0.56 | 1.11 | 1.67 | 2.22 | 2.95 | 4.10 |\n\n**(a)** Describe how the student should measure the diameter of the wire to obtain an accurate value. **(2)**\n\n**(b)** Use the data for forces up to 12.0 N to show that the Young modulus of copper is about $1.2 \\times 10^{11}$ Pa. **(4)**\n\n**(c)** Explain what is meant by the limit of proportionality, and use the data to state between which two forces it occurs. **(2)**\n\n**(d)** **(i)** Calculate the elastic strain energy stored in the wire when the force is 12.0 N. **(2)**\n\n**(ii)** Estimate the total work done on the wire as the force is increased from 0 to 18.0 N. **(2)**\n\n**(e)** When the 18.0 N load is removed, the wire does not return to its original length. Explain this observation. **(2)**", "markScheme": "**(a)**\n- **B1** Use a micrometer (screw gauge), checking for zero error\n- **B1** Take readings at several points along the wire (and in perpendicular directions at each point) and calculate the mean\n**(b)**\n- **M1** Cross-sectional area $A = \\dfrac{\\pi d^2}{4} = \\pi(0.190 \\times 10^{-3})^2 = 1.13 \\times 10^{-7}\\ \\mathrm{m^2}$\n- **M1** Use of stress $= F/A$, e.g. $\\dfrac{12.0}{1.13 \\times 10^{-7}} = 1.06 \\times 10^{8}$ Pa\n- **M1** Use of strain $= \\Delta x/x$, e.g. $\\dfrac{2.22 \\times 10^{-3}}{2.50} = 8.88 \\times 10^{-4}$, and $E$ = stress ÷ strain (or stiffness $k = 12.0/2.22 \\times 10^{-3} = 5.41 \\times 10^{3}\\ \\mathrm{N\\,m^{-1}}$ and $E = kx/A$)\n- **A1*** $E = 1.19 \\times 10^{11}$ Pa (accept $1.18 \\times 10^{11}$ to $1.20 \\times 10^{11}$ Pa)\n**(c)**\n- **B1** The limit of proportionality is the point beyond which force (stress) is no longer proportional to extension (strain), i.e. Hooke's law no longer applies\n- **B1** Between 12.0 N and 15.0 N, because each 3.0 N increase up to 12.0 N gives the same extension increase (about 0.55 mm) but from 12.0 N to 15.0 N the extension increases by 0.73 mm\n**(d)(i)**\n- **M1** Use of $\\Delta E_{el} = \\tfrac12 F\\Delta x = \\tfrac12 \\times 12.0 \\times 2.22 \\times 10^{-3}$\n- **A1** $1.33 \\times 10^{-2}$ J\n**(d)(ii)**\n- **M1** Work done = area under the force–extension graph: their (d)(i) plus trapezia $\\tfrac12(12.0 + 15.0)(0.73 \\times 10^{-3}) + \\tfrac12(15.0 + 18.0)(1.15 \\times 10^{-3})$, or an equivalent area estimate such as counting squares\n- **A1** $4.2 \\times 10^{-2}$ J (accept 0.040 J to 0.044 J)\n**(e)**\n- **B1** The wire has been stretched beyond its elastic limit\n- **B1** So it has undergone plastic deformation: part of the extension is permanent and remains when the load is removed", "solution": "**(a)** Use a micrometer screw gauge, first checking it for zero error. Measure the diameter at several points along the wire, and in two perpendicular directions at each point, then calculate the mean.\n\n**(b)**\n$$A = \\frac{\\pi d^2}{4} = \\frac{\\pi(0.380 \\times 10^{-3})^2}{4} = 1.134 \\times 10^{-7}\\ \\mathrm{m^2}$$\nUse the 12.0 N reading, which is in the linear region:\n$$\\text{stress} = \\frac{F}{A} = \\frac{12.0}{1.134 \\times 10^{-7}} = 1.058 \\times 10^{8}\\ \\mathrm{Pa}$$\n$$\\text{strain} = \\frac{\\Delta x}{x} = \\frac{2.22 \\times 10^{-3}}{2.50} = 8.88 \\times 10^{-4}$$\n$$E = \\frac{\\text{stress}}{\\text{strain}} = \\frac{1.058 \\times 10^{8}}{8.88 \\times 10^{-4}} = 1.19 \\times 10^{11}\\ \\mathrm{Pa} \\approx 1.2 \\times 10^{11}\\ \\mathrm{Pa}$$\n\n**(c)** The limit of proportionality is the point beyond which force is no longer proportional to extension, so Hooke's law no longer applies. Up to 12.0 N, each 3.0 N increase adds about 0.55 mm of extension. From 12.0 N to 15.0 N the extension increases by 0.73 mm. The limit of proportionality is therefore between 12.0 N and 15.0 N.\n\n**(d)(i)**\n$$\\Delta E_{el} = \\tfrac12 F\\Delta x = \\tfrac12 \\times 12.0 \\times 2.22 \\times 10^{-3} = 1.33 \\times 10^{-2}\\ \\mathrm{J}$$\n\n**(d)(ii)** Work done = area under the force–extension graph. Above 12.0 N, estimate the area with trapezia:\n$$\\tfrac12(12.0 + 15.0)(0.73 \\times 10^{-3}) + \\tfrac12(15.0 + 18.0)(1.15 \\times 10^{-3}) = 9.86 \\times 10^{-3} + 1.90 \\times 10^{-2}\\ \\mathrm{J}$$\n$$W \\approx 1.33 \\times 10^{-2} + 9.86 \\times 10^{-3} + 1.90 \\times 10^{-2} = 4.2 \\times 10^{-2}\\ \\mathrm{J}$$\n\n**(e)** The wire has been stretched beyond its elastic limit, so it has undergone plastic deformation. That deformation is permanent, so the wire keeps some extension after the load is removed."},
{"id": "ial-phy2-001", "type": "written", "spec": "phy2:3.9", "specs": ["phy2:3.10", "phy2:3.11", "phy2:3.2", "phy2:3.8"], "difficulty": 2, "marks": 14, "stem": "A student investigates stationary waves on a string. One end of a horizontal string is attached to a vibration generator, which is connected to a signal generator. The string passes over a pulley 0.900 m from the vibration generator, and a mass of 0.250 kg hangs from the free end of the string. The mass per unit length of the string is $6.4 \\times 10^{-4}\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m^{-1}}$.\n\n**(a)** Explain how a stationary wave is formed on the string. **(3)**\n\n**(b)** The frequency of the signal generator is slowly increased from a low value.\n\n**(i)** Show that the speed of transverse waves on the string is about $62\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$. **(3)**\n\n**(ii)** At one frequency, a stationary wave with three antinodes is formed between the vibration generator and the pulley. Calculate this frequency. **(3)**\n\n**(c)** For the stationary wave in (b)(ii), point A on the string is 0.10 m from the pulley and point B is 0.40 m from the pulley. State the phase difference between the oscillations of A and B. Give a reason for your answer. **(2)**\n\n**(d)** The student then investigates how the frequency $f$ of the fundamental mode depends on the tension $T$ in the string. The distance between the vibration generator and the pulley is kept at 0.900 m.\n\n**(i)** State one other quantity that must be kept constant. **(1)**\n\n**(ii)** Explain how the student could use a graph to show that $f \\propto \\sqrt{T}$. **(2)**", "markScheme": "**(a)**\n- **B1** The wave travels along the string and is reflected at the pulley (fixed end)\n- **B1** Two waves of the same frequency (and wavelength) travel in opposite directions along the string\n- **B1** The waves superpose: nodes form where the waves are always in antiphase (destructive interference) and antinodes where they are in phase (constructive interference)\n\n**(b)(i)**\n- **M1** Tension $T = mg = 0.250 \\times 9.81 = 2.45\\ \\mathrm{N}$\n- **M1** Uses $v = \\sqrt{T/\\mu}$, e.g. $v = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2.45}{6.4 \\times 10^{-4}}}$\n- **A1*** $v = 61.9\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$ (at least 3 s.f. needed for a show that)\n\n**(b)(ii)**\n- **B1** Three antinodes so $0.900 = \\tfrac{3}{2}\\lambda$, giving $\\lambda = 0.600\\ \\mathrm{m}$\n- **M1** Uses $v = f\\lambda$: $f = \\dfrac{61.9}{0.600}$ (or using 62)\n- **A1** $f = 103\\ \\mathrm{Hz}$ (accept 103 to 104 Hz)\n\n**(c)**\n- **B1** Phase difference $= \\pi$ rad (180°) / A and B are in antiphase\n- **B1** Adjacent nodes are $\\lambda/2 = 0.30\\ \\mathrm{m}$ apart, so there is a node (0.30 m from the pulley) between A and B / A and B are in adjacent loops (segments)\n\n**(d)(i)**\n- **B1** The mass per unit length of the string (use the same string)\n\n**(d)(ii)**\n- **B1** Plot $f$ against $\\sqrt{T}$ (or $f^2$ against $T$)\n- **B1** A straight line through the origin shows that $f \\propto \\sqrt{T}$", "solution": "**(a)** The vibration generator sends a transverse wave along the string. The wave is reflected at the pulley, so two waves of the same frequency and wavelength (and similar amplitude) travel along the string in opposite directions. They superpose. At some points (nodes) the two waves are always in antiphase and cancel, so there is no displacement. At the points midway between nodes (antinodes) they are in phase and the amplitude is a maximum. The pattern does not move along the string, so it is a stationary wave.\n\n**(b)(i)** $T = mg = 0.250 \\times 9.81 = 2.45\\ \\mathrm{N}$\n\n$$v = \\sqrt{\\frac{T}{\\mu}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{2.45}{6.4\\times10^{-4}}} = 61.9\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}} \\approx 62\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$$\n\n**(b)(ii)** Three antinodes means three loops, each $\\lambda/2$ long: $0.900 = \\tfrac{3}{2}\\lambda$, so $\\lambda = 0.600\\ \\mathrm{m}$.\n\n$$f = \\frac{v}{\\lambda} = \\frac{61.9}{0.600} = 103\\ \\mathrm{Hz}$$\n\n**(c)** Adjacent nodes are $\\lambda/2 = 0.30$ m apart, so the nodes are at 0, 0.30 m, 0.60 m and 0.90 m from the pulley. A (0.10 m) is in the first loop and B (0.40 m) is in the second loop, with the node at 0.30 m between them. All points in one loop oscillate in phase, and points in adjacent loops oscillate in antiphase, so the phase difference is $\\pi$ rad (180°).\n\n**(d)(i)** The mass per unit length $\\mu$ of the string (use the same string throughout).\n\n**(d)(ii)** For the fundamental mode $\\lambda = 2L$, so $f = \\dfrac{1}{2L}\\sqrt{\\dfrac{T}{\\mu}}$. With $L$ and $\\mu$ constant, $f \\propto \\sqrt{T}$. Plot $f$ against $\\sqrt{T}$ (or $f^2$ against $T$). A straight line through the origin confirms the relationship."},
{"id": "ial-phy2-002", "type": "written", "spec": "phy2:3.34", "specs": ["phy2:3.33", "phy2:3.35", "phy2:3.36", "phy2:3.32", "phy2:3.31", "phy2:3.12"], "difficulty": 2, "marks": 14, "stem": "A clean metal surface in a vacuum is illuminated with monochromatic light, and photoelectrons are emitted from the surface.\n\n**(a)** State what is meant by the threshold frequency of the metal. **(1)**\n\n**(b)** The maximum kinetic energy $E_{\\mathrm{k\\,max}}$ of the photoelectrons is measured for light of two different frequencies $f$. The results are shown in the table.\n\n| $f$ / $10^{14}\\ \\mathrm{Hz}$ | $E_{\\mathrm{k\\,max}}$ / eV |\n|---|---|\n| 7.5 | 0.79 |\n| 9.5 | 1.62 |\n\n**(i)** Show that these results give a value for the Planck constant of about $6.6 \\times 10^{-34}\\ \\mathrm{J\\,s}$. **(3)**\n\n**(ii)** Calculate the work function of the metal in eV. **(3)**\n\n**(c)** Light of wavelength 420 nm and intensity $0.35\\ \\mathrm{W\\,m^{-2}}$ is incident normally on an area of $1.5\\ \\mathrm{cm^2}$ of the metal surface. Calculate the number of photons incident on this area each second. **(3)**\n\n**(d)** Until the photoelectric effect was investigated, the wave model of light was widely accepted. Explain why the observation that no photoelectrons are emitted below the threshold frequency, however intense the light, cannot be explained by the wave model but can be explained by the photon model. **(4)**", "markScheme": "**(a)**\n- **B1** The minimum frequency of electromagnetic radiation that causes (photo)electrons to be emitted from the surface of the metal\n\n**(b)(i)**\n- **M1** Converts at least one energy from eV to J, e.g. $0.79 \\times 1.6 \\times 10^{-19} = 1.26 \\times 10^{-19}\\ \\mathrm{J}$\n- **M1** Uses $hf = \\phi + E_{\\mathrm{k\\,max}}$ for both sets of results (or gradient idea): $h = \\dfrac{(1.62 - 0.79) \\times 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}}{(9.5 - 7.5) \\times 10^{14}}$\n- **A1*** $h = 6.64 \\times 10^{-34}\\ \\mathrm{J\\,s}$\n\n**(b)(ii)**\n- **M1** Uses $\\phi = hf - E_{\\mathrm{k\\,max}}$, e.g. $\\phi = 6.64 \\times 10^{-34} \\times 7.5 \\times 10^{14} - 1.26 \\times 10^{-19}$ ($= 3.72 \\times 10^{-19}\\ \\mathrm{J}$)\n- **M1** Converts between J and eV correctly (divides by $1.6 \\times 10^{-19}$), or works throughout in eV using $h/e$\n- **A1** $\\phi = 2.3\\ \\mathrm{eV}$ (accept 2.30 to 2.33 eV; using $h = 6.63 \\times 10^{-34}$ or $6.6 \\times 10^{-34}$ is acceptable)\n\n**(c)**\n- **M1** Power incident $P = IA = 0.35 \\times 1.5 \\times 10^{-4} = 5.25 \\times 10^{-5}\\ \\mathrm{W}$\n- **M1** Photon energy $E = \\dfrac{hc}{\\lambda} = \\dfrac{6.63 \\times 10^{-34} \\times 3.00 \\times 10^{8}}{420 \\times 10^{-9}} = 4.74 \\times 10^{-19}\\ \\mathrm{J}$\n- **A1** Number per second $= P/E = 1.1 \\times 10^{14}\\ \\mathrm{s^{-1}}$\n\n**(d)**\n- **B1** Wave model: energy arrives continuously (spread over the wavefront) and the energy delivered depends on the intensity (amplitude), not the frequency\n- **B1** So in the wave model an electron could absorb energy gradually and escape at any frequency if the light were intense enough (or after a long enough time)\n- **B1** Photon model: light consists of photons of energy $E = hf$, and each electron absorbs the energy of one photon (one-to-one interaction)\n- **B1** Below the threshold frequency $hf < \\phi$, so no single photon gives an electron enough energy to escape; greater intensity only increases the number of photons per second, not the energy of each photon", "solution": "**(a)** The threshold frequency is the minimum frequency of electromagnetic radiation that causes photoelectrons to be emitted from the surface of the metal.\n\n**(b)(i)** Use $hf = \\phi + E_{\\mathrm{k\\,max}}$ for both frequencies and subtract (this is the gradient of a graph of $E_{\\mathrm{k\\,max}}$ against $f$):\n\n$$h = \\frac{\\Delta E_{\\mathrm{k\\,max}}}{\\Delta f} = \\frac{(1.62 - 0.79)\\times 1.6\\times10^{-19}}{(9.5-7.5)\\times10^{14}} = \\frac{1.33\\times10^{-19}}{2.0\\times10^{14}} = 6.64\\times10^{-34}\\ \\mathrm{J\\,s}$$\n\nThis is about $6.6\\times10^{-34}\\ \\mathrm{J\\,s}$.\n\n**(b)(ii)** $E_{\\mathrm{k\\,max}} = 0.79 \\times 1.6\\times10^{-19} = 1.26\\times10^{-19}\\ \\mathrm{J}$\n\n$$\\phi = hf - E_{\\mathrm{k\\,max}} = 6.64\\times10^{-34}\\times 7.5\\times10^{14} - 1.26\\times10^{-19} = 3.72\\times10^{-19}\\ \\mathrm{J}$$\n\n$$\\phi = \\frac{3.72\\times10^{-19}}{1.6\\times10^{-19}} = 2.3\\ \\mathrm{eV}$$\n\n**(c)** Power incident on the area: $P = IA = 0.35 \\times 1.5\\times10^{-4} = 5.25\\times10^{-5}\\ \\mathrm{W}$\n\nEnergy of one photon: $E = \\dfrac{hc}{\\lambda} = \\dfrac{6.63\\times10^{-34}\\times3.00\\times10^{8}}{420\\times10^{-9}} = 4.74\\times10^{-19}\\ \\mathrm{J}$\n\nNumber of photons per second $= \\dfrac{5.25\\times10^{-5}}{4.74\\times10^{-19}} = 1.1\\times10^{14}\\ \\mathrm{s^{-1}}$\n\n**(d)** In the wave model, energy arrives continuously, spread over the whole wavefront, and the energy delivered depends on the intensity (amplitude) of the wave, not its frequency. An electron should therefore absorb energy gradually until it has enough to escape, so with intense enough light (or after waiting long enough) emission should happen at any frequency. This is not what is observed.\n\nIn the photon model, light consists of photons, each with energy $E = hf$. Each electron absorbs the energy of one photon only (a one-to-one interaction). Below the threshold frequency $hf < \\phi$, so no single photon has enough energy to release an electron. Increasing the intensity only increases the number of photons arriving per second, not the energy of each photon, so still no electrons are emitted."},
{"id": "ial-phy2-003", "type": "written", "spec": "phy2:3.15", "specs": ["phy2:3.13", "phy2:3.14", "phy2:3.16"], "difficulty": 3, "marks": 13, "stem": "An optical fibre consists of a glass core of refractive index 1.52 surrounded by a cladding of refractive index 1.46.\n\n**(a)** A student determines the refractive index of a block made from the core glass. She directs rays of light from air into the block at several different angles of incidence $i$ and measures the corresponding angles of refraction $r$. She plots a graph of $\\sin i$ (vertical axis) against $\\sin r$ (horizontal axis) and obtains a straight line through the origin with a gradient of 1.52. Explain why the gradient of this graph is equal to the refractive index of the glass. **(2)**\n\n**(b)** Explain, in terms of the speed of light, why a ray of light that enters the glass at an angle to the normal changes direction. **(2)**\n\n**(c)** The fibre is straight and its flat end face is perpendicular to the axis of the fibre. A ray of light in air strikes the end face at an angle of incidence of 30°. The ray refracts into the core and travels to the core–cladding boundary.\n\n**(i)** Calculate the critical angle for the core–cladding boundary. **(2)**\n\n**(ii)** Determine whether the ray is totally internally reflected when it reaches the core–cladding boundary. **(4)**\n\n**(iii)** Calculate the maximum angle of incidence at the end face for which light entering the core will be totally internally reflected at the core–cladding boundary. **(3)**", "markScheme": "**(a)**\n- **B1** Snell's law with $n_{\\text{air}} = 1$ gives $1 \\times \\sin i = n \\sin r$, so $\\sin i = n \\sin r$\n- **B1** Comparing with $y = mx$ (straight line through the origin), the gradient $\\dfrac{\\sin i}{\\sin r}$ equals $n$\n\n**(b)**\n- **B1** Light travels more slowly in glass than in air ($v = c/n$)\n- **B1** The part of the wavefront that enters the glass first slows down first, so the wavefront changes direction and the ray bends towards the normal (oe, e.g. wavelength decreases while frequency is unchanged)\n\n**(c)(i)**\n- **M1** Uses $n_1 \\sin C = n_2$: $\\sin C = \\dfrac{1.46}{1.52}$ ($= 0.961$)\n- **A1** $C = 73.8°$ (accept 73.8° or 73.9°)\n\n**(c)(ii)**\n- **M1** Uses Snell's law at the end face: $1.00 \\times \\sin 30° = 1.52 \\sin r$\n- **A1** $r = 19.2°$\n- **M1** Angle of incidence at the core–cladding boundary $= 90° - r = 70.8°$ (ft their $r$)\n- **E1** $70.8° < 73.8°$ (less than the critical angle), so the ray is not totally internally reflected; some light is refracted into the cladding (conclusion must be consistent with their angles)\n\n**(c)(iii)**\n- **M1** Maximum angle of refraction at the end face $= 90° - C = 16.2°$ (ft their $C$)\n- **M1** Uses $\\sin \\theta_{\\max} = 1.52 \\sin 16.2°$ ($= 0.424$)\n- **A1** $\\theta_{\\max} = 25°$ (accept 24.9° to 25.1°)", "solution": "**(a)** Snell's law at the air–glass boundary: $n_{\\text{air}}\\sin i = n\\sin r$ with $n_{\\text{air}} = 1$, so $\\sin i = n\\sin r$. This has the form $y = mx$, so a graph of $\\sin i$ against $\\sin r$ is a straight line through the origin whose gradient is $n$.\n\n**(b)** Light travels more slowly in glass than in air ($v = c/n$). When a wavefront meets the boundary at an angle, the part that enters the glass first slows down first, while the rest is still moving faster in air. The wavefront therefore changes direction, so the ray bends towards the normal. (The frequency is unchanged, so the wavelength decreases.)\n\n**(c)(i)** $n_1\\sin C = n_2$:\n\n$$\\sin C = \\frac{1.46}{1.52} = 0.961 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad C = 73.8^\\circ$$\n\n**(c)(ii)** At the end face: $1.00\\times\\sin30^\\circ = 1.52\\sin r$, so $\\sin r = 0.329$ and $r = 19.2^\\circ$.\n\nThe end face is perpendicular to the core–cladding boundary, so the angle of incidence at the boundary is $90^\\circ - 19.2^\\circ = 70.8^\\circ$.\n\nSince $70.8^\\circ < 73.8^\\circ$ (less than the critical angle), the ray is not totally internally reflected. Part of the light is refracted into the cladding.\n\n**(c)(iii)** For total internal reflection, the angle of incidence at the boundary must be at least $C$. So the angle of refraction at the end face must be no more than $90^\\circ - 73.8^\\circ = 16.2^\\circ$.\n\n$$\\sin\\theta_{\\max} = 1.52\\sin16.2^\\circ = 0.424 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\theta_{\\max} = 25^\\circ$$\n\nAny ray entering at more than about $25^\\circ$, such as the $30^\\circ$ ray in (c)(ii), is not totally internally reflected."},
{"id": "ial-phy2-004", "type": "written", "spec": "phy2:3.22", "specs": ["phy2:3.17", "phy2:3.18", "phy2:3.19", "phy2:3.20", "phy2:3.21", "phy2:3.23"], "difficulty": 2, "marks": 14, "stem": "A student investigates thin lenses.\n\n**(a)** Explain what is meant by the focal length of a diverging lens. **(2)**\n\n**(b)** A converging lens has a power of +8.0 D. An object of height 2.4 cm is placed on the principal axis, 20.0 cm from the lens.\n\n**(i)** Calculate the distance of the image from the lens. **(3)**\n\n**(ii)** Calculate the height of the image. **(2)**\n\n**(iii)** Describe how two rays from the top of the object could be drawn on a scale ray diagram to locate the top of the image. **(2)**\n\n**(c)** A thin diverging lens of power −3.0 D is placed in contact with the converging lens to form a lens combination. The object is moved so that it is 12.0 cm from the combination.\n\n**(i)** Show that the focal length of the combination is 0.20 m. **(2)**\n\n**(ii)** Calculate the image distance and state, with a reason, whether the image is real or virtual. **(3)**", "markScheme": "**(a)**\n- **B1** Rays parallel to the principal axis diverge after passing through the lens and appear to come from a single point on the axis (the principal focus), on the same side of the lens as the incident light\n- **B1** The focal length is the distance from the (optical centre of the) lens to this principal focus\n\n**(b)(i)**\n- **B1** $f = 1/P = 1/8.0 = 0.125\\ \\mathrm{m}$\n- **M1** Uses $\\dfrac{1}{u} + \\dfrac{1}{v} = \\dfrac{1}{f}$: $\\dfrac{1}{v} = \\dfrac{1}{0.125} - \\dfrac{1}{0.200}$\n- **A1** $v = 0.333\\ \\mathrm{m}$ (33.3 cm)\n\n**(b)(ii)**\n- **M1** Magnification $m = v/u = 0.333/0.200 = 1.67$ (ft their $v$)\n- **A1** Image height $= 1.67 \\times 2.4 = 4.0\\ \\mathrm{cm}$ (ft)\n\n**(b)(iii)**\n- **B1** One correct ray from the top of the object, e.g. parallel to the principal axis, then refracted through the principal focus on the far side of the lens\n- **B1** A second different correct ray, e.g. through the optical centre undeviated (or through the near principal focus, emerging parallel to the axis), and the top of the image is where the refracted rays cross\n\n**(c)(i)**\n- **M1** $P = P_1 + P_2 = 8.0 + (-3.0) = 5.0\\ \\mathrm{D}$\n- **A1*** $f = 1/5.0 = 0.20\\ \\mathrm{m}$\n\n**(c)(ii)**\n- **M1** $\\dfrac{1}{v} = \\dfrac{1}{0.20} - \\dfrac{1}{0.12}$ ($= -3.33\\ \\mathrm{m^{-1}}$)\n- **A1** $v = -0.30\\ \\mathrm{m}$ (accept 0.30 m on the same side of the lens as the object)\n- **B1** Virtual, because $v$ is negative (real-is-positive): the rays only appear to come from the image and it cannot be formed on a screen (oe)", "solution": "**(a)** Rays parallel to the principal axis diverge after passing through a diverging lens and appear to come from a single point on the axis, on the same side of the lens as the incident light. This point is the principal focus. The focal length is the distance from the optical centre of the lens to the principal focus (negative in the real-is-positive convention).\n\n**(b)(i)** $f = \\dfrac{1}{P} = \\dfrac{1}{8.0} = 0.125\\ \\mathrm{m}$\n\n$$\\frac{1}{v} = \\frac{1}{f} - \\frac{1}{u} = \\frac{1}{0.125} - \\frac{1}{0.200} = 8.0 - 5.0 = 3.0\\ \\mathrm{m^{-1}}$$\n\n$v = 0.333\\ \\mathrm{m}$ (33.3 cm), on the opposite side of the lens from the object.\n\n**(b)(ii)** $m = \\dfrac{v}{u} = \\dfrac{0.333}{0.200} = 1.67$, so image height $= 1.67 \\times 2.4 = 4.0\\ \\mathrm{cm}$. The image is real, inverted and magnified.\n\n**(b)(iii)** Ray 1: from the top of the object parallel to the principal axis; after the lens it passes through the principal focus on the far side. Ray 2: from the top of the object through the optical centre of the lens, undeviated. (Alternatively, a ray through the principal focus on the object side emerges parallel to the axis.) The top of the image is where the refracted rays cross.\n\n**(c)(i)** $P = P_1 + P_2 = (+8.0) + (-3.0) = +5.0\\ \\mathrm{D}$, so $f = \\dfrac{1}{5.0} = 0.20\\ \\mathrm{m}$.\n\n**(c)(ii)**\n\n$$\\frac{1}{v} = \\frac{1}{0.20} - \\frac{1}{0.12} = 5.0 - 8.33 = -3.33\\ \\mathrm{m^{-1}} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad v = -0.30\\ \\mathrm{m}$$\n\nThe image is 0.30 m from the lens, on the same side as the object. It is virtual: $v$ is negative in the real-is-positive convention. The rays only appear to come from the image and do not actually meet there, so it cannot be formed on a screen."},
{"id": "ial-phy2-005", "type": "written", "spec": "phy2:3.26", "specs": ["phy2:3.25", "phy2:3.27", "phy2:3.2"], "difficulty": 2, "marks": 13, "stem": "**(a)** Describe how Huygens' construction can be used to explain what happens when plane wavefronts pass through a gap whose width is similar to the wavelength of the waves. **(3)**\n\n**(b)** A student uses a laser and a diffraction grating with 300 lines per mm to determine the wavelength of the laser light. The laser beam is incident normally on the grating, and a pattern of bright spots is formed on a screen 1.80 m from the grating. The student measures the distance between the two second-order maxima, one on each side of the central maximum, as 1.21 m.\n\n**(i)** Calculate the wavelength of the laser light. **(4)**\n\n**(ii)** Give one advantage of measuring the distance between the two second-order maxima rather than the distance from the central maximum to one first-order maximum. **(1)**\n\n**(iii)** Determine the highest order of maximum that can be observed with this grating and laser. **(2)**\n\n**(c)** The laser is replaced with a different laser that emits light of frequency $4.4 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz. Calculate the angle between the central maximum and the first-order maximum. **(3)**", "markScheme": "**(a)**\n- **B1** Every point on a wavefront acts as a source of secondary (circular/spherical) wavelets, which spread out at the speed of the wave\n- **B1** The new wavefront is the envelope (common tangent) of the secondary wavelets\n- **B1** At the gap only part of the wavefront passes; wavelets from points near the edges spread into the region behind the barrier, so the emerging wavefronts are curved and the wave spreads out (diffracts)\n\n**(b)(i)**\n- **B1** $d = \\dfrac{1}{3.00 \\times 10^{5}} = 3.33 \\times 10^{-6}\\ \\mathrm{m}$\n- **M1** $\\tan \\theta = \\dfrac{0.605}{1.80}$, giving $\\theta = 18.6°$\n- **M1** Uses $n\\lambda = d \\sin \\theta$ with $n = 2$\n- **A1** $\\lambda = 5.3 \\times 10^{-7}\\ \\mathrm{m}$ (accept 531 nm; using $\\sin\\theta \\approx \\tan\\theta$, giving 5.6 × 10⁻⁷ m, loses this mark)\n\n**(b)(ii)**\n- **B1** The distance measured is larger, so the percentage uncertainty is smaller\n\n**(b)(iii)**\n- **M1** Uses $\\sin\\theta = 1$ (θ = 90°): $n = \\dfrac{d}{\\lambda} = \\dfrac{3.33 \\times 10^{-6}}{5.31 \\times 10^{-7}} = 6.3$ (ft their $\\lambda$)\n- **A1** Highest order = 6 (must be a whole number rounded down; ft)\n\n**(c)**\n- **M1** $\\lambda = c/f = \\dfrac{3.00 \\times 10^{8}}{4.4 \\times 10^{14}} = 6.82 \\times 10^{-7}\\ \\mathrm{m}$\n- **M1** $\\sin \\theta = \\dfrac{\\lambda}{d} = \\dfrac{6.82 \\times 10^{-7}}{3.33 \\times 10^{-6}}$ ($= 0.205$)\n- **A1** $\\theta = 11.8°$", "solution": "**(a)** In Huygens' construction every point on a wavefront acts as a source of secondary circular (spherical) wavelets, which travel outwards at the speed of the wave. The new position of the wavefront is the envelope (common tangent) of these wavelets. At a gap, only the part of the wavefront inside the gap continues. The wavelets from points near the edges of the gap spread into the region behind the barrier, so the emerging wavefronts are curved and the wave spreads out (diffracts). The effect is most noticeable when the gap width is similar to the wavelength.\n\n**(b)(i)** $d = \\dfrac{1}{300 \\times 10^3} = 3.33\\times10^{-6}\\ \\mathrm{m}$\n\nThe distance from the central maximum to one second-order maximum is $1.21/2 = 0.605\\ \\mathrm{m}$.\n\n$$\\tan\\theta = \\frac{0.605}{1.80} = 0.336 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\theta = 18.6^\\circ$$\n\n$$\\lambda = \\frac{d\\sin\\theta}{n} = \\frac{3.33\\times10^{-6}\\times\\sin18.6^\\circ}{2} = 5.3\\times10^{-7}\\ \\mathrm{m}\\ (531\\ \\mathrm{nm})$$\n\n**(b)(ii)** The distance measured is larger, so the percentage uncertainty in the measurement is smaller.\n\n**(b)(iii)** The largest possible angle is $90^\\circ$, where $\\sin\\theta = 1$:\n\n$$n = \\frac{d}{\\lambda} = \\frac{3.33\\times10^{-6}}{5.31\\times10^{-7}} = 6.3$$\n\n$n$ must be a whole number, so the highest order observed is $n = 6$.\n\n**(c)** $\\lambda = \\dfrac{c}{f} = \\dfrac{3.00\\times10^{8}}{4.4\\times10^{14}} = 6.82\\times10^{-7}\\ \\mathrm{m}$\n\n$$\\sin\\theta = \\frac{\\lambda}{d} = \\frac{6.82\\times10^{-7}}{3.33\\times10^{-6}} = 0.205 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\theta = 11.8^\\circ$$"},
{"id": "ial-phy2-006", "type": "written", "spec": "phy2:4.16", "specs": ["phy2:4.15", "phy2:4.6", "phy2:4.7", "phy2:4.4", "phy2:4.5"], "difficulty": 2, "marks": 12, "stem": "A student investigates the e.m.f. $\\varepsilon$ and internal resistance $r$ of a cell. The cell is connected in series with an ammeter, a switch and a variable resistor. A voltmeter is connected across the terminals of the cell. The student records the terminal potential difference $V$ for several values of the current $I$.\n\n**(a)** Explain why the terminal potential difference decreases as the current increases. **(2)**\n\n**(b)** The student plots a graph of $V$ (vertical axis) against $I$ (horizontal axis). The best-fit straight line passes through the points (0.10 A, 1.48 V) and (0.70 A, 1.12 V). Determine $\\varepsilon$ and $r$. **(4)**\n\n**(c)** Suggest why the student opened the switch between readings. **(1)**\n\n**(d)** The cell is then connected to two 2.0 Ω resistors that are connected in parallel with each other.\n\n**(i)** Calculate the current in the cell. **(3)**\n\n**(ii)** Calculate the power dissipated in one of the 2.0 Ω resistors. **(2)**", "markScheme": "**(a)**\n- **B1** The cell has internal resistance, so energy is transferred (dissipated) inside the cell when there is a current (lost volts $= Ir$)\n- **B1** $V = \\varepsilon - Ir$, so as $I$ increases the lost volts increase and the terminal p.d. decreases\n\n**(b)**\n- **M1** Gradient $= \\dfrac{1.12 - 1.48}{0.70 - 0.10} = -0.60$\n- **A1** $r = 0.60\\ \\Omega$ (r = − gradient)\n- **M1** $\\varepsilon = V + Ir = 1.48 + 0.10 \\times 0.60$ (or extrapolates the line to $I = 0$)\n- **A1** $\\varepsilon = 1.54\\ \\mathrm{V}$\n\n**(c)**\n- **B1** To stop the cell heating up, which would change its internal resistance (or to stop the cell running down so that the e.m.f. stays constant)\n\n**(d)(i)**\n- **M1** Parallel combination: $\\dfrac{1}{R} = \\dfrac{1}{2.0} + \\dfrac{1}{2.0}$, so $R = 1.0\\ \\Omega$\n- **M1** $I = \\dfrac{\\varepsilon}{R + r} = \\dfrac{1.54}{1.0 + 0.60}$ (ft their $\\varepsilon$ and $r$)\n- **A1** $I = 0.96\\ \\mathrm{A}$\n\n**(d)(ii)**\n- **M1** Current in each resistor $= 0.96/2 = 0.48\\ \\mathrm{A}$ and uses $P = I^2R$ (or terminal p.d. $= 0.96\\ \\mathrm{V}$ and uses $P = V^2/R$)\n- **A1** $P = 0.46\\ \\mathrm{W}$ (ft their $I$)", "solution": "**(a)** The cell has internal resistance $r$. When there is a current, energy is transferred inside the cell as charge is driven through this internal resistance, so the p.d. across the terminals is less than the e.m.f.: $V = \\varepsilon - Ir$. As $I$ increases, the lost volts $Ir$ increase, so $V$ decreases.\n\n**(b)** Compare $V = \\varepsilon - Ir$ with $y = mx + c$: the gradient is $-r$ and the intercept on the $V$ axis is $\\varepsilon$.\n\n$$\\text{gradient} = \\frac{1.12 - 1.48}{0.70 - 0.10} = \\frac{-0.36}{0.60} = -0.60\\ \\Omega \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad r = 0.60\\ \\Omega$$\n\n$$\\varepsilon = V + Ir = 1.48 + 0.10\\times0.60 = 1.54\\ \\mathrm{V}$$\n\n**(c)** So that the cell does not heat up, which would change its internal resistance, and does not run down, which would change its e.m.f.\n\n**(d)(i)** $\\dfrac{1}{R} = \\dfrac{1}{2.0} + \\dfrac{1}{2.0}$, so $R = 1.0\\ \\Omega$.\n\n$$I = \\frac{\\varepsilon}{R + r} = \\frac{1.54}{1.0 + 0.60} = 0.96\\ \\mathrm{A}$$\n\n**(d)(ii)** The two resistors are identical, so the current divides equally: $I_1 = 0.96/2 = 0.48\\ \\mathrm{A}$.\n\n$$P = I_1^2R = 0.48^2\\times2.0 = 0.46\\ \\mathrm{W}$$\n\n(Or: terminal p.d. $= 0.96 \\times 1.0 = 0.96\\ \\mathrm{V}$, so $P = V^2/R = 0.96^2/2.0 = 0.46\\ \\mathrm{W}$.)"},
{"id": "ial-phy2-007", "type": "written", "spec": "phy2:4.10", "specs": ["phy2:4.9", "phy2:4.11"], "difficulty": 1, "marks": 11, "stem": "A student carries out an experiment to determine the resistivity of the metal used to make a wire. She measures the diameter of the wire with a micrometer screw gauge, and then measures the resistance $R$ of different lengths $l$ of the wire.\n\n**(a)** The student measures the diameter at several points along the wire, and in different directions at each point. Explain why. **(1)**\n\n**(b)** The mean diameter of the wire is 0.38 mm. The student plots a graph of $R$ against $l$. The gradient of the best-fit line is $9.5\\ \\Omega\\,\\mathrm{m^{-1}}$. Calculate the resistivity of the metal. **(3)**\n\n**(c)** The best-fit line does not pass through the origin but has a small positive intercept on the $R$ axis. Suggest a reason for this, and explain why it does not affect the value of resistivity calculated from the gradient. **(2)**\n\n**(d)** A copper wire with the same diameter carries a current of 1.5 A. Copper has $8.5 \\times 10^{28}$ conduction electrons per cubic metre. Calculate the drift velocity of the conduction electrons in the copper wire. **(3)**\n\n**(e)** The resistivity of a semiconductor such as silicon is very much greater than the resistivity of a metal. Explain this with reference to the equation $I = nqvA$. **(2)**", "markScheme": "**(a)**\n- **B1** The wire may not be uniform (or perfectly circular) in cross-section, so a mean is more representative / reduces the effect of random errors\n\n**(b)**\n- **M1** $A = \\dfrac{\\pi d^2}{4} = \\pi \\times (0.19 \\times 10^{-3})^2 = 1.13 \\times 10^{-7}\\ \\mathrm{m^2}$\n- **M1** $R = \\rho l / A$, so gradient $= \\rho / A$, giving $\\rho = 9.5 \\times 1.13 \\times 10^{-7}$\n- **A1** $\\rho = 1.1 \\times 10^{-6}\\ \\Omega\\,\\mathrm{m}$ (accept 1.07 to 1.08 × 10⁻⁶ Ω m)\n\n**(c)**\n- **B1** Resistance of the connecting leads or contacts (or a zero error on the meter)\n- **B1** This adds the same constant resistance to every reading, so the gradient (and hence $\\rho$) is unchanged\n\n**(d)**\n- **M1** Rearranges $I = nqvA$ to $v = \\dfrac{I}{nqA}$\n- **M1** $v = \\dfrac{1.5}{8.5 \\times 10^{28} \\times 1.6 \\times 10^{-19} \\times 1.13 \\times 10^{-7}}$ (ft their $A$)\n- **A1** $v = 9.7 \\times 10^{-4}\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$\n\n**(e)**\n- **B1** The number of charge carriers per unit volume $n$ is very much smaller in a semiconductor than in a metal\n- **B1** So for a sample of the same dimensions with the same p.d. (similar drift velocity), the current is much smaller, so the resistance and hence the resistivity is much greater", "solution": "**(a)** The wire may not have a uniform (or perfectly circular) cross-section. Taking readings at several points and in different directions, then calculating a mean, gives a more representative value of the diameter and reduces the effect of random errors.\n\n**(b)** $A = \\dfrac{\\pi d^2}{4} = \\pi\\times(0.19\\times10^{-3})^2 = 1.13\\times10^{-7}\\ \\mathrm{m^2}$\n\nSince $R = \\dfrac{\\rho}{A}l$, the gradient of the $R$ against $l$ graph is $\\dfrac{\\rho}{A}$:\n\n$$\\rho = \\text{gradient}\\times A = 9.5\\times1.13\\times10^{-7} = 1.1\\times10^{-6}\\ \\Omega\\,\\mathrm{m}$$\n\n**(c)** The intercept is caused by the resistance of the connecting leads and contacts (or a zero error). This adds the same extra resistance to every reading. It shifts the line up but does not change its gradient, so $\\rho$ calculated from the gradient is unaffected.\n\n**(d)**\n\n$$v = \\frac{I}{nqA} = \\frac{1.5}{8.5\\times10^{28}\\times1.6\\times10^{-19}\\times1.13\\times10^{-7}} = 9.7\\times10^{-4}\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$$\n\n**(e)** In a semiconductor the number of charge carriers per unit volume, $n$, is very much smaller than in a metal. From $I = nqvA$, a sample of the same dimensions with the same p.d. across it (so a similar drift velocity) carries a much smaller current. The resistance, and therefore the resistivity, is much greater."},
{"id": "ial-phy2-008", "type": "written", "spec": "phy2:4.14", "specs": ["phy2:4.13", "phy2:4.17", "phy2:4.6", "phy2:4.4", "phy2:4.5"], "difficulty": 3, "marks": 14, "stem": "A temperature-sensing circuit consists of a negative temperature coefficient (NTC) thermistor connected in series with a fixed resistor of resistance 2.2 kΩ across a 9.0 V supply of negligible internal resistance. The output potential difference $V_{\\text{out}}$ is the potential difference across the fixed resistor.\n\n**(a)** Explain, in terms of the charge carriers and the lattice, why the resistance of the NTC thermistor decreases as its temperature increases. **(3)**\n\n**(b)** At 20 °C the resistance of the thermistor is 6.4 kΩ. Calculate $V_{\\text{out}}$ at this temperature. **(2)**\n\n**(c)** The output terminals are connected to an alarm unit. The alarm unit acts as a resistor of resistance 4.0 kΩ connected in parallel with the fixed resistor. The alarm sounds when the potential difference across it reaches 6.0 V.\n\n**(i)** Use conservation of charge and conservation of energy to show that the total resistance $R$ of two resistors $R_1$ and $R_2$ connected in parallel is given by $\\dfrac{1}{R} = \\dfrac{1}{R_1} + \\dfrac{1}{R_2}$. **(3)**\n\n**(ii)** Calculate the resistance of the thermistor when the alarm sounds. **(4)**\n\n**(d)** Explain why the alarm sounds at a higher temperature than it would if the alarm unit had a very high resistance. **(2)**", "markScheme": "**(a)**\n- **B1** As temperature increases, more electrons gain enough energy to become conduction electrons, so the number of charge carriers per unit volume $n$ increases\n- **B1** The lattice ions vibrate with greater amplitude, so conduction electrons collide with them more often (which on its own would increase the resistance)\n- **B1** The increase in the number of charge carriers has the greater effect, so the current for a given p.d. increases ($I = nqvA$) and the resistance decreases\n\n**(b)**\n- **M1** $V_{\\text{out}} = 9.0 \\times \\dfrac{2.2}{2.2 + 6.4}$\n- **A1** $V_{\\text{out}} = 2.3\\ \\mathrm{V}$\n\n**(c)(i)**\n- **B1** Conservation of charge: the current divides at the junction, so $I = I_1 + I_2$\n- **B1** Conservation of energy: the p.d. $V$ is the same across each parallel branch, so $I_1 = V/R_1$, $I_2 = V/R_2$ and $I = V/R$\n- **B1** Substitutes to give $\\dfrac{V}{R} = \\dfrac{V}{R_1} + \\dfrac{V}{R_2}$ and cancels $V$ to obtain $\\dfrac{1}{R} = \\dfrac{1}{R_1} + \\dfrac{1}{R_2}$\n\n**(c)(ii)**\n- **M1** Fixed resistor and alarm in parallel: $\\dfrac{1}{R} = \\dfrac{1}{2.2} + \\dfrac{1}{4.0}$\n- **A1** $R = 1.42\\ \\mathrm{k\\Omega}$\n- **M1** P.d. across thermistor $= 9.0 - 6.0 = 3.0\\ \\mathrm{V}$ and uses $\\dfrac{R_{\\text{th}}}{1.42} = \\dfrac{3.0}{6.0}$ (or $I = 6.0/1420 = 4.23\\ \\mathrm{mA}$ and $R_{\\text{th}} = 3.0/I$)\n- **A1** $R_{\\text{th}} = 710\\ \\Omega$ (accept 0.71 kΩ; ft their parallel resistance)\n\n**(d)**\n- **B1** Without loading, $V_{\\text{out}} = 6.0$ V when $R_{\\text{th}} = 1.1\\ \\mathrm{k\\Omega}$; with the alarm connected, the lower part of the divider has a smaller resistance (1.42 kΩ), so the thermistor resistance must fall to a lower value (710 Ω) before the alarm sounds (ft their (c)(ii))\n- **B1** The resistance of an NTC thermistor decreases as temperature increases, so this lower resistance is reached at a higher temperature", "solution": "**(a)** As the temperature of the thermistor increases, more electrons gain enough energy to become conduction electrons, so the number of charge carriers per unit volume $n$ increases. The lattice ions also vibrate with larger amplitude, so conduction electrons collide with them more often, which on its own would increase the resistance. In an NTC thermistor the increase in $n$ has much the greater effect, so for a given p.d. the current increases ($I = nqvA$) and the resistance decreases.\n\n**(b)**\n\n$$V_{\\text{out}} = V_{\\text{in}}\\times\\frac{R_2}{R_1 + R_2} = 9.0\\times\\frac{2.2}{2.2 + 6.4} = 2.3\\ \\mathrm{V}$$\n\n**(c)(i)** Charge is conserved, so the current $I$ entering the junction equals the sum of the branch currents: $I = I_1 + I_2$. Energy is conserved, so the p.d. $V$ across each parallel branch is the same as the p.d. across the combination. So $I = V/R$, $I_1 = V/R_1$ and $I_2 = V/R_2$:\n\n$$\\frac{V}{R} = \\frac{V}{R_1} + \\frac{V}{R_2} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\frac{1}{R} = \\frac{1}{R_1} + \\frac{1}{R_2}$$\n\n**(c)(ii)** The fixed resistor and the alarm are in parallel:\n\n$$\\frac{1}{R} = \\frac{1}{2.2} + \\frac{1}{4.0} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad R = 1.42\\ \\mathrm{k\\Omega}$$\n\nWhen the alarm sounds, the p.d. across the parallel combination is 6.0 V, so the p.d. across the thermistor is $9.0 - 6.0 = 3.0\\ \\mathrm{V}$. The same current passes through both parts of the divider, so\n\n$$\\frac{R_{\\text{th}}}{1.42\\ \\mathrm{k\\Omega}} = \\frac{3.0}{6.0} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad R_{\\text{th}} = 0.71\\ \\mathrm{k\\Omega} = 710\\ \\Omega$$\n\n**(d)** If the alarm had a very high resistance, the lower part of the divider would be just 2.2 kΩ, and $V_{\\text{out}} = 6.0$ V would need $R_{\\text{th}} = 2.2/2 = 1.1\\ \\mathrm{k\\Omega}$. With the 4.0 kΩ alarm in parallel, the lower part is only 1.42 kΩ, so the thermistor resistance must fall further, to 710 Ω, before $V_{\\text{out}}$ reaches 6.0 V. The resistance of an NTC thermistor decreases as temperature increases, so this lower resistance is reached at a higher temperature."},
{"id": "ial-phy2-009", "type": "written", "spec": "phy2:3.37", "specs": ["phy2:3.28", "phy2:3.32", "phy2:3.35"], "difficulty": 3, "marks": 14, "stem": "The table shows some of the energy levels of the atoms of a gas.\n\n| Level | Energy / eV |\n|---|---|\n| $n = 4$ | $-1.40$ |\n| $n = 3$ | $-3.10$ |\n| $n = 2$ | $-5.20$ |\n| $n = 1$ (ground state) | $-9.80$ |\n\n**(a)** When a high potential difference is applied across a tube containing the gas, the gas emits light. When viewed through a diffraction grating, the light forms a line spectrum. Explain how the line spectrum is produced. **(2)**\n\n**(b)** Calculate the wavelength of the photon emitted when an electron in an atom moves from level $n = 3$ to level $n = 2$. **(3)**\n\n**(c)** A free electron with a kinetic energy of 6.0 eV collides with an atom of the gas in its ground state.\n\n**(i)** Determine the possible values of the kinetic energy of the free electron after the collision. **(3)**\n\n**(ii)** Calculate the de Broglie wavelength of the free electron before the collision. **(3)**\n\n**(d)** In an electron diffraction experiment, a beam of electrons is directed at a thin film of graphite in which the spacing between carbon atoms is about $1.4 \\times 10^{-10}$ m. A pattern of concentric rings is observed on a fluorescent screen. Explain what this observation shows about electrons, and why the electrons used must have a much greater kinetic energy than 6.0 eV. **(3)**", "markScheme": "**(a)**\n- **B1** An electron in an excited atom moves from a higher to a lower energy level, emitting a photon whose energy equals the difference between the levels, $hf = E_1 - E_2$\n- **B1** The energy levels are discrete, so only certain photon energies, and so only certain frequencies (wavelengths), are emitted; each line corresponds to one transition\n\n**(b)**\n- **M1** $\\Delta E = -3.10 - (-5.20) = 2.10\\ \\mathrm{eV} = 2.10 \\times 1.6 \\times 10^{-19} = 3.36 \\times 10^{-19}\\ \\mathrm{J}$\n- **M1** Uses $\\lambda = \\dfrac{hc}{\\Delta E} = \\dfrac{6.63 \\times 10^{-34} \\times 3.00 \\times 10^{8}}{3.36 \\times 10^{-19}}$\n- **A1** $\\lambda = 5.9 \\times 10^{-7}\\ \\mathrm{m}$ (accept 592 nm)\n\n**(c)(i)**\n- **B1** Energy needed to excite from $n = 1$ to $n = 2$ is 4.60 eV, and from $n = 1$ to $n = 3$ is 6.70 eV\n- **B1** Only excitation to $n = 2$ is possible (6.0 eV < 6.70 eV), leaving the electron with $6.0 - 4.60 = 1.4\\ \\mathrm{eV}$\n- **B1** If the atom is not excited, the electron keeps 6.0 eV, so the possible values are 6.0 eV and 1.4 eV\n\n**(c)(ii)**\n- **M1** $E_\\mathrm{k} = 6.0 \\times 1.6 \\times 10^{-19} = 9.6 \\times 10^{-19}\\ \\mathrm{J}$ and $p = \\sqrt{2mE_\\mathrm{k}} = \\sqrt{2 \\times 9.11 \\times 10^{-31} \\times 9.6 \\times 10^{-19}} = 1.32 \\times 10^{-24}\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$ (or $v = 1.45 \\times 10^{6}\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$ and $p = mv$)\n- **M1** Uses $\\lambda = \\dfrac{h}{p} = \\dfrac{6.63 \\times 10^{-34}}{1.32 \\times 10^{-24}}$\n- **A1** $\\lambda = 5.0 \\times 10^{-10}\\ \\mathrm{m}$\n\n**(d)**\n- **B1** The rings are a diffraction pattern; diffraction is a property of waves, so electrons have a wave nature (behave as waves)\n- **B1** Significant diffraction only occurs when the wavelength is similar to the gap (the spacing between the atoms)\n- **B1** 6.0 eV electrons have $\\lambda = 5.0 \\times 10^{-10}$ m, too large compared with $1.4 \\times 10^{-10}$ m; a greater kinetic energy gives a greater momentum and so a smaller wavelength ($\\lambda = h/p$), comparable to the atomic spacing (ft their (c)(ii))", "solution": "**(a)** The p.d. accelerates electrons, which collide with gas atoms and excite them, raising atomic electrons to higher energy levels. When an electron falls back to a lower level, it emits a photon with energy equal to the difference between the two levels, $hf = E_1 - E_2$. Because the energy levels are discrete, only certain energy differences are possible, so only certain frequencies (wavelengths) are emitted. Each bright line corresponds to one particular transition.\n\n**(b)** $\\Delta E = -3.10 - (-5.20) = 2.10\\ \\mathrm{eV} = 2.10\\times1.6\\times10^{-19} = 3.36\\times10^{-19}\\ \\mathrm{J}$\n\n$$\\lambda = \\frac{hc}{\\Delta E} = \\frac{6.63\\times10^{-34}\\times3.00\\times10^{8}}{3.36\\times10^{-19}} = 5.9\\times10^{-7}\\ \\mathrm{m}\\ (592\\ \\mathrm{nm})$$\n\n**(c)(i)** Energy needed from the ground state:\n- to $n = 2$: $9.80 - 5.20 = 4.60$ eV\n- to $n = 3$: $9.80 - 3.10 = 6.70$ eV\n- to $n = 4$: $9.80 - 1.40 = 8.40$ eV\n- to ionise the atom: 9.80 eV\n\nThe electron has only 6.0 eV, so the only possible excitation is to $n = 2$. After this the free electron has $6.0 - 4.60 = 1.4$ eV. If no excitation happens, the collision is elastic and the electron keeps 6.0 eV (the atom is so much more massive that it gains negligible kinetic energy). The possible kinetic energies are 6.0 eV and 1.4 eV.\n\n**(c)(ii)** $E_\\mathrm{k} = 6.0\\times1.6\\times10^{-19} = 9.6\\times10^{-19}\\ \\mathrm{J}$\n\n$$p = \\sqrt{2mE_\\mathrm{k}} = \\sqrt{2\\times9.11\\times10^{-31}\\times9.6\\times10^{-19}} = 1.32\\times10^{-24}\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$$\n\n$$\\lambda = \\frac{h}{p} = \\frac{6.63\\times10^{-34}}{1.32\\times10^{-24}} = 5.0\\times10^{-10}\\ \\mathrm{m}$$\n\n**(d)** The rings are a diffraction pattern. Diffraction is a property of waves, so the observation shows that electrons have a wave nature, with de Broglie wavelength $\\lambda = h/p$. Diffraction is significant only when the wavelength is comparable to the size of the gap, which here is the spacing between atoms, about $1.4\\times10^{-10}$ m. Electrons of 6.0 eV have a wavelength of $5.0\\times10^{-10}$ m, which is too large compared with this spacing. Increasing the kinetic energy increases the momentum $p$, so the de Broglie wavelength $\\lambda = h/p$ falls to a value comparable to the atomic spacing."},
{"id": "ial-phy2-010", "type": "written", "spec": "phy2:3.7", "specs": ["phy2:3.3", "phy2:3.8", "phy2:3.6", "phy2:3.2"], "difficulty": 3, "marks": 14, "stem": "Two loudspeakers, $S_1$ and $S_2$, are placed side by side 1.5 m apart, facing in the same direction, in a large open space. Both loudspeakers are connected to the same signal generator, and each emits sound of frequency 1.70 kHz. The speed of sound in air is $340\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$.\n\n**(a)** Sound is a longitudinal wave. Describe the motion of the air molecules as the sound wave passes, and explain how this produces variations in air pressure. **(2)**\n\n**(b)** Explain why the two loudspeakers act as coherent sources. **(2)**\n\n**(c)** A student walks slowly along a line parallel to the line joining the loudspeakers, a few metres in front of them. She hears the sound become alternately louder and quieter. Explain why. **(3)**\n\n**(d)** Point P is 3.40 m from $S_1$ and 3.70 m from $S_2$. Determine whether the student hears a loud sound or a quiet sound at P. **(3)**\n\n**(e)** In a separate experiment, the student uses one loudspeaker, the signal generator, a microphone and a two-beam oscilloscope to determine the speed of sound in air. Describe how she should carry out the experiment. **(4)**", "markScheme": "**(a)**\n- **B1** Air molecules oscillate (about their mean positions) parallel to the direction in which the wave transfers energy\n- **B1** Where the molecules are closer together there is a compression (higher pressure); where they are further apart there is a rarefaction (lower pressure)\n\n**(b)**\n- **B1** Both loudspeakers are driven by the same signal, so they emit sound of the same frequency (wavelength)\n- **B1** There is a constant phase difference between the two sources (they are in phase)\n\n**(c)**\n- **B1** The waves from $S_1$ and $S_2$ overlap and superpose (interfere)\n- **B1** Loud: path difference $= n\\lambda$, waves arrive in phase, constructive interference, large resultant amplitude\n- **B1** Quiet: path difference $= (n + \\tfrac{1}{2})\\lambda$, waves arrive in antiphase, destructive interference, small resultant amplitude\n\n**(d)**\n- **M1** $\\lambda = v/f = 340/1700 = 0.200\\ \\mathrm{m}$\n- **M1** Path difference $= 3.70 - 3.40 = 0.30\\ \\mathrm{m} = 1.5\\lambda$ (or phase difference $= 2\\pi \\times 0.30/0.200 = 3\\pi$ rad)\n- **E1** Path difference is $(n + \\tfrac{1}{2})\\lambda$, so the waves arrive in antiphase, interfere destructively and a quiet sound is heard at P\n\n**(e)**\n- **B1** Connect the signal generator to the loudspeaker and to one input of the oscilloscope; connect the microphone to the other input\n- **B1** Move the microphone directly away from the loudspeaker (along a metre rule) from a position where the two traces are in phase; each time the traces come back into phase the microphone has moved one wavelength\n- **B1** Measure the distance moved for several (e.g. 10) wavelengths and divide by the number of wavelengths to find $\\lambda$\n- **B1** Read $f$ from the signal generator (or measure the period $T$ using the time-base and use $f = 1/T$) and calculate $v = f\\lambda$", "solution": "**(a)** The air molecules oscillate backwards and forwards about their mean positions, parallel to the direction in which the wave transfers energy. Where the molecules are pushed closer together there is a compression, a region of higher pressure. Where they are spread further apart there is a rarefaction, a region of lower pressure. So the pressure varies above and below atmospheric pressure along the wave.\n\n**(b)** Both loudspeakers are driven by the same signal generator, so they emit sound of exactly the same frequency (and wavelength), with a constant phase difference between them (here they are in phase). Sources with the same frequency and a constant phase difference are coherent, which is needed for a stable interference pattern.\n\n**(c)** The sound waves from $S_1$ and $S_2$ overlap and superpose. Where the path difference is a whole number of wavelengths, $n\\lambda$, the waves arrive in phase and interfere constructively. The resultant amplitude is large, so the sound is loud. Where the path difference is $(n + \\tfrac12)\\lambda$, the waves arrive in antiphase and interfere destructively. The resultant amplitude is small, so the sound is quiet. As the student walks along the line the path difference changes continuously, so she passes through alternate loud and quiet regions.\n\n**(d)** $\\lambda = \\dfrac{v}{f} = \\dfrac{340}{1700} = 0.200\\ \\mathrm{m}$\n\nPath difference $= 3.70 - 3.40 = 0.30\\ \\mathrm{m} = \\dfrac{0.30}{0.200}\\lambda = 1.5\\lambda$\n\nPhase difference $= 2\\pi\\times1.5 = 3\\pi$ rad, which is equivalent to $\\pi$ rad, so the waves arrive in antiphase. They interfere destructively, so a quiet sound is heard at P.\n\n**(e)**\n- Connect the signal generator to the loudspeaker and also to one input (Y1) of the oscilloscope. Connect the microphone to the other input (Y2).\n- Place the microphone in front of the loudspeaker beside a metre rule along the line of the sound, and move it until the two traces are in phase (peaks lined up). Record its position.\n- Move the microphone directly away from the loudspeaker, counting each time the traces come back into phase; each time corresponds to a further distance of one wavelength. Measure the distance moved for several (e.g. 10) wavelengths and divide by the number of wavelengths to find $\\lambda$. This reduces the percentage uncertainty.\n- Read the frequency $f$ from the signal generator, or measure the period $T$ from the oscilloscope time-base and use $f = 1/T$. Calculate $v = f\\lambda$. Repeat at several frequencies and find a mean."},
{"id": "ial-phy2-011", "type": "mcq", "spec": "phy2:3.1", "specs": ["phy2:3.2"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "A radar system emits electromagnetic waves of wavelength 2.5 cm in air. What is the period of these waves? **(1)**", "options": ["$8.3 \\times 10^{-11}$ s", "$8.3 \\times 10^{-9}$ s", "$7.5 \\times 10^{6}$ s", "$1.2 \\times 10^{10}$ s"], "answer": "A", "solution": "**A is correct.** $f = \\dfrac{c}{\\lambda} = \\dfrac{3.00\\times10^{8}}{0.025} = 1.2\\times10^{10}\\ \\mathrm{Hz}$, so $T = \\dfrac{1}{f} = 8.3\\times10^{-11}\\ \\mathrm{s}$.\n\n- B is wrong: it uses the wavelength in cm (2.5) instead of metres (0.025).\n- C is wrong: it multiplies $c$ by $\\lambda$ instead of dividing.\n- D is wrong: this is the frequency in Hz, not the period."},
{"id": "ial-phy2-012", "type": "mcq", "spec": "phy2:3.24", "specs": ["phy2:3.4", "phy2:3.3"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "Which of the following correctly identifies a wave that can be plane polarised and gives the correct reason? **(1)**", "options": ["Sound, because the air molecules oscillate perpendicular to the direction of energy transfer", "Ultrasound, because the oscillations are parallel to the direction of energy transfer", "Microwaves, because the oscillations are perpendicular to the direction of energy transfer", "Visible light, because the oscillations are parallel to the direction of energy transfer"], "answer": "C", "solution": "**C is correct.** Only transverse waves can be plane polarised: their oscillations are perpendicular to the direction of energy transfer, so the oscillations can be restricted to a single plane. Microwaves are electromagnetic waves, which are transverse.\n\n- A is wrong: sound is longitudinal; the air molecules oscillate parallel to the direction of energy transfer, so sound cannot be polarised.\n- B is wrong: ultrasound is longitudinal, and oscillations parallel to the direction of energy transfer cannot be restricted to one plane, so it cannot be polarised.\n- D is wrong: light can be polarised, but the reason given is wrong; light is transverse, with oscillations perpendicular to the direction of energy transfer."},
{"id": "ial-phy2-013", "type": "mcq", "spec": "phy2:3.30", "specs": ["phy2:3.29"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "An ultrasound probe sends a short pulse into the body. At the front surface of an organ, part of the pulse is reflected and part is transmitted. The transmitted part is then partly reflected at the back surface of the organ. The two echoes are detected 52 μs and 91 μs after the pulse is emitted. The speed of ultrasound in the organ is $1540\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$. What is the thickness of the organ? **(1)**", "options": ["1.5 cm", "3.0 cm", "6.0 cm", "7.0 cm"], "answer": "B", "solution": "**B is correct.** Because the pulse is partly transmitted at the front surface, some of it reaches the back surface and gives a second echo. The extra time for the second echo, $91 - 52 = 39\\ \\mu\\mathrm{s}$, is the time to cross the organ and come back. So\n\n$$\\text{thickness} = \\frac{vt}{2} = \\frac{1540\\times39\\times10^{-6}}{2} = 0.030\\ \\mathrm{m} = 3.0\\ \\mathrm{cm}$$\n\n- A is wrong: it divides by 2 twice.\n- C is wrong: it does not divide by 2 for the there-and-back journey.\n- D is wrong: it uses the full 91 μs, which gives the depth of the back surface below the probe, not the thickness of the organ."},
{"id": "ial-phy2-014", "type": "mcq", "spec": "phy2:3.8", "specs": ["phy2:3.5", "phy2:3.1"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A displacement–distance graph for a transverse progressive wave at one instant shows exactly three complete cycles of the wave over a distance of 2.40 m. Points X and Y on the wave are 0.30 m apart in the direction of travel of the wave. What is the phase difference between the oscillations at X and at Y? **(1)**", "options": ["$\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ rad", "$\\dfrac{3\\pi}{8}$ rad", "$\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}$ rad", "$\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$ rad"], "answer": "C", "solution": "**C is correct.** Three complete cycles in 2.40 m gives $\\lambda = 2.40/3 = 0.80$ m. Then\n\n$$\\phi = \\frac{2\\pi \\times \\text{path difference}}{\\lambda} = \\frac{2\\pi\\times0.30}{0.80} = \\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\ \\text{rad}$$\n\n- A is wrong: it uses 2.40 m as the wavelength.\n- B is wrong: it leaves out the factor of 2 ($\\pi\\times0.30/0.80$).\n- D is wrong: it uses $\\lambda = 0.40$ m, counting six cycles instead of three."},
{"id": "ial-phy2-015", "type": "mcq", "spec": "phy2:4.8", "specs": ["phy2:4.17", "phy2:4.3"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "The current $I$ in a filament lamp is measured for a range of positive values of the potential difference $V$ across it, and a graph of $I$ (vertical axis) against $V$ (horizontal axis) is plotted. Which statement correctly describes and explains the shape of the graph? **(1)**", "options": ["The graph is a straight line through the origin, because the filament obeys Ohm's law", "The gradient decreases as $V$ increases, because the filament gets hotter and the lattice ions vibrate with greater amplitude, so the resistance increases", "The gradient increases as $V$ increases, because the filament gets hotter and more conduction electrons are released, so the resistance decreases", "The current is almost zero until $V$ reaches about 0.6 V and then increases rapidly, because the resistance of the filament falls sharply"], "answer": "B", "solution": "**B is correct.** As the current increases the metal filament gets hotter. The lattice ions vibrate with greater amplitude, so conduction electrons collide with them more often and the resistance $V/I$ increases. The $I$–$V$ graph is therefore a curve whose gradient decreases as $V$ increases.\n\n- A is wrong: Ohm's law applies only at constant temperature, and the filament's temperature changes a lot, so the lamp is non-ohmic.\n- C is wrong: this describes an NTC thermistor; in a metal the number of conduction electrons stays essentially constant as the temperature changes.\n- D is wrong: this describes a diode, not a filament lamp."},
{"id": "ial-phy2-016", "type": "mcq", "spec": "phy2:4.12", "specs": ["phy2:4.13", "phy2:4.5"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A uniform resistance wire XY has length 1.00 m and resistance 4.0 Ω. It is connected in series with a 2.0 Ω resistor and a 9.0 V supply of negligible internal resistance. End Y of the wire is connected to the negative terminal of the supply, which is at 0 V, and the 2.0 Ω resistor is connected between the positive terminal and end X. Point P is on the wire, 0.25 m from Y. What is the potential at P? **(1)**", "options": ["1.5 V", "2.3 V", "4.5 V", "6.8 V"], "answer": "A", "solution": "**A is correct.** $I = \\dfrac{9.0}{4.0 + 2.0} = 1.5\\ \\mathrm{A}$, so the p.d. across the wire is $1.5 \\times 4.0 = 6.0\\ \\mathrm{V}$. X is at 6.0 V and Y is at 0 V, and the potential falls uniformly along a uniform wire. P is a quarter of the way along from Y, so its potential is $6.0 \\times 0.25 = 1.5\\ \\mathrm{V}$.\n\n- B is wrong: it assumes the whole 9.0 V is across the wire ($9.0 \\times 0.25$).\n- C is wrong: this is the p.d. between X and P ($6.0 \\times 0.75$), not the potential at P.\n- D is wrong: it uses 9.0 V across the wire and measures from the wrong end ($9.0 \\times 0.75$)."},
{"id": "ial-phy2-017", "type": "mcq", "spec": "phy2:4.4", "specs": ["phy2:4.1", "phy2:4.2", "phy2:4.7"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "A current of 0.25 A flows into a junction in a circuit and divides between two branches. The current in branch 1 is 0.10 A and the potential difference across branch 2 is 6.0 V. How much energy is transferred in branch 2 in 2.0 minutes? **(1)**", "options": ["1.8 J", "72 J", "108 J", "180 J"], "answer": "C", "solution": "**C is correct.** Charge is conserved at the junction, so the current in branch 2 is $0.25 - 0.10 = 0.15\\ \\mathrm{A}$. The charge flowing in 2.0 minutes is $Q = It = 0.15 \\times 120 = 18\\ \\mathrm{C}$, and the energy transferred is $W = QV = 18 \\times 6.0 = 108\\ \\mathrm{J}$.\n\n- A is wrong: it uses $t = 2.0$ s instead of 120 s.\n- B is wrong: it uses the branch 1 current (0.10 A).\n- D is wrong: it uses the total current (0.25 A) instead of the current in branch 2."},
{"id": "ial-phy2-018", "type": "mcq", "spec": "phy2:4.18", "specs": ["phy2:4.14", "phy2:4.13"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "A light-dependent resistor (LDR) is connected in series with a fixed resistor across a battery of negligible internal resistance. The intensity of the light falling on the LDR increases. Which statement correctly describes the change in the resistance of the LDR, the reason for it, and the effect on the potential difference across the fixed resistor? **(1)**", "options": ["The resistance increases because the lattice ions vibrate with greater amplitude, so the p.d. across the fixed resistor decreases", "The resistance decreases because the lattice ions vibrate with smaller amplitude, so the p.d. across the fixed resistor increases", "The resistance decreases because more conduction electrons are released, so the p.d. across the fixed resistor decreases", "The resistance decreases because more conduction electrons are released, so the p.d. across the fixed resistor increases"], "answer": "D", "solution": "**D is correct.** Photons absorbed in the LDR give electrons enough energy to become conduction electrons, so the number of charge carriers per unit volume increases and the resistance of the LDR decreases. The total resistance falls, so the current rises. The LDR now takes a smaller share of the supply p.d., so the p.d. across the fixed resistor ($IR$) increases.\n\n- A is wrong: the LDR's resistance decreases with increasing light intensity, and lattice vibration is not the mechanism.\n- B is wrong: the change in resistance is caused by the number of conduction electrons, not by a change in lattice vibrations.\n- C is wrong: the reason is right, but a smaller LDR resistance means a larger share of the p.d. across the fixed resistor, not a smaller one."},
{"id": "ial-phy4-001", "type": "written", "spec": "phy4:5.1", "specs": ["phy4:5.6", "phy4:5.5"], "difficulty": 1, "marks": 10, "stem": "A hockey puck of mass $0.17\\ \\mathrm{kg}$ is at rest on horizontal ice. A player strikes the puck with a hockey stick. During the contact, the force exerted on the puck by the stick increases uniformly from zero to a maximum of $680\\ \\mathrm{N}$ in $3.0\\ \\mathrm{ms}$ and then decreases uniformly to zero. The total contact time is $8.0\\ \\mathrm{ms}$.\n\n**(a)** State what is represented by the area under a force–time graph. **(1)**\n\n**(b)** Show that the speed of the puck immediately after it leaves the stick is about $16\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$. **(3)**\n\n**(c)** Calculate the kinetic energy of the puck immediately after it leaves the stick, using the equation $E_k = \\dfrac{p^2}{2m}$. **(2)**\n\n**(d)** Later, the puck slides directly towards a vertical board at the edge of the rink and strikes it at right angles with a speed of $15.2\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$. The puck rebounds along its original path with a speed of $11.0\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$ and is in contact with the board for $0.020\\ \\mathrm{s}$. Calculate the average force exerted on the puck by the board. **(3)**\n\n**(e)** Explain why the collision between the puck and the board is not elastic. **(1)**", "markScheme": "**(a)**\n- **B1** Impulse or change in momentum (of the puck)\n\n**(b)**\n- **M1** Impulse = area under graph $= \\frac{1}{2} \\times 680 \\times 8.0 \\times 10^{-3}$ $(= 2.72\\ \\mathrm{N\\,s})$\n- **M1** Uses impulse $= \\Delta p = mv$ (puck initially at rest): $v = \\dfrac{2.72}{0.17}$\n- **A1*** $v = 16.0\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$ (given answer: working must be seen and the value given to at least 3 s.f.)\n\n**(c)**\n- **M1** $E_k = \\dfrac{2.72^2}{2 \\times 0.17}$ (ft their momentum; accept $p = 0.17 \\times 16$)\n- **A1** $E_k = 22\\ \\mathrm{J}$ (accept 21.8 J)\n\n**(d)**\n- **M1** $\\Delta p = 0.17 \\times (15.2 + 11.0)$ $(= 4.45\\ \\mathrm{N\\,s})$, i.e. allows for the reversal of direction\n- **M1** $F = \\dfrac{\\Delta p}{\\Delta t}$ with $\\Delta t = 0.020\\ \\mathrm{s}$ (use of $0.17 \\times (15.2 - 11.0)$, giving $36\\ \\mathrm{N}$, scores this mark only)\n- **A1** $F = 220\\ \\mathrm{N}$ (accept 223 N)\n\n**(e)**\n- **B1** The kinetic energy of the puck decreases (kinetic energy is not conserved), since the rebound speed is less than the impact speed and the mass is unchanged", "solution": "**(a)** The area under a force–time graph represents the impulse, which is equal to the change in momentum.\n\n**(b)** The graph is a triangle of base $8.0\\ \\mathrm{ms}$ and height $680\\ \\mathrm{N}$:\n$$\\text{impulse} = \\tfrac{1}{2} \\times 680 \\times 8.0 \\times 10^{-3} = 2.72\\ \\mathrm{N\\,s}$$\nThe puck starts at rest, so impulse $= \\Delta p = mv$:\n$$v = \\frac{2.72}{0.17} = 16.0\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}} \\approx 16\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$$\n\n**(c)**\n$$E_k = \\frac{p^2}{2m} = \\frac{2.72^2}{2 \\times 0.17} = 21.8\\ \\mathrm{J} \\approx 22\\ \\mathrm{J}$$\n\n**(d)** Taking the direction towards the board as positive, $u = +15.2\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$ and $v = -11.0\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$:\n$$\\Delta p = m(v - u) = 0.17 \\times (-11.0 - 15.2) = -4.45\\ \\mathrm{N\\,s}$$\n$$F = \\frac{\\Delta p}{\\Delta t} = \\frac{4.45}{0.020} = 223\\ \\mathrm{N} \\approx 220\\ \\mathrm{N}$$\nThe average force on the puck is about $220\\ \\mathrm{N}$, directed away from the board.\n\n**(e)** Kinetic energy before $= \\tfrac{1}{2} \\times 0.17 \\times 15.2^2 = 19.6\\ \\mathrm{J}$; kinetic energy after $= \\tfrac{1}{2} \\times 0.17 \\times 11.0^2 = 10.3\\ \\mathrm{J}$. The speed decreases while the mass is unchanged, so the kinetic energy decreases. Kinetic energy is not conserved, so the collision is not elastic (it is inelastic)."},
{"id": "ial-phy4-002", "type": "written", "spec": "phy4:5.2", "specs": ["phy4:5.5"], "difficulty": 2, "marks": 11, "stem": "A student investigates the conservation of momentum using two gliders, A and B, on a horizontal air track. Each glider carries a card of length $10.0\\ \\mathrm{cm}$. Glider A, of mass $0.250\\ \\mathrm{kg}$, is pushed towards glider B, of mass $0.200\\ \\mathrm{kg}$, which is at rest. Before the collision, the card on A passes through light gate 1. The gliders stick together on impact, and the card on B then passes through light gate 2. Each light gate is connected to a data logger that records the time for which its light beam is blocked.\n\n| Light gate | Card | Time beam blocked / s |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 (before collision) | on glider A | 0.125 |\n| 2 (after collision) | on glider B | 0.230 |\n\n**(a)** Explain why an air track is used in this investigation. **(2)**\n\n**(b)** **(i)** Calculate the total momentum of the gliders before the collision. **(2)**\n\n**(ii)** Calculate the total momentum of the gliders after the collision. **(2)**\n\n**(iii)** Comment on whether these results support the principle of conservation of momentum. **(2)**\n\n**(c)** Show that the collision is inelastic. **(3)**", "markScheme": "**(a)**\n- **B1** The cushion of air reduces friction (between the gliders and the track)\n- **B1** So there is no (resultant) external force on the gliders, hence total momentum is conserved or the speeds stay constant between the light gates and the collision\n\n**(b)(i)**\n- **M1** $u = \\dfrac{0.100}{0.125} = 0.800\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$\n- **A1** $p = 0.250 \\times 0.800 = 0.200\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$ (accept N s)\n\n**(b)(ii)**\n- **M1** $v = \\dfrac{0.100}{0.230} = 0.435\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$\n- **A1** $p = (0.250 + 0.200) \\times 0.435 = 0.196\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$ (accept 0.195 to 0.196; accept N s)\n\n**(b)(iii)**\n- **M1** Calculates the percentage difference: $\\dfrac{0.200 - 0.196}{0.200} \\times 100\\% \\approx 2\\%$ (accept 2.2%; ft their values)\n- **E1** The difference is small (within experimental uncertainty, or the small loss is due to friction or air resistance), so the results support conservation of momentum\n\n**(c)**\n- **M1** Kinetic energy before $= \\frac{1}{2} \\times 0.250 \\times 0.800^2 = 0.080\\ \\mathrm{J}$\n- **M1** Kinetic energy after $= \\frac{1}{2} \\times 0.450 \\times 0.435^2 = 0.043\\ \\mathrm{J}$ (accept use of $E_k = p^2/2m$; ft their (b) values)\n- **E1** The total kinetic energy after the collision is less than before (kinetic energy is not conserved), so the collision is inelastic", "solution": "**(a)** The air cushion reduces the friction between the gliders and the track almost to zero, so no resultant external force acts on the gliders. Their speeds stay constant between the light gates and the collision, and the total momentum of the system is conserved.\n\n**(b)(i)** $u_A = \\dfrac{0.100}{0.125} = 0.800\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$, and B is at rest, so\n$$p_{\\text{before}} = 0.250 \\times 0.800 = 0.200\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$$\n\n**(b)(ii)** $v = \\dfrac{0.100}{0.230} = 0.435\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$ for the combined gliders, so\n$$p_{\\text{after}} = 0.450 \\times 0.435 = 0.196\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$$\n\n**(b)(iii)** Percentage difference $= \\dfrac{0.200 - 0.196}{0.200} \\times 100\\% \\approx 2\\%$. This difference is small and can be explained by experimental uncertainty or a little residual friction and air resistance, so the results support the principle of conservation of momentum.\n\n**(c)**\n$$E_{k,\\text{before}} = \\tfrac{1}{2} \\times 0.250 \\times 0.800^2 = 0.080\\ \\mathrm{J}$$\n$$E_{k,\\text{after}} = \\tfrac{1}{2} \\times 0.450 \\times 0.435^2 = 0.043\\ \\mathrm{J}$$\nThe total kinetic energy falls by almost half. Kinetic energy is not conserved, so the collision is inelastic."},
{"id": "ial-phy4-003", "type": "written", "spec": "phy4:5.3", "specs": ["phy4:5.4", "phy4:5.5"], "difficulty": 3, "marks": 13, "stem": "A student uses video analysis to investigate a collision between two small steel spheres on a smooth horizontal table. Sphere X, of mass $0.050\\ \\mathrm{kg}$, moves towards sphere Y, of mass $0.030\\ \\mathrm{kg}$, which is at rest. The collision is not head-on, so after the collision the spheres move off in different directions. A video camera mounted vertically above the table records the motion at 50 frames per second, and a metre rule lies on the table in the field of view. The spheres may be treated as particles.\n\n**(a)** Explain why the camera is mounted vertically above the table and why the metre rule is placed on the table in the field of view. **(2)**\n\n**(b)** Before the collision, the centre of X moves a distance of $12.0\\ \\mathrm{cm}$ in 10 frames of the video. Show that the speed of X before the collision is about $0.6\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$. **(2)**\n\n**(c)** After the collision, X moves with a speed of $0.40\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$ in a direction at $30^\\circ$ to its original direction of motion. Determine the magnitude and direction of the velocity of Y after the collision. **(6)**\n\n**(d)** Deduce whether the collision is elastic. **(3)**", "markScheme": "**(a)**\n- **B1** The metre rule provides a scale, so that distances on the video image can be converted into real distances\n- **B1** The camera is vertically above (perpendicular to the table) so the image is not distorted by perspective, so the same scale applies in all directions on the table or to avoid parallax error\n\n**(b)**\n- **M1** Time for 10 frames $t = \\dfrac{10}{50} = 0.20\\ \\mathrm{s}$\n- **A1*** $v = \\dfrac{0.120}{0.20} = 0.60\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$\n\n**(c)**\n- **M1** Momentum before $= 0.050 \\times 0.60 = 0.030\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$ and resolves momentum of X after: $0.020\\cos 30^\\circ = 0.0173$ and $0.020\\sin 30^\\circ = 0.0100\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$\n- **M1** Conservation of momentum parallel to the original direction: momentum of Y parallel $= 0.030 - 0.0173 = 0.0127\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$\n- **M1** Conservation of momentum perpendicular to the original direction: momentum of Y perpendicular $= 0.0100\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$ (opposite in sense to that of X)\n- **M1** Combines components: $p_Y = \\sqrt{0.0127^2 + 0.0100^2} = 0.0161\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$ or $\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{0.0100}{0.0127}$ (velocity components $0.423$ and $0.333\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$ also acceptable)\n- **A1** $v_Y = \\dfrac{0.0161}{0.030} = 0.54\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$\n- **A1** Direction at $38^\\circ$ to the original direction of X, on the opposite side of that line to X (accept awrt 38°)\n\n**(d)**\n- **M1** Kinetic energy before $= \\frac{1}{2} \\times 0.050 \\times 0.60^2 = 9.0 \\times 10^{-3}\\ \\mathrm{J}$\n- **M1** Kinetic energy after $= \\frac{1}{2} \\times 0.050 \\times 0.40^2 + \\frac{1}{2} \\times 0.030 \\times 0.54^2 = 8.4 \\times 10^{-3}\\ \\mathrm{J}$ (accept $8.3 \\times 10^{-3}$ J; ft their $v_Y$)\n- **E1** Total kinetic energy decreases (is not conserved), so the collision is inelastic (conclusion must be consistent with their values)", "solution": "**(a)** The metre rule provides a scale, so that distances measured on the video image can be converted into real distances. The camera is vertically above the table (its axis perpendicular to the table) so that the image is not distorted by perspective. The same scale then applies to motion in every direction on the table, and parallax errors are avoided.\n\n**(b)** Time for 10 frames: $t = \\dfrac{10}{50} = 0.20\\ \\mathrm{s}$. Speed $v = \\dfrac{0.120}{0.20} = 0.60\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$.\n\n**(c)** Momentum before (along the original direction of X): $0.050 \\times 0.60 = 0.030\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$; the perpendicular momentum before is zero.\n\nMomentum of X after: $0.050 \\times 0.40 = 0.020\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$, with components\n- parallel: $0.020\\cos 30^\\circ = 0.0173\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$\n- perpendicular: $0.020\\sin 30^\\circ = 0.0100\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$\n\nConservation of momentum in each direction gives, for Y:\n- parallel: $p_{Y\\parallel} = 0.030 - 0.0173 = 0.0127\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$\n- perpendicular: $p_{Y\\perp} = 0.0100\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$, on the opposite side of the original line from X\n\n$$p_Y = \\sqrt{0.0127^2 + 0.0100^2} = 0.0161\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad v_Y = \\frac{0.0161}{0.030} = 0.54\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$$\n$$\\tan\\theta = \\frac{0.0100}{0.0127} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\theta = 38^\\circ$$\nY moves at $0.54\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$ at $38^\\circ$ to the original direction of X, on the opposite side of that line to X.\n\n**(d)**\n$$E_{k,\\text{before}} = \\tfrac{1}{2} \\times 0.050 \\times 0.60^2 = 9.0 \\times 10^{-3}\\ \\mathrm{J}$$\n$$E_{k,\\text{after}} = \\tfrac{1}{2} \\times 0.050 \\times 0.40^2 + \\tfrac{1}{2} \\times 0.030 \\times 0.54^2 = 4.0 \\times 10^{-3} + 4.4 \\times 10^{-3} = 8.4 \\times 10^{-3}\\ \\mathrm{J}$$\nThe total kinetic energy decreases, so kinetic energy is not conserved and the collision is inelastic."},
{"id": "ial-phy4-004", "type": "written", "spec": "phy4:5.11", "specs": ["phy4:5.7", "phy4:5.8", "phy4:5.9", "phy4:5.10"], "difficulty": 2, "marks": 13, "stem": "A fairground ride consists of a large vertical cylinder of internal radius $2.5\\ \\mathrm{m}$ that rotates about its vertical axis. Riders stand with their backs against the inside wall. When the cylinder is rotating at a constant rate, the floor is lowered and the riders stay in place against the wall without sliding down.\n\n**(a)** At its operating speed, the cylinder turns through an angle of $135^\\circ$ in $0.75\\ \\mathrm{s}$. **(i)** Convert $135^\\circ$ into radians. **(1)**\n\n**(ii)** Show that the angular velocity of the cylinder is about $3.1\\ \\mathrm{rad\\,s^{-1}}$. **(2)**\n\n**(iii)** Calculate the speed of a rider. **(2)**\n\n**(b)** **(i)** Explain why a resultant force is needed to keep a rider moving in a circle, even though the rider's speed is constant. **(2)**\n\n**(ii)** Identify the force that provides the centripetal force on a rider. **(1)**\n\n**(iii)** A rider has mass $60\\ \\mathrm{kg}$. Calculate the magnitude of the centripetal force on this rider. **(3)**\n\n**(c)** Explain why the rider does not slide down the wall after the floor is lowered. **(2)**", "markScheme": "**(a)(i)**\n- **B1** $135^\\circ \\times \\dfrac{2\\pi}{360^\\circ} = 2.36\\ \\mathrm{rad}$ (accept $\\frac{3\\pi}{4}$ rad)\n\n**(a)(ii)**\n- **M1** $\\omega = \\dfrac{\\Delta\\theta}{\\Delta t} = \\dfrac{2.36}{0.75}$\n- **A1*** $\\omega = 3.14\\ \\mathrm{rad\\,s^{-1}}$ (given answer: must see 3.14 or a more precise value, or $\\pi$ rad s⁻¹)\n\n**(a)(iii)**\n- **M1** $v = \\omega r = 3.14 \\times 2.5$\n- **A1** $v = 7.9\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$ (accept 7.75 to 7.9, e.g. 7.75 from $\\omega = 3.1$)\n\n**(b)(i)**\n- **B1** The direction of motion changes continuously, so the velocity changes and the rider is accelerating (even though the speed is constant)\n- **B1** By Newton's laws a resultant force is needed to produce this acceleration, and it acts towards the centre of the circle\n\n**(b)(ii)**\n- **B1** The normal contact (reaction) force exerted by the wall on the rider\n\n**(b)(iii)**\n- **M1** $a = r\\omega^2 = 2.5 \\times 3.14^2$ $(= 24.7\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-2}})$ or $a = \\dfrac{v^2}{r}$ with their $v$\n- **M1** $F = ma = 60 \\times 24.7$\n- **A1** $F = 1480\\ \\mathrm{N}$ (accept 1440 to 1480 N, or $1.5 \\times 10^3$ N)\n\n**(c)**\n- **B1** A frictional force from the wall acts vertically upwards on the rider\n- **B1** The frictional force is equal in magnitude to the rider's weight, so the resultant vertical force is zero (no vertical acceleration)", "solution": "**(a)(i)** $135^\\circ \\times \\dfrac{2\\pi}{360^\\circ} = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} = 2.36\\ \\mathrm{rad}$\n\n**(a)(ii)** $\\omega = \\dfrac{\\Delta\\theta}{\\Delta t} = \\dfrac{2.36}{0.75} = 3.14\\ \\mathrm{rad\\,s^{-1}} \\approx 3.1\\ \\mathrm{rad\\,s^{-1}}$\n\n**(a)(iii)** $v = \\omega r = 3.14 \\times 2.5 = 7.9\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$\n\n**(b)(i)** Velocity is a vector. Although the rider's speed is constant, the direction of motion changes continuously, so the velocity changes and the rider is accelerating. By Newton's second law an acceleration needs a resultant force. For circular motion this resultant (centripetal) force acts towards the centre of the circle.\n\n**(b)(ii)** The normal contact (reaction) force exerted on the rider by the wall of the cylinder. It acts horizontally, towards the axis.\n\n**(b)(iii)**\n$$a = r\\omega^2 = 2.5 \\times 3.14^2 = 24.7\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-2}}$$\n$$F = ma = 60 \\times 24.7 = 1480\\ \\mathrm{N}$$\n\n**(c)** The wall exerts an upward frictional force on the rider. The rider stays at the same height, so there is no vertical acceleration. The frictional force therefore equals the rider's weight ($60 \\times 9.81 = 589\\ \\mathrm{N}$) in magnitude, and the resultant vertical force is zero. The large normal contact force from (b)(iii) is what allows such a large frictional force."},
{"id": "ial-phy4-005", "type": "written", "spec": "phy4:6.4", "specs": ["phy4:6.3", "phy4:6.7", "phy4:6.5", "phy4:6.8"], "difficulty": 3, "marks": 14, "stem": "Two small charged spheres, A and B, are fixed $0.30\\ \\mathrm{m}$ apart in air. A carries a charge of $+4.0\\ \\mathrm{nC}$ and B carries a charge of $+1.0\\ \\mathrm{nC}$. The spheres may be treated as point charges.\n\n**(a)** Calculate the magnitude of the electrostatic force between A and B. **(2)**\n\n**(b)** P is the point on the straight line between A and B at which the resultant electric field strength is zero. Determine the distance of P from A. **(3)**\n\n**(c)** Calculate the electric potential at P. **(3)**\n\n**(d)** Explain why the electric potential at P is not zero, even though the resultant electric field strength at P is zero. **(2)**\n\n**(e)** Equipotential surfaces are drawn around an isolated point charge at equal intervals of potential. State and explain how the separation of adjacent equipotential surfaces changes with distance from the charge. **(2)**\n\n**(f)** A small particle with a charge of $+0.50\\ \\mathrm{nC}$ is moved from a very large distance away to point P. Calculate the work that must be done on the particle. **(2)**", "markScheme": "**(a)**\n- **M1** $F = \\dfrac{Q_1 Q_2}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r^2} = \\dfrac{8.99 \\times 10^9 \\times 4.0 \\times 10^{-9} \\times 1.0 \\times 10^{-9}}{0.30^2}$\n- **A1** $F = 4.0 \\times 10^{-7}\\ \\mathrm{N}$\n\n**(b)**\n- **M1** Equates the field strengths: $\\dfrac{4.0 \\times 10^{-9}}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 x^2} = \\dfrac{1.0 \\times 10^{-9}}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 (0.30 - x)^2}$\n- **M1** Simplifies, e.g. $\\dfrac{x}{0.30 - x} = 2$ or $x = 2(0.30 - x)$\n- **A1** $x = 0.20\\ \\mathrm{m}$\n\n**(c)**\n- **M1** Uses $V = \\dfrac{Q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r}$ for one charge, e.g. $V_A = \\dfrac{8.99 \\times 10^9 \\times 4.0 \\times 10^{-9}}{0.20} = 180\\ \\mathrm{V}$\n- **M1** Adds the two potentials: $V = 180 + \\dfrac{8.99 \\times 10^9 \\times 1.0 \\times 10^{-9}}{0.10} = 180 + 90$ (ft their distance from (b))\n- **A1** $V = 270\\ \\mathrm{V}$\n\n**(d)**\n- **B1** Field strength is a vector: the fields due to A and B at P are equal in magnitude and opposite in direction, so they cancel\n- **B1** Potential is a scalar: the potentials due to A and B at P are both positive, so they add (zero field means zero potential gradient, not zero potential)\n\n**(e)**\n- **B1** The separation increases with distance from the charge\n- **B1** The field strength decreases with distance ($E \\propto 1/r^2$) and $E = -\\dfrac{\\Delta V}{\\Delta x}$, so a larger $\\Delta x$ is needed for the same $\\Delta V$\n\n**(f)**\n- **M1** $W = Q\\Delta V = 0.50 \\times 10^{-9} \\times 270$ (ft their (c))\n- **A1** $W = 1.35 \\times 10^{-7}\\ \\mathrm{J}$ (accept $1.3 \\times 10^{-7}$ J or $1.4 \\times 10^{-7}$ J)", "solution": "**(a)**\n$$F = \\frac{Q_1Q_2}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r^2} = \\frac{8.99 \\times 10^9 \\times 4.0 \\times 10^{-9} \\times 1.0 \\times 10^{-9}}{0.30^2} = 4.0 \\times 10^{-7}\\ \\mathrm{N}$$\n\n**(b)** Let P be a distance $x$ from A. Between the charges, the fields due to A and B point in opposite directions, so for a zero resultant:\n$$\\frac{4.0 \\times 10^{-9}}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 x^2} = \\frac{1.0 \\times 10^{-9}}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 (0.30 - x)^2} \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\frac{x^2}{(0.30 - x)^2} = 4 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\frac{x}{0.30 - x} = 2$$\nso $x = 0.60 - 2x$, giving $x = 0.20\\ \\mathrm{m}$.\n\n**(c)** P is $0.20\\ \\mathrm{m}$ from A and $0.10\\ \\mathrm{m}$ from B:\n$$V_A = \\frac{8.99 \\times 10^9 \\times 4.0 \\times 10^{-9}}{0.20} = 180\\ \\mathrm{V}, \\qquad V_B = \\frac{8.99 \\times 10^9 \\times 1.0 \\times 10^{-9}}{0.10} = 90\\ \\mathrm{V}$$\nPotential is a scalar, so $V = 180 + 90 = 270\\ \\mathrm{V}$.\n\n**(d)** Electric field strength is a vector. At P the fields due to A and B are equal in magnitude but opposite in direction, so they cancel. Potential is a scalar. The potentials due to the two positive charges are both positive and add, so they cannot cancel. A zero field at P means that the potential gradient there is zero, not that the potential is zero.\n\n**(e)** The separation increases with distance from the charge. The field strength decreases with distance ($E \\propto 1/r^2$), and since $E = -\\Delta V/\\Delta x$, a larger distance $\\Delta x$ is needed for the same change in potential $\\Delta V$ where the field is weaker.\n\n**(f)** The potential at a very large distance is zero, so\n$$W = Q\\Delta V = 0.50 \\times 10^{-9} \\times 270 = 1.35 \\times 10^{-7}\\ \\mathrm{J}$$"},
{"id": "ial-phy4-006", "type": "written", "spec": "phy4:6.6", "specs": ["phy4:7.3", "phy4:6.2", "phy4:6.5", "phy4:6.15"], "difficulty": 3, "marks": 14, "stem": "In an electron deflection tube, electrons are released from a heated filament and are then accelerated from rest through a potential difference of $2400\\ \\mathrm{V}$ in a vacuum. Gravitational effects on the electrons are negligible.\n\n**(a)** Explain how electrons are released from the filament. **(2)**\n\n**(b)** Show that the speed of the electrons after acceleration is about $2.9 \\times 10^{7}\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$. **(2)**\n\n**(c)** The electrons then enter the uniform electric field between two horizontal parallel plates. They enter travelling horizontally, midway between the plates. The plates are $6.0\\ \\mathrm{cm}$ long and $4.0\\ \\mathrm{cm}$ apart, and there is a potential difference of $300\\ \\mathrm{V}$ between them. **(i)** Calculate the electric field strength between the plates. **(2)**\n\n**(ii)** Determine the vertical displacement of an electron as it leaves the region between the plates. **(5)**\n\n**(d)** A uniform magnetic field is now applied in the region between the plates, perpendicular to both the electric field and the initial velocity of the electrons. The magnetic flux density is adjusted until the electrons pass between the plates without being deflected. Calculate this magnetic flux density. **(3)**", "markScheme": "**(a)**\n- **B1** A current in the filament heats it\n- **B1** (Some) electrons gain enough (kinetic) energy to escape from the surface of the metal or to overcome the attraction of the positive ions\n\n**(b)**\n- **M1** $eV = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2$: $1.60 \\times 10^{-19} \\times 2400 = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 9.11 \\times 10^{-31} \\times v^2$\n- **A1*** $v = 2.90 \\times 10^7\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$ (given answer: must be given to at least 3 s.f.)\n\n**(c)(i)**\n- **M1** $E = \\dfrac{V}{d} = \\dfrac{300}{0.040}$\n- **A1** $E = 7500\\ \\mathrm{V\\,m^{-1}}$ (accept N C⁻¹)\n\n**(c)(ii)**\n- **M1** $F = eE = 1.60 \\times 10^{-19} \\times 7500$ $(= 1.2 \\times 10^{-15}\\ \\mathrm{N})$\n- **M1** $a = \\dfrac{F}{m} = \\dfrac{1.2 \\times 10^{-15}}{9.11 \\times 10^{-31}}$ $(= 1.32 \\times 10^{15}\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-2}})$\n- **M1** Horizontal velocity constant, so time between plates $t = \\dfrac{0.060}{2.90 \\times 10^7}$ $(= 2.07 \\times 10^{-9}\\ \\mathrm{s})$\n- **M1** $s = \\frac{1}{2}at^2$ (initial vertical velocity zero)\n- **A1** $s = 2.8 \\times 10^{-3}\\ \\mathrm{m}$ (2.8 mm) (ft their E from (c)(i))\n\n**(d)**\n- **M1** Magnetic force equals electric force: $Bev = eE$\n- **M1** $B = \\dfrac{E}{v} = \\dfrac{7500}{2.90 \\times 10^7}$ (ft their E and v)\n- **A1** $B = 2.6 \\times 10^{-4}\\ \\mathrm{T}$", "solution": "**(a)** A current through the filament heats it. Some of the free (conduction) electrons gain enough kinetic energy to escape from the surface of the metal, overcoming the attraction of the positive ions. This is thermionic emission.\n\n**(b)** The work done on each electron by the accelerating p.d. becomes kinetic energy:\n$$eV = \\tfrac{1}{2}mv^2 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; v = \\sqrt{\\frac{2eV}{m}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{2 \\times 1.60 \\times 10^{-19} \\times 2400}{9.11 \\times 10^{-31}}} = 2.90 \\times 10^7\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$$\n\n**(c)(i)** $E = \\dfrac{V}{d} = \\dfrac{300}{0.040} = 7500\\ \\mathrm{V\\,m^{-1}}$\n\n**(c)(ii)** Vertical force and acceleration:\n$$F = eE = 1.60 \\times 10^{-19} \\times 7500 = 1.2 \\times 10^{-15}\\ \\mathrm{N}, \\qquad a = \\frac{F}{m} = \\frac{1.2 \\times 10^{-15}}{9.11 \\times 10^{-31}} = 1.32 \\times 10^{15}\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-2}}$$\nThe horizontal velocity is constant, so the time spent between the plates is\n$$t = \\frac{0.060}{2.90 \\times 10^7} = 2.07 \\times 10^{-9}\\ \\mathrm{s}$$\nThe initial vertical velocity is zero, so\n$$s = \\tfrac{1}{2}at^2 = \\tfrac{1}{2} \\times 1.32 \\times 10^{15} \\times (2.07 \\times 10^{-9})^2 = 2.8 \\times 10^{-3}\\ \\mathrm{m} = 2.8\\ \\mathrm{mm}$$\nThe displacement is towards the positive plate. Since $2.8\\ \\mathrm{mm} < 20\\ \\mathrm{mm}$, the electron leaves the plates without hitting one.\n\n**(d)** The electrons are undeflected when the magnetic force balances the electric force:\n$$Bev = eE \\;\\Rightarrow\\; B = \\frac{E}{v} = \\frac{7500}{2.90 \\times 10^7} = 2.6 \\times 10^{-4}\\ \\mathrm{T}$$"},
{"id": "ial-phy4-007", "type": "written", "spec": "phy4:6.13", "specs": ["phy4:6.9", "phy4:6.10", "phy4:6.11", "phy4:6.12"], "difficulty": 3, "marks": 14, "stem": "**(a)** Explain, with reference to a graph of potential difference $V$ against charge $Q$ for a capacitor, why the energy stored by a capacitor is given by $W = \\frac{1}{2}QV$. **(3)**\n\n**(b)** A student uses a data logger to investigate the discharge of a $470\\ \\mu\\mathrm{F}$ capacitor through a resistor of resistance $R$. The capacitor is first charged to a potential difference of $12.0\\ \\mathrm{V}$. As the capacitor discharges, the data logger records the potential difference $V$ across it at regular intervals of time $t$. The student plots a graph of $\\ln(V/\\mathrm{V})$ against $t$ and obtains a straight line with a gradient of $-0.0213\\ \\mathrm{s^{-1}}$. **(i)** State one advantage of using a data logger in this experiment. **(1)**\n\n**(ii)** Explain why the graph of $\\ln(V/\\mathrm{V})$ against $t$ is a straight line. **(2)**\n\n**(iii)** Determine $R$. **(3)**\n\n**(c)** Determine the time taken for the energy stored by the capacitor to fall to 25% of its initial value. **(3)**\n\n**(d)** The student replaces the resistor with one of resistance $2R$ and repeats the experiment from the same initial potential difference. Describe how the graph of $V$ against $t$ would differ from the original graph. **(2)**", "markScheme": "**(a)**\n- **B1** $V = Q/C$, so (for constant $C$) the graph of $V$ against $Q$ is a straight line through the origin\n- **B1** Work done in adding a small charge $\\Delta Q$ is $V\\Delta Q$, the area of a thin strip under the graph, so energy stored = work done = area under the graph\n- **B1** The total area is a triangle, so $W = \\frac{1}{2}QV$\n\n**(b)(i)**\n- **B1** It takes many readings at short, precise, regular time intervals or there is no reaction time error, as $V$ and $t$ are recorded simultaneously\n\n**(b)(ii)**\n- **B1** $V = V_0 e^{-t/RC}$, so $\\ln V = \\ln V_0 - \\dfrac{t}{RC}$\n- **B1** Compared with $y = mx + c$: $R$, $C$ and $V_0$ are constant, so the graph is a straight line with gradient $-\\dfrac{1}{RC}$ (and intercept $\\ln V_0$)\n\n**(b)(iii)**\n- **M1** Gradient $= -\\dfrac{1}{RC}$, so $RC = \\dfrac{1}{0.0213} = 46.9\\ \\mathrm{s}$\n- **M1** $R = \\dfrac{46.9}{470 \\times 10^{-6}}$\n- **A1** $R = 1.0 \\times 10^5\\ \\Omega$ (accept 99.9 kΩ or 100 kΩ)\n\n**(c)**\n- **M1** $W = \\frac{1}{2}CV^2$, so $W \\propto V^2$ and the energy falls to 25% when $V = 0.5V_0$ $(= 6.0\\ \\mathrm{V})$\n- **M1** $t = RC\\ln\\left(\\dfrac{V_0}{V}\\right) = 46.9 \\times \\ln 2$ (ft their $RC$; use of $\\ln 4$, giving 65 s, scores this mark only)\n- **A1** $t = 33\\ \\mathrm{s}$ (accept 32.5 s)\n\n**(d)**\n- **B1** The graph starts at the same initial potential difference (12.0 V) and is still an exponential decay curve\n- **B1** The potential difference decreases more slowly, because the time constant is doubled, e.g. it takes twice as long to fall to half its initial value", "solution": "**(a)** For a capacitor $V = Q/C$, and $C$ is constant, so the graph of $V$ against $Q$ is a straight line through the origin. Adding a small extra charge $\\Delta Q$ at p.d. $V$ takes work $V\\Delta Q$, which is the area of a thin strip under the graph. Adding all the strips, the total work done (the energy stored) is the area under the graph. This is a triangle, so $W = \\tfrac{1}{2}QV$.\n\n**(b)(i)** A data logger can record many readings at short, precisely timed, regular intervals. There is no reaction-time error, because $V$ and $t$ are recorded together.\n\n**(b)(ii)** $V = V_0 e^{-t/RC}$, so taking natural logs gives\n$$\\ln V = \\ln V_0 - \\frac{t}{RC}$$\nThis has the form $y = mx + c$ with $y = \\ln V$ and $x = t$. Because $R$, $C$ and $V_0$ are constants, the graph is a straight line with gradient $-1/RC$ and intercept $\\ln V_0$.\n\n**(b)(iii)**\n$$-\\frac{1}{RC} = -0.0213\\ \\mathrm{s^{-1}} \\;\\Rightarrow\\; RC = \\frac{1}{0.0213} = 46.9\\ \\mathrm{s}$$\n$$R = \\frac{46.9}{470 \\times 10^{-6}} = 9.99 \\times 10^4\\ \\Omega \\approx 1.0 \\times 10^5\\ \\Omega$$\n\n**(c)** $W = \\tfrac{1}{2}CV^2$, so $W \\propto V^2$. For $W = 0.25W_0$ we need $V = 0.5V_0 = 6.0\\ \\mathrm{V}$.\n$$V = V_0 e^{-t/RC} \\;\\Rightarrow\\; t = RC\\ln\\frac{V_0}{V} = 46.9 \\times \\ln 2 = 32.5\\ \\mathrm{s} \\approx 33\\ \\mathrm{s}$$\n\n**(d)** With resistance $2R$ the time constant doubles, to about $94\\ \\mathrm{s}$. The graph still starts at $12.0\\ \\mathrm{V}$ and is still an exponential decay, but the p.d. falls more slowly. For example, it now takes about $65\\ \\mathrm{s}$ instead of $33\\ \\mathrm{s}$ to fall to $6.0\\ \\mathrm{V}$, and the curve lies above the original at all times after $t = 0$."},
{"id": "ial-phy4-008", "type": "written", "spec": "phy4:6.19", "specs": ["phy4:6.14", "phy4:6.17", "phy4:6.18"], "difficulty": 2, "marks": 13, "stem": "**(a)** A student holds a bar magnet vertically, with its north pole pointing downwards, and releases it from rest above a vertical coil. The magnet falls through the coil. The coil is connected to a data logger that records the e.m.f. induced across the coil. **(i)** Explain why an e.m.f. is induced across the coil as the magnet falls into it. **(2)**\n\n**(ii)** State one change to the coil and one change to the way the magnet is released that would each increase the maximum e.m.f. induced. **(2)**\n\n**(b)** The data logger is removed and the two ends of the coil are joined together, so that a current is induced in the coil as the magnet falls into it. Explain, with reference to Lenz's law, why the magnet now falls into the coil with an acceleration less than $g$. **(3)**\n\n**(c)** A small search coil of 150 turns and cross-sectional area $4.0 \\times 10^{-4}\\ \\mathrm{m^2}$ is placed at the centre of a long solenoid, with the axis of the search coil parallel to the axis of the solenoid. The current in the solenoid is reduced uniformly so that the magnetic flux density at the centre of the solenoid decreases from $6.0 \\times 10^{-3}\\ \\mathrm{T}$ to zero in $0.025\\ \\mathrm{s}$. **(i)** Calculate the initial magnetic flux linkage of the search coil. **(2)**\n\n**(ii)** Calculate the magnitude of the e.m.f. induced across the search coil. **(2)**\n\n**(iii)** The search coil is turned so that its axis is perpendicular to the axis of the solenoid, and the procedure is repeated. State and explain the e.m.f. now induced across the search coil. **(2)**", "markScheme": "**(a)(i)**\n- **B1** The magnetic flux linking the coil changes as the magnet moves into it or the coil cuts the magnetic field lines (flux linkage changes)\n- **B1** By Faraday's law, the induced e.m.f. is proportional to the rate of change of flux linkage\n\n**(a)(ii)**\n- **B1** Coil: increase the number of turns on the coil\n- **B1** Release: drop the magnet from a greater height above the coil (so it moves faster through the coil)\n\n**(b)**\n- **B1** The induced current produces a magnetic field that opposes the change (in flux) that produces it (Lenz's law)\n- **B1** The top of the coil becomes a north pole, which repels the approaching north pole of the magnet\n- **B1** So there is an upward force on the magnet, the resultant force is less than its weight, and its acceleration is less than g\n\n**(c)(i)**\n- **M1** $N\\Phi = NBA = 150 \\times 6.0 \\times 10^{-3} \\times 4.0 \\times 10^{-4}$\n- **A1** $N\\Phi = 3.6 \\times 10^{-4}\\ \\mathrm{Wb}$ (accept Wb turns)\n\n**(c)(ii)**\n- **M1** $\\varepsilon = \\dfrac{\\Delta(N\\Phi)}{\\Delta t} = \\dfrac{3.6 \\times 10^{-4}}{0.025}$ (ft their (c)(i))\n- **A1** $\\varepsilon = 0.014\\ \\mathrm{V}$ (accept 0.0144 V or 14 mV)\n\n**(c)(iii)**\n- **B1** No e.m.f. is induced (the e.m.f. is zero)\n- **B1** The plane of the coil is parallel to the field, so no flux passes through the coil: the flux linkage is zero and does not change", "solution": "**(a)(i)** As the magnet falls into the coil, the magnetic flux through the coil increases, so the flux linkage of the coil changes (the turns cut the magnetic field lines). By Faraday's law, an e.m.f. is induced that is proportional to the rate of change of flux linkage.\n\n**(a)(ii)** Coil: use a coil with more turns. Release: drop the magnet from a greater height, so that it moves faster through the coil and the flux linkage changes at a greater rate.\n\n**(b)** Now a current flows in the coil. By Lenz's law, the induced current is in a direction that opposes the change producing it. As the north pole approaches, the top of the coil becomes a north pole, which repels the magnet. This gives an upward magnetic force on the magnet, so the resultant force on it is less than its weight and its acceleration is less than $g$. The work done against this force is transferred to electrical energy in the coil.\n\n**(c)(i)**\n$$N\\Phi = NBA = 150 \\times 6.0 \\times 10^{-3} \\times 4.0 \\times 10^{-4} = 3.6 \\times 10^{-4}\\ \\mathrm{Wb}$$\n\n**(c)(ii)** The flux linkage falls uniformly to zero, so\n$$\\varepsilon = \\frac{\\Delta(N\\Phi)}{\\Delta t} = \\frac{3.6 \\times 10^{-4}}{0.025} = 0.0144\\ \\mathrm{V} \\approx 0.014\\ \\mathrm{V}\\ (14\\ \\mathrm{mV})$$\n\n**(c)(iii)** The e.m.f. is zero. The plane of the search coil is now parallel to the field lines, so no magnetic flux passes through it. Its flux linkage is zero throughout and does not change, so no e.m.f. is induced."},
{"id": "ial-phy4-009", "type": "written", "spec": "phy4:7.5", "specs": ["phy4:7.4", "phy4:7.6", "phy4:7.8", "phy4:7.9", "phy4:6.15", "phy4:5.6"], "difficulty": 3, "marks": 14, "stem": "**(a)** A particle of mass $m$ and charge $Q$ moves with speed $v$ in a plane perpendicular to a uniform magnetic field of flux density $B$. Show that the radius $r$ of its circular path is given by $r = \\dfrac{p}{BQ}$, where $p$ is the momentum of the particle. **(2)**\n\n**(b)** In a cyclotron, protons travel in semicircular paths inside two hollow D-shaped electrodes (dees) in a vacuum. A uniform magnetic field of flux density $0.80\\ \\mathrm{T}$ acts perpendicular to the dees, and an alternating potential difference is applied between them. The protons leave the cyclotron when the radius of their path reaches $0.45\\ \\mathrm{m}$. **(i)** Explain the roles of the electric field and the magnetic field in the cyclotron. **(2)**\n\n**(ii)** Show that the kinetic energy of the protons as they leave the cyclotron is about $6\\ \\mathrm{MeV}$. **(4)**\n\n**(c)** In a bubble chamber detector, a uniform magnetic field acts perpendicular to the plane in which the particles move. A high-energy photon enters the chamber and produces an electron and a positron. The photon leaves no track. The tracks of the electron and the positron start at the same point and curve in opposite directions, and each track spirals inwards. **(i)** Explain why the photon leaves no track, why the tracks of the electron and the positron curve in opposite directions, and why each track spirals inwards. **(3)**\n\n**(ii)** Calculate the minimum energy, in MeV, of a photon that can produce an electron–positron pair. **(3)**", "markScheme": "**(a)**\n- **B1** The magnetic force provides the centripetal force: $BQv = \\dfrac{mv^2}{r}$\n- **B1** Rearranges to $r = \\dfrac{mv}{BQ}$ and uses $p = mv$ to give $r = \\dfrac{p}{BQ}$\n\n**(b)(i)**\n- **B1** The electric field (across the gap between the dees) accelerates the protons, increasing their kinetic energy each time they cross the gap\n- **B1** The magnetic field exerts a force perpendicular to the velocity, so the protons move in semicircular paths and return to the gap\n\n**(b)(ii)**\n- **M1** $p = BQr = 0.80 \\times 1.60 \\times 10^{-19} \\times 0.45$ $(= 5.76 \\times 10^{-20}\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}})$\n- **M1** $E_k = \\dfrac{p^2}{2m} = \\dfrac{(5.76 \\times 10^{-20})^2}{2 \\times 1.67 \\times 10^{-27}}$ $(= 9.93 \\times 10^{-13}\\ \\mathrm{J})$\n- **M1** Converts to eV: $\\dfrac{9.93 \\times 10^{-13}}{1.60 \\times 10^{-19}}$\n- **A1*** $E_k = 6.2\\ \\mathrm{MeV}$ (given answer: must see 6.2 MeV or $6.2 \\times 10^6$ eV)\n\n**(c)(i)**\n- **B1** The photon is uncharged, so it does not ionise the liquid and leaves no track\n- **B1** The electron and positron have opposite charges, so the magnetic forces on them are in opposite directions (Fleming's left-hand rule)\n- **B1** The particles lose kinetic energy (by ionising the liquid), so their momentum decreases and, as $r = p/BQ$, the radius of the path decreases\n\n**(c)(ii)**\n- **M1** $\\Delta E = c^2\\Delta m$ with $\\Delta m = 2 \\times 9.11 \\times 10^{-31}\\ \\mathrm{kg}$: $\\Delta E = (3.00 \\times 10^8)^2 \\times 2 \\times 9.11 \\times 10^{-31}$ $(= 1.64 \\times 10^{-13}\\ \\mathrm{J})$\n- **M1** Converts to MeV: $\\dfrac{1.64 \\times 10^{-13}}{1.60 \\times 10^{-19} \\times 10^6}$ (use of one electron mass only, giving 0.51 MeV, scores this mark only)\n- **A1** $E = 1.02\\ \\mathrm{MeV}$ (accept 1.0 MeV)", "solution": "**(a)** The magnetic force on the particle is always perpendicular to its velocity, so it provides the centripetal force:\n$$BQv = \\frac{mv^2}{r} \\;\\Rightarrow\\; r = \\frac{mv}{BQ} = \\frac{p}{BQ}$$\n\n**(b)(i)** The electric field in the gap between the dees accelerates the protons, increasing their speed (kinetic energy) each time they cross the gap. The p.d. alternates so that the field always points the right way to accelerate them. The magnetic field exerts a force perpendicular to the protons' velocity, so they move in semicircles inside the dees and are returned to the gap. As their speed increases, the radius of their path increases.\n\n**(b)(ii)**\n$$p = BQr = 0.80 \\times 1.60 \\times 10^{-19} \\times 0.45 = 5.76 \\times 10^{-20}\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$$\n$$E_k = \\frac{p^2}{2m} = \\frac{(5.76 \\times 10^{-20})^2}{2 \\times 1.67 \\times 10^{-27}} = 9.93 \\times 10^{-13}\\ \\mathrm{J}$$\n$$E_k = \\frac{9.93 \\times 10^{-13}}{1.60 \\times 10^{-19}} = 6.2 \\times 10^{6}\\ \\mathrm{eV} = 6.2\\ \\mathrm{MeV} \\approx 6\\ \\mathrm{MeV}$$\n\n**(c)(i)**\n- No photon track: the photon is uncharged, so it does not ionise the liquid.\n- Opposite curvature: the electron and positron have equal but opposite charges, so the magnetic forces on them act in opposite directions (Fleming's left-hand rule).\n- Inward spirals: each particle loses kinetic energy as it ionises the liquid, so its momentum decreases. Since $r = p/BQ$ with $B$ and $Q$ constant, the radius of curvature decreases.\n\n**(c)(ii)** The minimum photon energy equals the rest energy of the two particles created:\n$$\\Delta E = c^2\\Delta m = (3.00 \\times 10^8)^2 \\times 2 \\times 9.11 \\times 10^{-31} = 1.64 \\times 10^{-13}\\ \\mathrm{J}$$\n$$\\frac{1.64 \\times 10^{-13}}{1.60 \\times 10^{-19}} = 1.02 \\times 10^6\\ \\mathrm{eV} = 1.02\\ \\mathrm{MeV}$$"},
{"id": "ial-phy4-010", "type": "written", "spec": "phy4:7.13", "specs": ["phy4:7.11", "phy4:7.12", "phy4:7.14", "phy4:7.1"], "difficulty": 2, "marks": 13, "stem": "**(a)** Explain why $^{12}_{6}\\mathrm{C}$ and $^{14}_{6}\\mathrm{C}$ are described as isotopes of carbon. **(1)**\n\n**(b)** In beta-minus decay, a neutron decays into a proton, an electron and an electron antineutrino: $\\mathrm{n} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{p} + \\mathrm{e}^- + \\bar{\\nu}_e$. **(i)** Show that charge, baryon number and lepton number are all conserved in this decay. **(3)**\n\n**(ii)** Write an equation for this decay in terms of quarks. **(2)**\n\n**(c)** The positive pion, $\\pi^+$, is a meson with quark composition $\\mathrm{u}\\bar{\\mathrm{d}}$. **(i)** Show that the charge of the $\\pi^+$ is $+e$. **(1)**\n\n**(ii)** The negative pion, $\\pi^-$, is the antiparticle of the $\\pi^+$. State the quark composition of the $\\pi^-$. **(1)**\n\n**(d)** Two interactions are proposed. Interaction 1: $\\pi^- + \\mathrm{p} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{n} + \\pi^0$. Interaction 2: $\\mathrm{p} + \\mathrm{e}^- \\rightarrow \\mathrm{n} + \\bar{\\nu}_e$. Using conservation laws, deduce which of these interactions is possible. **(3)**\n\n**(e)** The top quark was predicted to exist many years before it was detected. Explain why physicists predicted its existence. **(2)**", "markScheme": "**(a)**\n- **B1** Same number of protons (proton number 6) but different numbers of neutrons (6 and 8) or different nucleon numbers\n\n**(b)(i)**\n- **B1** Charge: $0 \\rightarrow (+1) + (-1) + 0 = 0$\n- **B1** Baryon number: $1 \\rightarrow 1 + 0 + 0 = 1$\n- **B1** Lepton number: $0 \\rightarrow 0 + 1 + (-1) = 0$\n\n**(b)(ii)**\n- **B1** A down quark changes to an up quark: $\\mathrm{d} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{u}$\n- **B1** Complete equation $\\mathrm{d} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{u} + \\mathrm{e}^- + \\bar{\\nu}_e$ (accept $\\mathrm{udd} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{uud} + \\mathrm{e}^- + \\bar{\\nu}_e$)\n\n**(c)(i)**\n- **B1** $+\\frac{2}{3}e + \\frac{1}{3}e = +e$ (antidown quark has charge $+\\frac{1}{3}e$)\n\n**(c)(ii)**\n- **B1** $\\bar{\\mathrm{u}}\\mathrm{d}$\n\n**(d)**\n- **B1** Charge and baryon number are conserved in both interactions (charge $0 \\rightarrow 0$; baryon number $1 \\rightarrow 1$)\n- **B1** Lepton number: interaction 1 is $0 \\rightarrow 0$ (conserved); interaction 2 is $+1 \\rightarrow -1$ (not conserved)\n- **B1** So interaction 1 is possible and interaction 2 is not possible\n\n**(e)**\n- **B1** The quark–lepton model is symmetrical: quarks occur in pairs (generations), matching the six leptons in three generations\n- **B1** The bottom quark had been discovered without a partner, so a sixth quark (the top quark) was needed to complete the pattern", "solution": "**(a)** Both nuclei have the same number of protons (proton number 6), so they are the same element. They have different numbers of neutrons (6 and 8), and so different nucleon numbers.\n\n**(b)(i)** For $\\mathrm{n} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{p} + \\mathrm{e}^- + \\bar{\\nu}_e$:\n- Charge: $0 \\rightarrow (+1) + (-1) + 0 = 0$, conserved\n- Baryon number: $1 \\rightarrow 1 + 0 + 0 = 1$, conserved\n- Lepton number: $0 \\rightarrow 0 + (+1) + (-1) = 0$, conserved\n\n**(b)(ii)** The neutron (udd) becomes a proton (uud), so one down quark changes into an up quark:\n$$\\mathrm{d} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{u} + \\mathrm{e}^- + \\bar{\\nu}_e$$\n\n**(c)(i)** The up quark has charge $+\\tfrac{2}{3}e$ and the antidown quark has charge $+\\tfrac{1}{3}e$, so the total charge is $+\\tfrac{2}{3}e + \\tfrac{1}{3}e = +e$.\n\n**(c)(ii)** The antiparticle has each quark replaced by its antiquark: $\\pi^- = \\bar{\\mathrm{u}}\\mathrm{d}$, with charge $-\\tfrac{2}{3}e - \\tfrac{1}{3}e = -e$.\n\n**(d)** Interaction 1, $\\pi^- + \\mathrm{p} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{n} + \\pi^0$:\n- charge $(-1) + (+1) = 0 \\rightarrow 0 + 0 = 0$\n- baryon number $0 + 1 = 1 \\rightarrow 1 + 0 = 1$\n- lepton number $0 \\rightarrow 0$\n\nAll three are conserved, so interaction 1 is possible.\n\nInteraction 2, $\\mathrm{p} + \\mathrm{e}^- \\rightarrow \\mathrm{n} + \\bar{\\nu}_e$:\n- charge $(+1) + (-1) = 0 \\rightarrow 0$\n- baryon number $1 \\rightarrow 1$\n- lepton number $0 + (+1) = +1 \\rightarrow 0 + (-1) = -1$\n\nLepton number is not conserved, so interaction 2 is not possible.\n\n**(e)** The quark–lepton model has a symmetry: quarks, like leptons, occur in pairs (three generations). There were six leptons, and once the bottom quark had been discovered without a partner, the symmetry of the model required a sixth quark, the top quark, to complete the third generation. This prediction was later confirmed experimentally."},
{"id": "ial-phy4-011", "type": "mcq", "spec": "phy4:5.8", "specs": ["phy4:5.7"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "A turntable rotates at a constant rate. It turns through an angle of $270^\\circ$ in $1.5\\ \\mathrm{s}$.\n\nWhat is the angular velocity of the turntable? **(1)**", "options": ["$4.7\\ \\mathrm{rad\\,s^{-1}}$", "$7.1\\ \\mathrm{rad\\,s^{-1}}$", "$3.1\\ \\mathrm{rad\\,s^{-1}}$", "$180\\ \\mathrm{rad\\,s^{-1}}$"], "answer": "C", "solution": "Convert the angle to radians: $270^\\circ \\times \\dfrac{2\\pi}{360^\\circ} = 4.71\\ \\mathrm{rad}$. Then\n$$\\omega = \\frac{\\Delta\\theta}{\\Delta t} = \\frac{4.71}{1.5} = 3.1\\ \\mathrm{rad\\,s^{-1}}$$\nThe answer is C.\n\n- A ($4.7\\ \\mathrm{rad\\,s^{-1}}$) is the angle in radians, not divided by the time.\n- B ($7.1\\ \\mathrm{rad\\,s^{-1}}$) multiplies the angle by the time instead of dividing.\n- D ($180\\ \\mathrm{rad\\,s^{-1}}$) is the angle in degrees divided by the time ($180^\\circ\\ \\mathrm{s^{-1}}$), not converted to radians."},
{"id": "ial-phy4-012", "type": "mcq", "spec": "phy4:5.11", "specs": ["phy4:5.10"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A ball of mass $0.20\\ \\mathrm{kg}$ is attached to a light string and whirled in a vertical circle of radius $0.80\\ \\mathrm{m}$. At the lowest point of the circle the speed of the ball is $5.0\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$.\n\nWhat is the tension in the string when the ball is at the lowest point? **(1)**", "options": ["$1.3\\ \\mathrm{N}$", "$4.3\\ \\mathrm{N}$", "$6.3\\ \\mathrm{N}$", "$8.2\\ \\mathrm{N}$"], "answer": "D", "solution": "At the lowest point the tension $T$ acts upwards (towards the centre) and the weight acts downwards. Their resultant provides the centripetal force:\n$$T - mg = \\frac{mv^2}{r} \\;\\Rightarrow\\; T = 0.20 \\times 9.81 + \\frac{0.20 \\times 5.0^2}{0.80} = 1.96 + 6.25 = 8.2\\ \\mathrm{N}$$\nThe answer is D.\n\n- A ($1.3\\ \\mathrm{N}$) uses $mv/r$ instead of $mv^2/r$ and ignores the weight.\n- B ($4.3\\ \\mathrm{N}$) subtracts the weight ($mv^2/r - mg$), which would apply at the top of the circle, not the bottom.\n- C ($6.3\\ \\mathrm{N}$) is the centripetal force alone and forgets that the tension must also support the weight."},
{"id": "ial-phy4-013", "type": "mcq", "spec": "phy4:6.2", "specs": ["phy4:6.1"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "A positron is held at rest at a point in an electric field, where it experiences a force $F$. The positron is replaced by an alpha particle held at rest at the same point.\n\nWhich of the following gives the force on the alpha particle? **(1)**", "options": ["$\\dfrac{F}{2}$ in the same direction as the force on the positron", "$2F$ in the same direction as the force on the positron", "$2F$ in the opposite direction to the force on the positron", "$4F$ in the same direction as the force on the positron"], "answer": "B", "solution": "The electric field strength $E$ at the point depends only on the field, not on the charge placed there. Since $E = F/Q$, the force is $F = EQ$, proportional to the charge. The positron has charge $+e$ and the alpha particle has charge $+2e$, so the force on the alpha particle is $2F$. Both charges are positive, so the forces are in the same direction. The answer is B.\n\n- A halves the force instead of doubling it.\n- C has the right magnitude but the wrong direction: both particles are positive.\n- D uses the nucleon number (4) of the alpha particle instead of its charge (+2e)."},
{"id": "ial-phy4-014", "type": "mcq", "spec": "phy4:6.16", "difficulty": 1, "stem": "A straight wire of length $15\\ \\mathrm{cm}$ carries a current of $3.0\\ \\mathrm{A}$. The wire lies in a uniform magnetic field of flux density $0.20\\ \\mathrm{T}$ and makes an angle of $30^\\circ$ with the direction of the field.\n\nWhat is the magnitude of the force on the wire? **(1)**", "options": ["$0.045\\ \\mathrm{N}$", "$0.078\\ \\mathrm{N}$", "$0.090\\ \\mathrm{N}$", "$4.5\\ \\mathrm{N}$"], "answer": "A", "solution": "$$F = BIl\\sin\\theta = 0.20 \\times 3.0 \\times 0.15 \\times \\sin 30^\\circ = 0.045\\ \\mathrm{N}$$\nThe answer is A.\n\n- B ($0.078\\ \\mathrm{N}$) uses $\\cos 30^\\circ$ instead of $\\sin 30^\\circ$.\n- C ($0.090\\ \\mathrm{N}$) ignores the angle, which would only be correct if the wire were perpendicular to the field.\n- D ($4.5\\ \\mathrm{N}$) uses the length in centimetres (15) instead of metres (0.15)."},
{"id": "ial-phy4-015", "type": "mcq", "spec": "phy4:7.2", "difficulty": 1, "stem": "In an alpha particle scattering experiment, a beam of alpha particles is directed at a very thin gold foil in a vacuum. Most of the alpha particles pass straight through the foil with little or no deflection.\n\nWhich conclusion can be drawn from this observation alone? **(1)**", "options": ["The nucleus is positively charged.", "The atom is a sphere of positive charge with electrons embedded in it.", "Most of the volume of an atom is empty space.", "The nucleus contains protons and neutrons."], "answer": "C", "solution": "If most alpha particles pass through thousands of layers of gold atoms almost undeflected, they must meet nothing that exerts a significant force on them for most of their path. Most of the volume of an atom must therefore be empty space. The answer is C.\n\n- A: the evidence that the nucleus is positively charged comes from the small number of alpha particles deflected through large angles (repulsion), not from the undeflected ones.\n- B: this is the earlier 'plum pudding' model, which the scattering experiment replaced with the nuclear model.\n- D: the experiment gives no information about the particles that make up the nucleus."},
{"id": "ial-phy4-016", "type": "mcq", "spec": "phy4:7.7", "difficulty": 2, "stem": "High-energy electrons are used to investigate the internal structure of nucleons, which have a diameter of about $1 \\times 10^{-15}\\ \\mathrm{m}$. To resolve this structure, the de Broglie wavelength of the electrons must be no greater than about this diameter.\n\nWhat is the momentum of an electron with a de Broglie wavelength of $1.0 \\times 10^{-15}\\ \\mathrm{m}$? **(1)**", "options": ["$6.6 \\times 10^{-49}\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$", "$1.5 \\times 10^{18}\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$", "$7.3 \\times 10^{11}\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$", "$6.6 \\times 10^{-19}\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$"], "answer": "D", "solution": "Using $\\lambda = h/p$:\n$$p = \\frac{h}{\\lambda} = \\frac{6.63 \\times 10^{-34}}{1.0 \\times 10^{-15}} = 6.6 \\times 10^{-19}\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$$\nThe answer is D. This is a very large momentum for an electron, which is why very high energies are needed: only then is the wavelength short enough to resolve structure inside a nucleon.\n\n- A multiplies $h$ by $\\lambda$ instead of dividing.\n- B calculates $\\lambda / h$, the inverse of the correct expression.\n- C calculates $h/(m\\lambda)$, which is a speed (in m s⁻¹), not a momentum. It is also impossible, since it exceeds the speed of light."},
{"id": "ial-phy4-017", "type": "mcq", "spec": "phy4:7.10", "difficulty": 2, "stem": "Muons are produced in the upper atmosphere about $15\\ \\mathrm{km}$ above sea level and travel towards the ground at a speed of $0.995c$. The mean lifetime of a muon at rest is $2.2\\ \\mu\\mathrm{s}$. Large numbers of these muons are detected at sea level.\n\nWhich statement is correct? **(1)**", "options": ["Without relativistic effects, a typical muon would travel only about 660 m before decaying, so the observations show that the lifetime of a fast-moving muon, as measured by an observer on the Earth, is increased.", "Without relativistic effects, a typical muon would travel about 15 km before decaying, so relativistic effects are not significant.", "The muons reach sea level because they travel faster than the speed of light.", "The lifetime of a fast-moving muon, as measured by an observer on the Earth, is decreased, so the muon reaches sea level before it decays."], "answer": "A", "solution": "Without relativity, a typical muon travels\n$$d = vt = 0.995 \\times 3.00 \\times 10^8 \\times 2.2 \\times 10^{-6} = 660\\ \\mathrm{m}$$\nbefore decaying, which is far less than 15 km. Since large numbers reach sea level, the lifetime of the fast-moving muons, measured in the Earth's frame, must be much longer than $2.2\\ \\mu\\mathrm{s}$. This relativistic increase in lifetime is significant because the muons travel so close to $c$. The answer is A.\n\n- B: the distance is wrong. $0.995c \\times 2.2\\ \\mu\\mathrm{s}$ is about 660 m, not 15 km.\n- C: no particle with mass can travel at or faster than the speed of light.\n- D: the lifetime of a moving particle measured by a stationary observer increases; a shorter lifetime would mean even fewer muons reached the ground."},
{"id": "ial-phy4-018", "type": "mcq", "spec": "phy4:7.11", "specs": ["phy4:7.1"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "How many up quarks are there in a nucleus of $^{7}_{3}\\mathrm{Li}$? **(1)**", "options": ["7", "10", "11", "21"], "answer": "B", "solution": "The nucleus has proton number 3 and nucleon number 7, so it contains 3 protons and $7 - 3 = 4$ neutrons. A proton is uud (2 up quarks) and a neutron is udd (1 up quark):\n$$\\text{up quarks} = 3 \\times 2 + 4 \\times 1 = 10$$\nThe answer is B.\n\n- A (7) is the nucleon number, not a number of quarks.\n- C (11) is the number of down quarks: $3 \\times 1 + 4 \\times 2 = 11$.\n- D (21) is the total number of quarks: $7 \\times 3 = 21$."},
{"id": "ial-phy5-001", "type": "written", "spec": "phy5:8.3", "specs": ["phy5:8.1", "phy5:8.4"], "difficulty": 1, "marks": 11, "stem": "A student determines the specific latent heat of fusion of ice using two identical filter funnels, A and B. Each funnel is packed with crushed ice at 0 °C and has an identical electrical heater buried in the ice. Only the heater in funnel A is switched on. Water drips from each funnel into a beaker placed below it.\n\nWhen water is dripping steadily from both funnels, the student places an empty beaker under each funnel and starts a stopwatch. After 300 s the beakers are removed and the mass of water in each is measured.\n\n| | Funnel A | Funnel B |\n|---|---|---|\n| mass of water collected / g | 48.6 | 11.2 |\n\nThe potential difference across the heater in funnel A is 12.0 V and the current in it is 3.50 A.\n\n**(a)** Explain why funnel B is included in this experiment. **(2)**\n\n**(b)** Calculate the specific latent heat of fusion of ice. **(3)**\n\n**(c)** As the ice melts, its internal energy increases but its temperature remains at 0 °C. Explain this in terms of the molecules of the ice. **(2)**\n\n**(d)** A freezer removes energy at a constant rate of 65.0 W from 0.250 kg of water that is initially at 18.0 °C. Calculate the minimum time taken for all of the water to become ice at 0 °C. Specific heat capacity of water $= 4.18 \\times 10^{3}\\ \\mathrm{J\\,kg^{-1}\\,K^{-1}}$; specific latent heat of fusion of ice $= 3.34 \\times 10^{5}\\ \\mathrm{J\\,kg^{-1}}$. **(4)**", "markScheme": "**(a)**\n- **B1** Ice in funnel B melts only because of energy transferred to it from the (warmer) surroundings\n- **B1** Funnel A gains energy from the surroundings at the same rate, so (mass from A − mass from B) is the mass melted by the heater alone / this allows for the energy gained from the surroundings\n\n**(b)**\n- **M1** Energy supplied by heater: $E = IVt = 3.50 \\times 12.0 \\times 300 = 1.26 \\times 10^{4}$ J\n- **M1** Mass melted by heater $\\Delta m = 48.6 - 11.2 = 37.4$ g $= 0.0374$ kg, used in $L = E/\\Delta m$\n- **A1** $L = 3.37 \\times 10^{5}\\ \\mathrm{J\\,kg^{-1}}$ (accept $3.4 \\times 10^{5}\\ \\mathrm{J\\,kg^{-1}}$; using 48.6 g alone gives $2.59 \\times 10^{5}$ and scores the first M1 only)\n\n**(c)**\n- **B1** The energy supplied increases the (intermolecular) potential energy of the molecules, as bonds between molecules are broken / molecules move further apart\n- **B1** The mean kinetic energy of the molecules does not change, and temperature depends on (is proportional to) the mean molecular kinetic energy, so the temperature is constant\n\n**(d)**\n- **M1** Energy removed cooling the water to 0 °C: $mc\\Delta\\theta = 0.250 \\times 4180 \\times 18.0 = 1.88 \\times 10^{4}$ J\n- **M1** Energy removed freezing the water: $L\\Delta m = 0.250 \\times 3.34 \\times 10^{5} = 8.35 \\times 10^{4}$ J\n- **M1** Adds the two energies ($1.02 \\times 10^{5}$ J) and uses $t = E/P$\n- **A1** $t = 1.57 \\times 10^{3}$ s (accept 1570 s or 26.2 min; must include unit)", "solution": "**(a)** The ice in funnel B melts only because energy is transferred to it from the warmer surroundings. Funnel A is identical, so it gains energy from the surroundings at the same rate as well as from its heater. Subtracting the mass collected from B from the mass collected from A gives the mass of ice melted by the heater alone. This allows for the energy gained from the surroundings.\n\n**(b)**\n$$E = IVt = 3.50 \\times 12.0 \\times 300 = 1.26 \\times 10^{4}\\ \\text{J}$$\n$$\\Delta m = 48.6 - 11.2 = 37.4\\ \\text{g} = 0.0374\\ \\text{kg}$$\n$$L = \\frac{E}{\\Delta m} = \\frac{1.26 \\times 10^{4}}{0.0374} = 3.37 \\times 10^{5}\\ \\mathrm{J\\,kg^{-1}}$$\n\n**(c)** Internal energy is the sum of the randomly distributed kinetic and potential energies of the molecules. While the ice melts, the energy supplied breaks the bonds between molecules, so their potential energy increases. The mean kinetic energy of the molecules does not change. Temperature depends on the mean kinetic energy, so it stays at 0 °C even though the internal energy increases.\n\n**(d)**\nCooling the water from 18.0 °C to 0 °C:\n$$\\Delta E_1 = mc\\Delta\\theta = 0.250 \\times 4180 \\times 18.0 = 1.88 \\times 10^{4}\\ \\text{J}$$\nFreezing the water at 0 °C:\n$$\\Delta E_2 = L\\Delta m = 0.250 \\times 3.34 \\times 10^{5} = 8.35 \\times 10^{4}\\ \\text{J}$$\nTotal: $\\Delta E = 1.02 \\times 10^{5}$ J\n$$t = \\frac{\\Delta E}{P} = \\frac{1.02 \\times 10^{5}}{65.0} = 1.57 \\times 10^{3}\\ \\text{s}\\ (\\approx 26\\ \\text{minutes})$$"},
{"id": "ial-phy5-002", "type": "written", "spec": "phy5:8.8", "specs": ["phy5:8.7", "phy5:8.5"], "difficulty": 2, "marks": 12, "stem": "A student investigates how the pressure $p$ of a fixed mass of air depends on its volume $V$ at constant temperature. The air is trapped in a sealed syringe connected to a pressure gauge. The student pushes the piston in, reducing the volume in steps, and records $V$ from the scale on the syringe and $p$ from the gauge. The temperature of the room is 21 °C.\n\n**(a)** Explain why the student should wait for a short time after each change in volume before recording the pressure. **(2)**\n\n**(b)** Explain why the student plots a graph of $p$ against $\\dfrac{1}{V}$ rather than a graph of $p$ against $V$. **(2)**\n\n**(c)** The graph of $p$ against $\\dfrac{1}{V}$ is a straight line through the origin with a gradient of $6.05\\ \\mathrm{Pa\\,m^{3}}$. Calculate the number of air molecules in the syringe. **(3)**\n\n**(d)** When the volume of the air is $50.0\\ \\mathrm{cm^{3}}$, its pressure is $1.21 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa and its temperature is 21 °C. The student then rapidly pushes the piston in, so that the volume decreases to $20.0\\ \\mathrm{cm^{3}}$ and the temperature of the air rises to 45 °C. No air escapes. Calculate the new pressure of the air. **(3)**\n\n**(e)** The student states that if the air could be cooled to absolute zero, its pressure would be zero. Explain what is meant by absolute zero and why the pressure of an ideal gas would be zero at this temperature. **(2)**", "markScheme": "**(a)**\n- **B1** Compressing the air does work on it, so its temperature rises (the temperature changes when the volume is changed)\n- **B1** Waiting allows energy to be transferred to the surroundings so the air returns to room temperature (so the temperature is the same for every reading)\n\n**(b)**\n- **B1** $pV = NkT$ so $p = NkT \\times \\dfrac{1}{V}$; with $N$ and $T$ constant, $p \\propto \\dfrac{1}{V}$\n- **B1** So $p$ against $1/V$ should be a straight line through the origin, which is easier to test/recognise than the curve obtained for $p$ against $V$ (and its gradient $NkT$ can be used)\n\n**(c)**\n- **M1** $T = 21 + 273 = 294$ K\n- **M1** gradient $= NkT$ so $N = \\dfrac{6.05}{1.38 \\times 10^{-23} \\times 294}$\n- **A1** $N = 1.49 \\times 10^{21}$ (using $T = 21$ scores the second M1 only)\n\n**(d)**\n- **M1** Both temperatures in kelvin: $T_1 = 294$ K and $T_2 = 318$ K\n- **M1** Uses $\\dfrac{p_1V_1}{T_1} = \\dfrac{p_2V_2}{T_2}$: $p_2 = \\dfrac{1.21 \\times 10^{5} \\times 50.0 \\times 318}{294 \\times 20.0}$ (or $p_2 = NkT_2/V_2$ with their $N$ and $V_2 = 2.00 \\times 10^{-5}\\ \\mathrm{m^3}$)\n- **A1** $p_2 = 3.27 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa (accept $3.3 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa)\n\n**(e)**\n- **B1** Absolute zero (0 K, about −273 °C) is the lowest possible temperature, at which the molecules have minimum (zero) kinetic energy, since mean molecular kinetic energy is proportional to absolute temperature\n- **B1** So the molecules would not collide with the walls / there would be no change of momentum at the walls, so no force on the walls and $p = 0$ (or $p = NkT/V = 0$ when $T = 0$)", "solution": "**(a)** Pushing the piston in does work on the air, which raises its temperature. Waiting gives time for energy to be transferred to the surroundings, so the air returns to room temperature before the pressure is read. This keeps the temperature constant, as the investigation requires.\n\n**(b)** For a fixed mass of gas at constant temperature, $pV = NkT$ gives $p = NkT\\left(\\dfrac{1}{V}\\right)$, so $p \\propto \\dfrac{1}{V}$. A graph of $p$ against $1/V$ should therefore be a straight line through the origin, which is easy to recognise, and its gradient ($NkT$) can be used. A graph of $p$ against $V$ is a curve, which cannot easily be shown to be an inverse proportion.\n\n**(c)** Gradient $= NkT$ with $T = 21 + 273 = 294$ K:\n$$N = \\frac{6.05}{1.38 \\times 10^{-23} \\times 294} = 1.49 \\times 10^{21}$$\n\n**(d)** $T_1 = 294$ K, $T_2 = 45 + 273 = 318$ K.\n$$p_2 = \\frac{p_1V_1T_2}{T_1V_2} = \\frac{1.21 \\times 10^{5} \\times 50.0 \\times 318}{294 \\times 20.0} = 3.27 \\times 10^{5}\\ \\text{Pa}$$\n(The volume ratio can be used directly in cm³.)\n\n**(e)** Absolute zero (0 K ≈ −273 °C) is the lowest possible temperature. The mean kinetic energy of the molecules is proportional to the absolute temperature, so at absolute zero the molecules have minimum (zero) kinetic energy. They would not move to the walls, so there would be no change of momentum and no force on the walls. The pressure would therefore be zero, as $p = NkT/V$ also shows."},
{"id": "ial-phy5-003", "type": "written", "spec": "phy5:8.6", "specs": ["phy5:8.9", "phy5:9.4"], "difficulty": 3, "marks": 14, "stem": "**(a)** A cube of side $l$ contains $N$ molecules of an ideal gas, each of mass $m$. Use the kinetic model of an ideal gas to derive the equation\n\n$$pV = \\tfrac{1}{3}Nm\\langle c^2\\rangle$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the pressure of the gas, $V$ is its volume and $\\langle c^2\\rangle$ is the mean square speed of the molecules. You should state clearly where you use the assumptions of the model. **(6)**\n\n**(b)** Use the equation in (a) and the equation $pV = NkT$ to show that the mean kinetic energy of a molecule of an ideal gas is $\\tfrac{3}{2}kT$. **(2)**\n\nThe core of the Sun consists mainly of protons and electrons, which can be modelled as an ideal gas. For two protons to fuse, they must come within a separation of about $2.0 \\times 10^{-15}$ m of each other.\n\n**(c)** Two protons, each with a kinetic energy equal to the mean kinetic energy of the particles in a gas at temperature $T$, approach each other head-on. Show that for the protons to reach a separation of $2.0 \\times 10^{-15}$ m, $T$ would need to be about $3 \\times 10^{9}$ K. **(3)**\n\n**(d)** The temperature of the Sun's core is only about $1.5 \\times 10^{7}$ K. Explain why fusion still takes place in the core at a rate high enough to maintain the energy output of the Sun. **(3)**", "markScheme": "**(a)**\n- **B1** One molecule with velocity component $u$ perpendicular to a wall; the collision with the wall is elastic so it rebounds with $-u$ and its change of momentum is $2mu$\n- **M1** Time between successive collisions with the same wall $= 2l/u$ (no collisions with other molecules in between / collision time negligible)\n- **M1** Force on wall = rate of change of momentum $= \\dfrac{2mu}{2l/u} = \\dfrac{mu^2}{l}$\n- **M1** Pressure = force ÷ area $= \\dfrac{mu^2}{l^3} = \\dfrac{mu^2}{V}$\n- **M1** For $N$ molecules $p = \\dfrac{Nm\\langle u^2\\rangle}{V}$; motion is random so $\\langle u^2\\rangle = \\langle v^2\\rangle = \\langle w^2\\rangle$ and $\\langle c^2\\rangle = 3\\langle u^2\\rangle$\n- **A1*** $pV = \\tfrac{1}{3}Nm\\langle c^2\\rangle$ correctly obtained, with at least two assumptions (elastic collisions, random motion, negligible collision time / no intermolecular forces, negligible molecular volume) stated where used\n\n**(b)**\n- **M1** Equates $\\tfrac{1}{3}Nm\\langle c^2\\rangle = NkT$ to give $m\\langle c^2\\rangle = 3kT$\n- **A1*** Multiplies by ½ to obtain $\\tfrac{1}{2}m\\langle c^2\\rangle = \\tfrac{3}{2}kT$\n\n**(c)**\n- **M1** Electric potential energy at closest approach $E_p = \\dfrac{Q_1Q_2}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r} = \\dfrac{8.99 \\times 10^{9} \\times (1.60 \\times 10^{-19})^2}{2.0 \\times 10^{-15}} = 1.15 \\times 10^{-13}$ J\n- **M1** Kinetic energy of each proton $= \\tfrac{1}{2}E_p$, equated to $\\tfrac{3}{2}kT$ (i.e. $3kT = E_p$)\n- **A1*** $T = 2.8 \\times 10^{9}$ K (at least 2 s.f. seen; not halving gives $5.6 \\times 10^{9}$ K and scores the first M1 only)\n\n**(d)**\n- **B1** The protons have a (wide) distribution of kinetic energies, so a small fraction have kinetic energies much greater than the mean, enough to get close enough to fuse (accept quantum tunnelling)\n- **B1** The density in the core is very high, so there are very many protons per unit volume and collisions between protons are very frequent\n- **B1** So although the probability of fusion in any one collision is very small, the number of fusion reactions per second is large enough to release energy at the rate needed", "solution": "**(a)** Consider one molecule moving with velocity component $u$ perpendicular to one face of the cube. The collision with the wall is **elastic**, so the molecule rebounds with velocity $-u$:\n$$\\Delta(\\text{momentum}) = mu - (-mu) = 2mu$$\nThe molecule travels a distance $2l$ before it hits the same wall again. Assuming it **does not collide with other molecules** on the way and the **time of the collision is negligible**, the time between collisions is $\\Delta t = 2l/u$.\n$$F = \\frac{\\Delta(\\text{momentum})}{\\Delta t} = \\frac{2mu}{2l/u} = \\frac{mu^2}{l}$$\n$$p = \\frac{F}{l^2} = \\frac{mu^2}{l^3} = \\frac{mu^2}{V}$$\nFor $N$ molecules with a range of speeds, $p = \\dfrac{Nm\\langle u^2\\rangle}{V}$. The motion is **random**, so there is no preferred direction: $\\langle u^2\\rangle = \\langle v^2\\rangle = \\langle w^2\\rangle$. Since $c^2 = u^2 + v^2 + w^2$, we have $\\langle c^2\\rangle = 3\\langle u^2\\rangle$. Therefore\n$$p = \\frac{Nm\\langle c^2\\rangle}{3V} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad pV = \\tfrac{1}{3}Nm\\langle c^2\\rangle$$\n(Other assumptions: no intermolecular forces except during collisions; the volume of the molecules is negligible compared with $V$.)\n\n**(b)**\n$$\\tfrac{1}{3}Nm\\langle c^2\\rangle = NkT \\Rightarrow m\\langle c^2\\rangle = 3kT \\Rightarrow \\tfrac{1}{2}m\\langle c^2\\rangle = \\tfrac{3}{2}kT$$\n\n**(c)** At closest approach, all the kinetic energy has been transferred to electric potential energy:\n$$E_p = \\frac{Q_1Q_2}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r} = \\frac{8.99 \\times 10^{9} \\times (1.60 \\times 10^{-19})^2}{2.0 \\times 10^{-15}} = 1.15 \\times 10^{-13}\\ \\text{J}$$\nEach proton supplies half of this, so $\\tfrac{3}{2}kT = \\tfrac{1}{2}E_p = 5.75 \\times 10^{-14}$ J:\n$$T = \\frac{5.75 \\times 10^{-14}}{1.5 \\times 1.38 \\times 10^{-23}} = 2.8 \\times 10^{9}\\ \\text{K} \\approx 3 \\times 10^{9}\\ \\text{K}$$\n\n**(d)** The value in (c) is for protons with the *mean* kinetic energy. The protons have a distribution of kinetic energies, so a small fraction have much more than the mean, enough to get close enough to fuse (quantum tunnelling also helps). The density in the core is very high, so there are very many protons per unit volume and collisions are very frequent. Although only a tiny fraction of collisions result in fusion, the number of fusion reactions per second is still large enough to release energy at the rate needed to maintain the core temperature and the Sun's output."},
{"id": "ial-phy5-004", "type": "written", "spec": "phy5:9.10", "specs": ["phy5:9.1", "phy5:9.2", "phy5:9.7"], "difficulty": 3, "marks": 13, "stem": "Some space probes are powered by a radioisotope thermoelectric generator (RTG). The RTG contains plutonium-238, ${}^{238}_{94}\\mathrm{Pu}$, which decays by α emission to an isotope of uranium (U). Part of the energy released by the decays is transferred to electrical energy.\n\n| nucleus or particle | mass / u |\n|---|---|\n| plutonium-238 nucleus | 237.99799 |\n| uranium nucleus formed | 233.99048 |\n| α particle | 4.00151 |\n\n**(a)** Write a balanced nuclear equation for the decay of plutonium-238. **(2)**\n\n**(b)** Show that the energy released in each decay is about $9 \\times 10^{-13}$ J. **(3)**\n\n**(c)** A probe is launched with an RTG containing 4.00 kg of plutonium-238. The half-life of plutonium-238 is 87.7 years.\n\n**(i)** Show that the initial activity of the plutonium is about $2.5 \\times 10^{15}$ Bq. **(4)**\n\n**(ii)** The RTG transfers 6.0% of the energy released by the decays to electrical energy. The probe needs an electrical power of at least 110 W. Determine whether the RTG will still meet this requirement 14.0 years after launch. **(4)**", "markScheme": "**(a)**\n- **B1** ${}^{238}_{94}\\mathrm{Pu} \\rightarrow {}^{234}_{92}\\mathrm{U} + \\ldots$ (uranium with nucleon number 234 and proton number 92)\n- **B1** $\\ldots + {}^{4}_{2}\\alpha$ (or ${}^{4}_{2}\\mathrm{He}$), with all numbers in the equation correct\n\n**(b)**\n- **M1** $\\Delta m = 237.99799 - (233.99048 + 4.00151) = 0.00600$ u\n- **M1** Converts to kg and uses $\\Delta E = c^2\\Delta m$: $\\Delta E = 0.00600 \\times 1.66 \\times 10^{-27} \\times (3.00 \\times 10^{8})^2$\n- **A1*** $\\Delta E = 8.96 \\times 10^{-13}$ J (or 5.60 MeV converted to J)\n\n**(c)(i)**\n- **M1** Number of nuclei $N = \\dfrac{4.00}{238 \\times 1.66 \\times 10^{-27}} = 1.01 \\times 10^{25}$ (accept 237.998 u for the mass of a nucleus)\n- **M1** Half-life in seconds: $87.7 \\times 3.15 \\times 10^{7} = 2.76 \\times 10^{9}$ s (accept 365 × 24 × 3600 s per year)\n- **M1** $\\lambda = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{t_{1/2}} = 2.51 \\times 10^{-10}\\ \\mathrm{s^{-1}}$ and $A = \\lambda N$\n- **A1*** $A = 2.54 \\times 10^{15}$ Bq\n\n**(c)(ii)**\n- **M1** Initial power released $= A\\Delta E = 2.54 \\times 10^{15} \\times 8.96 \\times 10^{-13} = 2.28 \\times 10^{3}$ W\n- **M1** Initial electrical power $= 0.060 \\times 2.28 \\times 10^{3} = 137$ W\n- **M1** Uses $A = A_0e^{-\\lambda t}$ (or $P = P_0e^{-\\lambda t}$) with $\\lambda t = \\dfrac{\\ln 2 \\times 14.0}{87.7} = 0.111$, giving a factor of 0.895\n- **A1** Electrical power after 14.0 years $= 122$ W (accept 120–123 W, e.g. from show-that values), which is greater than 110 W, so the RTG still meets the requirement", "solution": "**(a)**\n$${}^{238}_{94}\\mathrm{Pu} \\rightarrow {}^{234}_{92}\\mathrm{U} + {}^{4}_{2}\\alpha$$\n\n**(b)**\n$$\\Delta m = 237.99799 - (233.99048 + 4.00151) = 0.00600\\ \\text{u}$$\n$$\\Delta m = 0.00600 \\times 1.66 \\times 10^{-27} = 9.96 \\times 10^{-30}\\ \\text{kg}$$\n$$\\Delta E = c^2\\Delta m = (3.00 \\times 10^{8})^2 \\times 9.96 \\times 10^{-30} = 8.96 \\times 10^{-13}\\ \\text{J} \\approx 9 \\times 10^{-13}\\ \\text{J}\\ (5.60\\ \\text{MeV})$$\n\n**(c)(i)**\n$$N = \\frac{4.00}{238 \\times 1.66 \\times 10^{-27}} = 1.01 \\times 10^{25}$$\n$$t_{1/2} = 87.7 \\times 3.15 \\times 10^{7} = 2.76 \\times 10^{9}\\ \\text{s}, \\qquad \\lambda = \\frac{\\ln 2}{2.76 \\times 10^{9}} = 2.51 \\times 10^{-10}\\ \\mathrm{s^{-1}}$$\n$$A = \\lambda N = 2.51 \\times 10^{-10} \\times 1.01 \\times 10^{25} = 2.54 \\times 10^{15}\\ \\text{Bq} \\approx 2.5 \\times 10^{15}\\ \\text{Bq}$$\n\n**(c)(ii)**\nInitial power released: $P_0 = A\\Delta E = 2.54 \\times 10^{15} \\times 8.96 \\times 10^{-13} = 2.28 \\times 10^{3}$ W\n\nInitial electrical power: $0.060 \\times 2.28 \\times 10^{3} = 137$ W\n\nThe power is proportional to the activity, so after 14.0 years:\n$$\\lambda t = \\frac{\\ln 2}{87.7} \\times 14.0 = 0.111, \\qquad e^{-0.111} = 0.895$$\n$$P_{\\text{elec}} = 137 \\times 0.895 = 122\\ \\text{W}$$\n122 W > 110 W, so the RTG **will** still meet the requirement after 14.0 years."},
{"id": "ial-phy5-005", "type": "written", "spec": "phy5:9.8", "specs": ["phy5:9.5", "phy5:9.6", "phy5:9.9"], "difficulty": 1, "marks": 11, "stem": "A student investigates the absorption of γ radiation by lead. A cobalt-60 source, which emits β⁻ particles and γ radiation, is placed a fixed distance in front of a Geiger–Müller (GM) tube connected to a counter. Sheets of lead are placed between the source and the GM tube.\n\nBefore the source is brought into the laboratory, the student records 150 counts in 300 s.\n\nThe student then records the number of counts in 200 s for different thicknesses $x$ of lead.\n\n| $x$ / mm | 4.0 | 10.0 | 16.0 | 22.0 | 28.0 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| counts in 200 s | 1712 | 1236 | 894 | 670 | 506 |\n\n**(a)** The 150 counts recorded before the source was brought in are due to background radiation. State two sources of background radiation. **(2)**\n\n**(b)** Use the data to show that each 12 mm of lead approximately halves the count rate due to the source. **(3)**\n\n**(c)** Explain why all the radiation from the source that is detected by the GM tube in this experiment is γ radiation. **(2)**\n\n**(d)** State two precautions the student should take to reduce the radiation dose she receives from the source. **(2)**\n\n**(e)** The student repeats the measurement for $x = 16.0$ mm and records 931 counts in 200 s. Explain why the two counts are different and state how the student could make her value for the count rate more reliable. **(2)**", "markScheme": "**(a)**\n- **B1** One correct source: radon gas (from rocks and soil) or cosmic rays (from space) or rocks and building materials (e.g. granite) or food, drink and the human body (e.g. potassium-40) or medical uses (e.g. X-rays, tracers) or nuclear power, nuclear accidents or weapons testing\n- **B1** A second, different correct source from the same list\n\n**(b)**\n- **M1** Background count rate $= 150/300 = 0.50\\ \\mathrm{s^{-1}}$ (or 100 counts in 200 s)\n- **M1** Subtracts background: corrected count rates 8.06 s⁻¹ (4.0 mm), 3.97 s⁻¹ (16.0 mm), 2.03 s⁻¹ (28.0 mm) (or corrected counts 1612, 794, 406; or uses 10.0 mm and 22.0 mm: 5.68 s⁻¹ and 2.85 s⁻¹)\n- **A1** Ratios $3.97/8.06 = 0.49$ and $2.03/3.97 = 0.51$ (or $2.85/5.68 = 0.50$), all ≈ 0.5, so each 12 mm of lead halves the count rate (uncorrected counts give 0.52 and 0.57: no A1)\n\n**(c)**\n- **B1** β⁻ particles are charged and strongly ionising so they lose energy quickly and are absorbed by a few mm of aluminium, so they are all absorbed by the (at least 4.0 mm of) lead\n- **B1** γ radiation is uncharged (electromagnetic radiation) and weakly ionising, so it is much more penetrating and some passes through several cm of lead\n\n**(d)**\n- **B1** Handle the source with tongs/long-handled tools or keep the source at arm's length / as far from the body as possible\n- **B1** Return the source to its (lead-lined) storage box when not in use / minimise the time of exposure or never point the source towards anyone\n\n**(e)**\n- **B1** Radioactive decay is random (and spontaneous), so the number of γ photons emitted (and detected) in a fixed time varies\n- **B1** Count for a longer time or repeat the count several times and calculate a mean", "solution": "**(a)** Any two from: radon gas from rocks and soil; cosmic rays; rocks and building materials such as granite; food, drink and the human body (e.g. potassium-40); medical sources such as X-rays; nuclear power, accidents or weapons testing.\n\n**(b)** Background count rate $= 150/300 = 0.50\\ \\mathrm{s^{-1}}$.\n\nCorrected count rate $= \\dfrac{\\text{count}}{200} - 0.50$:\n\n| $x$ / mm | 4.0 | 16.0 | 28.0 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| corrected rate / s⁻¹ | 8.06 | 3.97 | 2.03 |\n\n$3.97/8.06 = 0.49$ and $2.03/3.97 = 0.51$. Both are ≈ 0.5, so each extra 12 mm of lead approximately halves the count rate due to the source. (From 10.0 mm to 22.0 mm: 5.68 → 2.85 s⁻¹, ratio 0.50.)\n\n**(c)** The β⁻ particles are charged and strongly ionising, so they lose energy rapidly and are stopped by a few mm of aluminium. They are therefore all absorbed by the lead (at least 4.0 mm thick). γ radiation is uncharged electromagnetic radiation and only weakly ionising, so it is very penetrating and some passes through several cm of lead.\n\n**(d)** For example: handle the source only with long-handled tongs, keeping it as far from the body as possible; keep the source in its lead-lined box when it is not being used (minimising the exposure time) and never point it at anyone.\n\n**(e)** Radioactive decay is random, so the number of γ photons emitted and detected in a fixed time varies from one measurement to the next. The student should count for a longer time, or repeat the count several times and take a mean."},
{"id": "ial-phy5-006", "type": "written", "spec": "phy5:10.7", "specs": ["phy5:10.3", "phy5:10.6", "phy5:10.9", "phy5:10.10", "phy5:10.11"], "difficulty": 2, "marks": 13, "stem": "A student uses resonance to determine an unknown mass. A mass $m$ hangs from a spring of stiffness $k$. The top of the spring is attached to a vibration generator, which is connected to a signal generator, so that the top of the spring oscillates vertically with a small amplitude at a frequency that can be varied. For each mass, the student slowly increases the driving frequency and records the frequency $f_0$ at which the amplitude of oscillation of the mass is greatest.\n\n**(a)** The mass undergoes forced oscillations.\n\n**(i)** Explain the difference between a free oscillation and a forced oscillation. **(2)**\n\n**(ii)** Explain why the amplitude of oscillation of the mass is greatest when the driving frequency is $f_0$. **(2)**\n\n**(b)** Show that a graph of $\\dfrac{1}{f_0^{2}}$ against $m$ should be a straight line through the origin. **(2)**\n\n**(c)** Using known masses, the student obtains a graph of $\\dfrac{1}{f_0^{2}}$ against $m$ with a gradient of $0.987\\ \\mathrm{s^{2}\\,kg^{-1}}$. With the unknown mass on the spring, $f_0 = 1.95$ Hz. Calculate the unknown mass. **(3)**\n\n**(d)** The student attaches a large piece of card to the mass, which increases the damping. Describe how the graph of amplitude of the mass against driving frequency changes. **(2)**\n\n**(e)** Some earthquake-resistant buildings contain steel components that are designed to deform plastically when the building oscillates. Explain how this reduces the amplitude of the oscillations of the building. **(2)**", "markScheme": "**(a)(i)**\n- **B1** Free oscillation: no periodic external (driving) force acts once the system has been displaced, so it oscillates at its natural frequency\n- **B1** Forced oscillation: a periodic driving force acts on the system and it oscillates at the frequency of the driving force\n\n**(a)(ii)**\n- **B1** At $f_0$ the driving frequency equals the natural frequency of the mass–spring system (resonance)\n- **B1** There is maximum (rate of) energy transfer from the driver to the oscillating mass, so the amplitude is a maximum\n\n**(b)**\n- **M1** Uses $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{m/k}$ and $f_0 = 1/T$ to give $f_0^{2} = \\dfrac{k}{4\\pi^{2}m}$\n- **A1*** $\\dfrac{1}{f_0^{2}} = \\dfrac{4\\pi^{2}}{k}m$; since $4\\pi^{2}/k$ is constant this is of the form $y = (\\text{constant})x$, a straight line through the origin with gradient $4\\pi^{2}/k$\n\n**(c)**\n- **M1** $\\dfrac{1}{f_0^{2}} = \\dfrac{1}{1.95^{2}} = 0.263\\ \\mathrm{s^{2}}$\n- **M1** $m = \\dfrac{0.263}{0.987}$ (or finds $k = 4\\pi^{2}/0.987 = 40.0\\ \\mathrm{N\\,m^{-1}}$ and uses $m = k/4\\pi^{2}f_0^{2}$)\n- **A1** $m = 0.266$ kg\n\n**(d)**\n- **B1** The peak (maximum amplitude) is lower\n- **B1** The peak is broader/flatter (accept: the peak occurs at a slightly lower frequency, as a second point only if broader is also implied)\n\n**(e)**\n- **B1** Work is done (energy is needed) to deform the ductile steel plastically, and this energy is not returned to the oscillation (it is dissipated as thermal energy)\n- **B1** So energy is removed from the oscillating building (the oscillations are damped), and with less energy the amplitude is smaller", "solution": "**(a)(i)** In a free oscillation, no periodic external force acts after the system has been displaced and released; it oscillates at its own natural frequency. In a forced oscillation, a periodic driving force acts on the system and it oscillates at the frequency of the driving force.\n\n**(a)(ii)** At $f_0$ the driving frequency equals the natural frequency of the mass–spring system, so resonance occurs. Energy is transferred from the vibration generator to the mass at the maximum rate, so the amplitude is greatest.\n\n**(b)**\n$$T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{m}{k}} \\Rightarrow f_0 = \\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\sqrt{\\frac{k}{m}} \\Rightarrow \\frac{1}{f_0^{2}} = \\frac{4\\pi^{2}}{k}\\,m$$\n$4\\pi^{2}/k$ is constant, so $1/f_0^{2} \\propto m$: a straight line through the origin with gradient $4\\pi^{2}/k$.\n\n**(c)**\n$$\\frac{1}{f_0^{2}} = \\frac{1}{1.95^{2}} = 0.263\\ \\mathrm{s^{2}}, \\qquad m = \\frac{0.263}{0.987} = 0.266\\ \\text{kg}$$\n\n**(d)** With more damping, the resonance peak is lower (smaller maximum amplitude) and broader (flatter), and it occurs at a slightly lower frequency.\n\n**(e)** Ductile steel that deforms plastically needs work to be done on it to change its shape, and this energy is not returned to the motion: it is dissipated as thermal energy. Energy is therefore removed from the building's oscillations, which are damped, so their amplitude is reduced."},
{"id": "ial-phy5-007", "type": "written", "spec": "phy5:10.1", "specs": ["phy5:10.2", "phy5:10.8", "phy5:10.9"], "difficulty": 3, "marks": 14, "stem": "A cylindrical buoy of mass 120 kg floats upright in sea water of density $1030\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m^{-3}}$. The buoy has a uniform cross-sectional area of $0.250\\ \\mathrm{m^{2}}$. The buoy is pushed vertically downwards from its equilibrium position and released, so that it oscillates vertically. Part of the buoy always remains above the water surface.\n\n**(a)** By considering the upthrust on the buoy when it is a distance $x$ below its equilibrium position, show that the buoy moves with simple harmonic motion. Ignore resistive forces. **(3)**\n\n**(b)** Show that the period of oscillation of the buoy is about 1.4 s. **(3)**\n\n**(c)** The buoy is pushed down 0.080 m below its equilibrium position and released. Calculate the maximum speed of the buoy. **(2)**\n\n**(d)** In practice, the oscillations of the buoy are damped. After 5 complete oscillations, the amplitude has decreased to 0.050 m.\n\n**(i)** Describe the energy changes that take place during the oscillations and explain why the amplitude decreases. **(3)**\n\n**(ii)** Calculate the energy transferred from the oscillating buoy to the surroundings during these 5 oscillations. Assume that the damping does not change the period. **(3)**", "markScheme": "**(a)**\n- **B1** At equilibrium upthrust = weight; at distance $x$ below equilibrium an extra volume $Ax$ of water is displaced, so the extra upthrust (weight of extra water displaced) is $\\rho Axg$\n- **B1** Resultant force $F = -\\rho Agx$: proportional to the displacement and directed (upwards) towards the equilibrium position (and downwards when above equilibrium)\n- **B1** $a = F/m = -\\left(\\dfrac{\\rho Ag}{m}\\right)x$; $\\rho$, $A$, $g$ and $m$ are constant so $a \\propto -x$, the condition for SHM (with $\\omega^{2} = \\rho Ag/m$)\n\n**(b)**\n- **M1** $\\omega = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\rho Ag}{m}} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1030 \\times 0.250 \\times 9.81}{120}} = 4.59\\ \\mathrm{rad\\,s^{-1}}$ (or $k = \\rho Ag = 2.53 \\times 10^{3}\\ \\mathrm{N\\,m^{-1}}$)\n- **M1** $T = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\omega}$ (or $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{m/k}$)\n- **A1*** $T = 1.37$ s\n\n**(c)**\n- **M1** $v_{\\max} = A\\omega = 0.080 \\times 4.59$\n- **A1** $v_{\\max} = 0.367\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$ (accept 0.36–0.37 m s⁻¹, e.g. from $T = 1.4$ s)\n\n**(d)(i)**\n- **B1** Kinetic energy is maximum at the equilibrium position and potential energy is maximum at the extremes of the motion; energy is continuously interchanged between kinetic and potential (total constant if undamped)\n- **B1** Resistive (drag) forces from the water act opposite to the motion, so work is done against them\n- **B1** Energy is transferred from the oscillating buoy to the surroundings (thermal energy of the water / water waves), so the total energy of the oscillation decreases and so the amplitude decreases\n\n**(d)(ii)**\n- **M1** Initial energy $= \\tfrac{1}{2}m(A\\omega)^{2} = \\tfrac{1}{2} \\times 120 \\times (0.080 \\times 4.59)^{2} = 8.08$ J (or $\\tfrac{1}{2}kA^{2}$ with $k = \\rho Ag$)\n- **M1** Energy after 5 oscillations $= \\tfrac{1}{2} \\times 120 \\times (0.050 \\times 4.59)^{2} = 3.16$ J, and subtracts\n- **A1** Energy transferred $= 4.9$ J (accept 4.7–4.9 J, e.g. from $T = 1.4$ s)", "solution": "**(a)** In equilibrium the upthrust equals the weight. When the buoy is a distance $x$ below equilibrium, an extra volume $Ax$ of water is displaced, so the upthrust increases by $\\rho Axg$. The resultant force is therefore\n$$F = -\\rho Agx$$\nIt acts upwards, towards the equilibrium position, when the buoy is below it, and downwards when the buoy is above it. Using $F = ma$:\n$$a = -\\left(\\frac{\\rho Ag}{m}\\right)x$$\n$\\rho$, $A$, $g$ and $m$ are constants, so the acceleration is proportional to the displacement and always directed towards the equilibrium position. This is SHM with $\\omega^{2} = \\rho Ag/m$.\n\n**(b)**\n$$\\omega = \\sqrt{\\frac{1030 \\times 0.250 \\times 9.81}{120}} = \\sqrt{21.05} = 4.59\\ \\mathrm{rad\\,s^{-1}}$$\n$$T = \\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega} = \\frac{2\\pi}{4.59} = 1.37\\ \\text{s} \\approx 1.4\\ \\text{s}$$\n\n**(c)**\n$$v_{\\max} = A\\omega = 0.080 \\times 4.59 = 0.367\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$$\n\n**(d)(i)** As the buoy oscillates, energy is continually interchanged between kinetic energy, which is maximum at the equilibrium position, and potential energy, which is maximum at the extreme positions. Drag forces from the water oppose the motion, so work is done against them. Energy is transferred from the buoy to the surroundings as thermal energy of the water and as water waves. The total energy of the oscillation falls, so the amplitude decreases.\n\n**(d)(ii)** Total energy $= \\tfrac{1}{2}m v_{\\max}^{2} = \\tfrac{1}{2}m(A\\omega)^{2}$:\n$$E_1 = \\tfrac{1}{2} \\times 120 \\times (0.080 \\times 4.59)^{2} = 8.08\\ \\text{J}$$\n$$E_2 = \\tfrac{1}{2} \\times 120 \\times (0.050 \\times 4.59)^{2} = 3.16\\ \\text{J}$$\n$$\\text{Energy transferred} = 8.08 - 3.16 = 4.9\\ \\text{J}$$"},
{"id": "ial-phy5-008", "type": "written", "spec": "phy5:11.9", "specs": ["phy5:11.8", "phy5:11.10", "phy5:11.14", "phy5:11.15"], "difficulty": 2, "marks": 14, "stem": "Star X can be modelled as a black body radiator. The peak in the spectrum of Star X is at a wavelength of 680 nm, and its luminosity is $6.8 \\times 10^{28}$ W. The mass of Star X is about 1.1 times the mass of the Sun.\n\nFor the Sun: surface temperature = 5800 K, luminosity = $3.8 \\times 10^{26}$ W.\n\n**(a)** Explain what is meant by a black body radiator. **(2)**\n\n**(b)** Calculate the surface temperature of Star X. **(2)**\n\n**(c)** Show that the radius of Star X is about $1.7 \\times 10^{10}$ m. **(3)**\n\n**(d)** Graphs are drawn of the intensity of the radiation emitted per unit surface area against wavelength for black bodies at the surface temperatures of Star X and of the Sun. Describe how the graph for the Sun differs from the graph for Star X. **(2)**\n\n**(e)** Star X is a red giant.\n\n**(i)** Explain, with reference to the data, why Star X is described as a red giant. **(2)**\n\n**(ii)** On a Hertzsprung–Russell diagram, luminosity is plotted on the vertical axis and surface temperature on the horizontal axis, with temperature increasing to the left. Describe how Star X will evolve from its present stage to the end of its life, and how its position on the Hertzsprung–Russell diagram will change. **(3)**", "markScheme": "**(a)**\n- **B1** A black body absorbs all the electromagnetic radiation incident on it (at all wavelengths)\n- **B1** It emits a continuous spectrum of radiation whose distribution of intensity with wavelength depends only on its temperature (it is the best possible emitter at that temperature)\n\n**(b)**\n- **M1** $T = \\dfrac{2.898 \\times 10^{-3}}{680 \\times 10^{-9}}$\n- **A1** $T = 4.26 \\times 10^{3}$ K\n\n**(c)**\n- **M1** Uses $L = \\sigma AT^{4}$ with $A = 4\\pi r^{2}$\n- **M1** $r = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{6.8 \\times 10^{28}}{4\\pi \\times 5.67 \\times 10^{-8} \\times (4.26 \\times 10^{3})^{4}}}$ (ft their $T$)\n- **A1*** $r = 1.70 \\times 10^{10}$ m\n\n**(d)**\n- **B1** The peak of the Sun's graph is at a shorter wavelength (about 500 nm)\n- **B1** The Sun's graph is higher at every wavelength / has a higher peak and a greater area under it (power per unit area ∝ $T^{4}$)\n\n**(e)(i)**\n- **B1** Red: its surface temperature (4300 K) is lower than the Sun's / its peak wavelength (680 nm) is at the red end of the visible spectrum, so it appears red (orange-red)\n- **B1** Giant: its luminosity is about 180 times the Sun's even though it emits less power per unit area, so its surface area (radius) must be very large\n\n**(e)(ii)**\n- **B1** When helium fusion in the core ends, the outer layers are ejected (as a planetary nebula), leaving the hot, dense core as a white dwarf\n- **B1** The white dwarf has a high surface temperature but a very small surface area so a low luminosity: the star moves from the upper right (red giant region) to the lower left of the diagram\n- **B1** With no fusion, the white dwarf cools, so its temperature and luminosity decrease and it moves down and to the right (towards becoming a black dwarf)", "solution": "**(a)** A black body absorbs all the electromagnetic radiation that falls on it, at every wavelength. It emits a continuous spectrum of radiation whose distribution of intensity with wavelength depends only on its temperature.\n\n**(b)**\n$$T = \\frac{2.898 \\times 10^{-3}}{\\lambda_{\\max}} = \\frac{2.898 \\times 10^{-3}}{680 \\times 10^{-9}} = 4.26 \\times 10^{3}\\ \\text{K}$$\n\n**(c)** $L = \\sigma 4\\pi r^{2}T^{4}$, so\n$$r = \\sqrt{\\frac{L}{4\\pi\\sigma T^{4}}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{6.8 \\times 10^{28}}{4\\pi \\times 5.67 \\times 10^{-8} \\times (4.26 \\times 10^{3})^{4}}} = 1.70 \\times 10^{10}\\ \\text{m}$$\n\n**(d)** The Sun is hotter. Its peak is at a shorter wavelength ($2.898 \\times 10^{-3}/5800 \\approx 500$ nm), and its curve is higher at every wavelength, with a much higher peak and a larger area under the curve.\n\n**(e)(i)** Its surface temperature (about 4300 K) is lower than the Sun's, and its peak is at 680 nm, at the red end of the visible spectrum, so it looks red. Its luminosity is $6.8 \\times 10^{28}/3.8 \\times 10^{26} \\approx 180$ times the Sun's even though each square metre emits less power. Its surface area must therefore be very large ($r \\approx 1.7 \\times 10^{10}$ m), so it is a giant.\n\n**(e)(ii)** Star X has a mass similar to the Sun's. When helium fusion in its core ends, its outer layers are ejected as a planetary nebula and the hot, dense core is left as a white dwarf. The white dwarf is very hot but tiny, so its luminosity is low: the star moves from the upper right of the HR diagram (red giants) to the lower left (white dwarfs). No fusion takes place in the white dwarf, so it gradually cools. Its temperature and luminosity fall, so it moves down and to the right."},
{"id": "ial-phy5-009", "type": "written", "spec": "phy5:11.17", "specs": ["phy5:11.11", "phy5:11.12", "phy5:11.13", "phy5:11.16", "phy5:11.18"], "difficulty": 3, "marks": 14, "stem": "Type Ia supernovae are used as standard candles. At its peak, a Type Ia supernova has a luminosity of about $2.0 \\times 10^{36}$ W.\n\nA Type Ia supernova is observed in a distant galaxy. The peak intensity of its radiation measured at the Earth is $9.9 \\times 10^{-15}\\ \\mathrm{W\\,m^{-2}}$. In the spectrum of light from the galaxy, a calcium absorption line that has a wavelength of 393.4 nm when measured in a laboratory is observed at a wavelength of 405.2 nm.\n\n**(a)** Explain why the distance to this galaxy is determined using a standard candle rather than by trigonometric parallax. **(3)**\n\n**(b)** Show that the distance from the Earth to the galaxy is about $4 \\times 10^{24}$ m. **(2)**\n\n**(c)** Calculate the speed of the galaxy relative to the Earth, and state whether the galaxy is moving towards or away from the Earth. **(3)**\n\n**(d)** Use your answers to (b) and (c) to estimate the age of the universe in years. **(3)**\n\n**(e)** Explain why there is controversy about the age and the ultimate fate of the universe. **(3)**", "markScheme": "**(a)**\n- **B1** At such large distances the parallax angle would be too small to measure (accurately); parallax only works for relatively nearby stars\n- **B1** A standard candle is an object of known luminosity\n- **B1** The intensity (radiation flux) received at the Earth is measured and $I = \\dfrac{L}{4\\pi d^{2}}$ is used to calculate $d$\n\n**(b)**\n- **M1** $d = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{L}{4\\pi I}} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2.0 \\times 10^{36}}{4\\pi \\times 9.9 \\times 10^{-15}}}$\n- **A1*** $d = 4.0 \\times 10^{24}$ m (accept $4.01 \\times 10^{24}$ m)\n\n**(c)**\n- **M1** $z = \\dfrac{\\Delta\\lambda}{\\lambda} = \\dfrac{405.2 - 393.4}{393.4} = 0.0300$ and $v = zc$\n- **A1** $v = 9.0 \\times 10^{6}\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$\n- **B1** Moving away from the Earth (receding), because the observed wavelength is longer than the laboratory wavelength (redshift)\n\n**(d)**\n- **M1** $H_0 = \\dfrac{v}{d} = \\dfrac{9.0 \\times 10^{6}}{4.0 \\times 10^{24}} = 2.2 \\times 10^{-18}\\ \\mathrm{s^{-1}}$ (ft their $v$)\n- **M1** Age $t \\approx \\dfrac{1}{H_0} = 4.5 \\times 10^{17}$ s\n- **A1** $t = 1.4 \\times 10^{10}$ years (dividing by $3.15 \\times 10^{7}$ s per year)\n\n**(e)**\n- **B1** The value of $H_0$ is uncertain, because the distances to distant galaxies are difficult to measure accurately (e.g. uncertainty in the luminosity of standard candles), so the age estimated from $1/H_0$ is uncertain (accept: the rate of expansion may not have been constant)\n- **B1** The ultimate fate depends on the mean density of the universe compared with the critical density: greater, and the universe will eventually contract; less, and it will expand forever; equal, and the rate of expansion approaches zero\n- **B1** The mean density is uncertain because of dark matter, which does not emit electromagnetic radiation, so it cannot be observed directly and the amount of it is unknown", "solution": "**(a)** The galaxy is so far away that its parallax angle would be far too small to measure, so trigonometric parallax can only be used for relatively nearby stars. A standard candle is an object of known luminosity $L$. The intensity $I$ received at the Earth is measured, and $I = L/4\\pi d^{2}$ gives the distance $d$.\n\n**(b)**\n$$d = \\sqrt{\\frac{L}{4\\pi I}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{2.0 \\times 10^{36}}{4\\pi \\times 9.9 \\times 10^{-15}}} = 4.0 \\times 10^{24}\\ \\text{m}$$\n\n**(c)**\n$$z = \\frac{\\Delta\\lambda}{\\lambda} = \\frac{405.2 - 393.4}{393.4} = 0.0300$$\n$$v = zc = 0.0300 \\times 3.00 \\times 10^{8} = 9.0 \\times 10^{6}\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$$\nThe observed wavelength is longer than the laboratory wavelength (redshift), so the galaxy is moving **away** from the Earth.\n\n**(d)**\n$$H_0 = \\frac{v}{d} = \\frac{9.0 \\times 10^{6}}{4.0 \\times 10^{24}} = 2.2 \\times 10^{-18}\\ \\mathrm{s^{-1}}$$\n$$t \\approx \\frac{1}{H_0} = 4.5 \\times 10^{17}\\ \\text{s} = \\frac{4.5 \\times 10^{17}}{3.15 \\times 10^{7}} = 1.4 \\times 10^{10}\\ \\text{years}$$\n\n**(e)** Age: the value of $H_0$ is uncertain because it is hard to measure the distances to distant galaxies accurately (for example, standard candle luminosities are uncertain), so the age $\\approx 1/H_0$ is uncertain. The rate of expansion may also not have been constant.\n\nFate: this depends on how the mean density of the universe compares with the critical density. If it is greater, gravity will eventually stop the expansion and the universe will contract. If it is less, the universe will expand forever. If it is equal, the rate of expansion will approach zero. The mean density is uncertain because of dark matter, which does not emit electromagnetic radiation and cannot be observed directly, so the amount of it is not known."},
{"id": "ial-phy5-010", "type": "written", "spec": "phy5:11.7", "specs": ["phy5:11.2", "phy5:11.3", "phy5:11.4", "phy5:11.5"], "difficulty": 3, "marks": 14, "stem": "Mars can be treated as a uniform sphere of mass $6.42 \\times 10^{23}$ kg and radius $3.39 \\times 10^{6}$ m. Mars rotates once on its axis every 24.6 hours.\n\n**(a)** Starting from Newton's law of gravitation, show that the gravitational field strength at a distance $r$ from the centre of Mars is given by $g = \\dfrac{GM}{r^{2}}$, where $M$ is the mass of Mars. **(2)**\n\n**(b)** Calculate the gravitational field strength at the surface of Mars. **(2)**\n\n**(c)** A satellite moves in a circular orbit of radius $r$ around Mars with period $T$. Show that $T^{2} = \\dfrac{4\\pi^{2}}{GM}r^{3}$. **(3)**\n\n**(d)** A satellite is to remain above the same point on the equator of Mars at all times. Calculate the height of this satellite above the surface of Mars. **(4)**\n\n**(e)** Calculate the increase in gravitational potential energy when a satellite of mass 1200 kg is moved from the surface of Mars to the orbit in (d). **(3)**", "markScheme": "**(a)**\n- **B1** Force on a mass $m$ at distance $r$: $F = \\dfrac{GMm}{r^{2}}$\n- **B1** Gravitational field strength is force per unit mass, $g = \\dfrac{F}{m}$, so $g = \\dfrac{GM}{r^{2}}$\n\n**(b)**\n- **M1** $g = \\dfrac{6.67 \\times 10^{-11} \\times 6.42 \\times 10^{23}}{(3.39 \\times 10^{6})^{2}}$\n- **A1** $g = 3.73\\ \\mathrm{N\\,kg^{-1}}$\n\n**(c)**\n- **M1** Gravitational force provides the centripetal force: $\\dfrac{GMm}{r^{2}} = \\dfrac{mv^{2}}{r}$ (or $= m\\omega^{2}r$)\n- **M1** Substitutes $v = \\dfrac{2\\pi r}{T}$ (or $\\omega = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{T}$)\n- **A1*** Rearranges correctly to $T^{2} = \\dfrac{4\\pi^{2}}{GM}r^{3}$\n\n**(d)**\n- **M1** $T = 24.6 \\times 3600 = 8.86 \\times 10^{4}$ s\n- **M1** $r^{3} = \\dfrac{GMT^{2}}{4\\pi^{2}} = \\dfrac{6.67 \\times 10^{-11} \\times 6.42 \\times 10^{23} \\times (8.86 \\times 10^{4})^{2}}{4\\pi^{2}}$\n- **M1** $r = 2.04 \\times 10^{7}$ m and height $h = r - R$\n- **A1** $h = 1.70 \\times 10^{7}$ m\n\n**(e)**\n- **M1** Uses $V_{\\text{grav}} = -\\dfrac{GM}{r}$ at both positions: $-1.26 \\times 10^{7}\\ \\mathrm{J\\,kg^{-1}}$ at the surface and $-2.10 \\times 10^{6}\\ \\mathrm{J\\,kg^{-1}}$ in orbit (ft their $r$)\n- **M1** $\\Delta E_{\\text{grav}} = m\\Delta V = 1200 \\times (1.26 \\times 10^{7} - 2.10 \\times 10^{6})$\n- **A1** $\\Delta E_{\\text{grav}} = 1.26 \\times 10^{10}$ J", "solution": "**(a)** By Newton's law of gravitation, the force on a mass $m$ at distance $r$ from the centre of Mars is $F = \\dfrac{GMm}{r^{2}}$. Gravitational field strength is force per unit mass:\n$$g = \\frac{F}{m} = \\frac{GM}{r^{2}}$$\n\n**(b)**\n$$g = \\frac{6.67 \\times 10^{-11} \\times 6.42 \\times 10^{23}}{(3.39 \\times 10^{6})^{2}} = 3.73\\ \\mathrm{N\\,kg^{-1}}$$\n\n**(c)** The gravitational force provides the centripetal force:\n$$\\frac{GMm}{r^{2}} = \\frac{mv^{2}}{r}, \\qquad v = \\frac{2\\pi r}{T}$$\n$$\\frac{GM}{r^{2}} = \\frac{4\\pi^{2}r}{T^{2}} \\Rightarrow T^{2} = \\frac{4\\pi^{2}}{GM}r^{3}$$\n\n**(d)** The satellite's period must equal the rotation period of Mars: $T = 24.6 \\times 3600 = 8.86 \\times 10^{4}$ s.\n$$r^{3} = \\frac{GMT^{2}}{4\\pi^{2}} = \\frac{6.67 \\times 10^{-11} \\times 6.42 \\times 10^{23} \\times (8.86 \\times 10^{4})^{2}}{4\\pi^{2}} = 8.51 \\times 10^{21}\\ \\mathrm{m^{3}}$$\n$$r = 2.04 \\times 10^{7}\\ \\text{m}, \\qquad h = r - R = 2.04 \\times 10^{7} - 3.39 \\times 10^{6} = 1.70 \\times 10^{7}\\ \\text{m}$$\n\n**(e)**\n$$V_{\\text{surface}} = -\\frac{GM}{R} = -\\frac{4.28 \\times 10^{13}}{3.39 \\times 10^{6}} = -1.26 \\times 10^{7}\\ \\mathrm{J\\,kg^{-1}}$$\n$$V_{\\text{orbit}} = -\\frac{GM}{r} = -\\frac{4.28 \\times 10^{13}}{2.04 \\times 10^{7}} = -2.10 \\times 10^{6}\\ \\mathrm{J\\,kg^{-1}}$$\n$$\\Delta E_{\\text{grav}} = m\\Delta V = 1200 \\times (1.26 \\times 10^{7} - 2.10 \\times 10^{6}) = 1.26 \\times 10^{10}\\ \\text{J}$$"},
{"id": "ial-phy5-011", "type": "mcq", "spec": "phy5:8.2", "difficulty": 1, "stem": "A student calibrates a thermostat. A negative temperature coefficient (NTC) thermistor and a fixed resistor are connected in series across a power supply of constant e.m.f. and negligible internal resistance. The thermistor is placed in a beaker of water, and the output p.d. across the fixed resistor is recorded as the water is heated.\n\nWhich statement correctly describes and explains the results? **(1)**", "options": ["The output p.d. decreases as the temperature increases, because the resistance of the thermistor decreases.", "The output p.d. increases as the temperature increases, because the resistance of the thermistor decreases.", "The output p.d. increases as the temperature increases, because the resistance of the thermistor increases.", "The output p.d. does not change as the temperature increases, because the e.m.f. of the supply is constant."], "answer": "B", "solution": "**B** is correct. As the temperature rises, the resistance of an NTC thermistor decreases. The fixed resistor then has a larger share of the total resistance, so it takes a larger share of the supply e.m.f. (equivalently, the current increases, so $V = IR$ across the fixed resistor increases). The output p.d. therefore increases.\n\n- **A** describes the p.d. across the thermistor, not across the fixed resistor.\n- **C**: the resistance of an NTC thermistor decreases, not increases, as temperature rises. If it did increase, the output would fall.\n- **D**: the e.m.f. is constant, but how it is shared depends on the ratio of the two resistances, which changes with temperature."},
{"id": "ial-phy5-012", "type": "mcq", "spec": "phy5:9.3", "specs": ["phy5:9.1"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "When a nucleus of uranium-235 absorbs a slow neutron, one possible fission reaction is\n\n$${}^{235}_{92}\\mathrm{U} + {}^{1}_{0}\\mathrm{n} \\rightarrow {}^{141}_{56}\\mathrm{Ba} + {}^{92}_{36}\\mathrm{Kr} + 3\\,{}^{1}_{0}\\mathrm{n}$$\n\n| nucleus | binding energy per nucleon / MeV |\n|---|---|\n| uranium-235 | 7.59 |\n| barium-141 | 8.33 |\n| krypton-92 | 8.51 |\n\nWhat is the energy released in this fission? **(1)**", "options": ["9.25 MeV", "166 MeV", "174 MeV", "195 MeV"], "answer": "C", "solution": "**C** is correct. Energy released = total binding energy of the products − total binding energy of the reactants. Free neutrons have no binding energy.\n\nProducts: $141 \\times 8.33 + 92 \\times 8.51 = 1174.5 + 782.9 = 1957.5$ MeV\n\nReactants: $235 \\times 7.59 = 1783.7$ MeV\n\nEnergy released $= 1957.5 - 1783.7 = 174$ MeV\n\n- **A** (9.25 MeV) combines the binding energies per nucleon ($8.33 + 8.51 - 7.59$) without multiplying each by its nucleon number.\n- **B** (166 MeV) uses 236 nucleons for the uranium, wrongly giving the absorbed neutron a binding energy.\n- **D** (195 MeV) multiplies the change in a simple average binding energy per nucleon ($8.42 - 7.59 = 0.83$ MeV) by 235. This ignores the different nucleon numbers of the products and the three free neutrons, which have zero binding energy."},
{"id": "ial-phy5-013", "type": "mcq", "spec": "phy5:10.5", "specs": ["phy5:10.4", "phy5:10.2"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "An object oscillates with simple harmonic motion. At time $t_1$ the gradient of the object's displacement–time graph has its maximum positive value.\n\nWhich statement about the object's velocity–time graph at time $t_1$ is correct? **(1)**", "options": ["The velocity has its maximum positive value and the gradient of the velocity–time graph is zero.", "The velocity is zero and the gradient of the velocity–time graph has its maximum positive value.", "The velocity is zero and the gradient of the velocity–time graph has its maximum negative value.", "The velocity has its maximum positive value and the gradient of the velocity–time graph has its maximum positive value."], "answer": "A", "solution": "**A** is correct. The gradient of a displacement–time graph is the velocity, so at $t_1$ the velocity has its maximum positive value. This happens as the object passes through the equilibrium position, where $x = 0$, so the acceleration $a = -\\omega^{2}x = 0$. The gradient of a velocity–time graph is the acceleration, so it is zero at $t_1$.\n\n- **B** and **C**: the velocity is zero only at the extreme positions, where the displacement–time gradient is zero, not maximum.\n- **D**: when the velocity is maximum, the velocity–time graph is at a turning point, so its gradient (the acceleration) is zero, not maximum."},
{"id": "ial-phy5-014", "type": "mcq", "spec": "phy5:9.10", "difficulty": 2, "stem": "The activity $A$ of a sample of a radioactive isotope is measured at regular intervals. A graph of $\\ln(A/\\mathrm{Bq})$ against time $t$ in hours is a straight line with a gradient of $-0.0578\\ \\mathrm{h^{-1}}$ and an intercept on the $\\ln(A/\\mathrm{Bq})$ axis of 9.90.\n\nWhich of the following gives the half-life of the isotope and the activity of the sample at $t = 0$? **(1)**", "options": ["half-life = 17 h; activity at $t = 0$ = 9.9 Bq", "half-life = 17 h; activity at $t = 0$ = $2.0 \\times 10^{4}$ Bq", "half-life = 12 h; activity at $t = 0$ = 9.9 Bq", "half-life = 12 h; activity at $t = 0$ = $2.0 \\times 10^{4}$ Bq"], "answer": "D", "solution": "**D** is correct. From $A = A_0e^{-\\lambda t}$, $\\ln A = \\ln A_0 - \\lambda t$, so the gradient is $-\\lambda$ and the intercept is $\\ln A_0$.\n\n$\\lambda = 0.0578\\ \\mathrm{h^{-1}}$, so $t_{1/2} = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{\\lambda} = \\dfrac{0.693}{0.0578} = 12.0$ h\n\n$\\ln(A_0/\\mathrm{Bq}) = 9.90$, so $A_0 = e^{9.90} = 2.0 \\times 10^{4}$ Bq\n\n- 17 h (**A**, **B**) is $1/\\lambda$; it leaves out the factor $\\ln 2$.\n- 9.9 Bq (**A**, **C**) treats the intercept as the activity itself, when it is $\\ln A_0$."},
{"id": "ial-phy5-015", "type": "mcq", "spec": "phy5:11.6", "specs": ["phy5:11.1"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "Which of the following statements about gravitational fields and electric fields is correct? **(1)**", "options": ["A gravitational field is a region in which a charged particle experiences a force.", "The force between two point masses and the force between two point charges can each be either attractive or repulsive.", "The potential at a distance $r$ from a point mass and the potential at a distance $r$ from a point charge are each proportional to $1/r^{2}$.", "The field strength at a distance $r$ from a point mass and the field strength at a distance $r$ from a point charge are each proportional to $1/r^{2}$."], "answer": "D", "solution": "**D** is correct. Both are radial inverse-square fields: $g = \\dfrac{GM}{r^{2}}$ and $E = \\dfrac{Q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r^{2}}$.\n\n- **A**: a gravitational field is a region in which a **mass** experiences a force. A force on a charge defines an electric field.\n- **B**: gravitational forces are always attractive. Only electric forces can be attractive or repulsive.\n- **C**: both potentials are proportional to $1/r$ ($V = -GM/r$ and $V = Q/4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r$), not $1/r^{2}$."},
{"id": "ial-phy5-016", "type": "mcq", "spec": "phy5:9.7", "specs": ["phy5:9.6"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "Sodium-22, ${}^{22}_{11}\\mathrm{Na}$, decays by $\\beta^{+}$ emission.\n\nWhich equation correctly represents this decay? **(1)**", "options": ["${}^{22}_{11}\\mathrm{Na} \\rightarrow {}^{22}_{10}\\mathrm{Ne} + {}^{0}_{+1}\\beta + \\nu_e$", "${}^{22}_{11}\\mathrm{Na} \\rightarrow {}^{22}_{10}\\mathrm{Ne} + {}^{0}_{+1}\\beta + \\bar{\\nu}_e$", "${}^{22}_{11}\\mathrm{Na} \\rightarrow {}^{22}_{12}\\mathrm{Mg} + {}^{0}_{-1}\\beta + \\bar{\\nu}_e$", "${}^{22}_{11}\\mathrm{Na} \\rightarrow {}^{22}_{12}\\mathrm{Mg} + {}^{0}_{+1}\\beta + \\nu_e$"], "answer": "A", "solution": "**A** is correct. In $\\beta^{+}$ decay a proton changes into a neutron, emitting a positron and an electron **neutrino**. The nucleon number is unchanged (22) and the proton number falls by 1 (11 → 10, neon). Check: $22 = 22 + 0 + 0$ and $11 = 10 + 1 + 0$.\n\n- **B**: a positron is emitted with a neutrino, not an antineutrino (lepton number would not be conserved).\n- **C** shows $\\beta^{-}$ decay, in which a neutron becomes a proton.\n- **D**: the proton number increases while a positive particle is emitted, so charge is not conserved ($11 \\neq 12 + 1$)."},
{"id": "ial-phy5-017", "type": "mcq", "spec": "phy5:8.9", "specs": ["phy5:8.5"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "Container X holds helium gas at a temperature of 27 °C. Container Y holds argon gas at a temperature of 327 °C. The mass of an argon atom is 10 times the mass of a helium atom. Both gases behave as ideal gases.\n\nWhat is the value of $\\dfrac{\\text{r.m.s. speed of the helium atoms}}{\\text{r.m.s. speed of the argon atoms}}$? **(1)**", "options": ["5.0", "2.2", "0.91", "0.45"], "answer": "B", "solution": "**B** is correct. $\\tfrac{1}{2}m\\langle c^2\\rangle = \\tfrac{3}{2}kT$, so $c_{\\text{rms}} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{3kT}{m}}$. The temperatures in kelvin are 300 K (helium) and 600 K (argon). Taking the mass of a helium atom as 1 unit:\n$$\\frac{c_{\\text{He}}}{c_{\\text{Ar}}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{T_{\\text{He}}/m_{\\text{He}}}{T_{\\text{Ar}}/m_{\\text{Ar}}}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{300/1}{600/10}} = \\sqrt{5} = 2.2$$\n\n- **A** (5.0) leaves out the square root.\n- **C** (0.91) uses the Celsius temperatures 27 and 327 instead of kelvin.\n- **D** (0.45) is the inverse ratio (argon to helium)."},
{"id": "ial-phy5-018", "type": "mcq", "spec": "phy5:11.12", "difficulty": 2, "stem": "As the Earth orbits the Sun, the apparent position of a nearby star changes against the background of very distant stars. Observations made six months apart show that the total change in the angular position of the star is $1.2 \\times 10^{-6}$ rad. The radius of the Earth's orbit is $1.5 \\times 10^{11}$ m.\n\nWhat is the distance from the Sun to the star? **(1)**", "options": ["$9.0 \\times 10^{4}$ m", "$1.3 \\times 10^{17}$ m", "$2.5 \\times 10^{17}$ m", "$5.0 \\times 10^{17}$ m"], "answer": "C", "solution": "**C** is correct. Over six months the Earth moves from one side of its orbit to the other, so the total angular shift is twice the parallax angle: $\\theta = 0.60 \\times 10^{-6}$ rad. For a small angle, $\\tan\\theta \\approx \\theta = r/d$:\n$$d = \\frac{r}{\\theta} = \\frac{1.5 \\times 10^{11}}{0.60 \\times 10^{-6}} = 2.5 \\times 10^{17}\\ \\text{m}$$\n\n- **A** multiplies $r$ by $\\theta$ instead of dividing.\n- **B** ($1.25 \\times 10^{17}$ m) uses the total shift of $1.2 \\times 10^{-6}$ rad as the parallax angle.\n- **D** uses the diameter of the orbit ($3.0 \\times 10^{11}$ m) with the halved angle."}
]);
